globalThis.RY_LESSON_LIBRARY={"build":"2026-10-28-spiral-coach-v38","subjects":{"ELA":{"name":"ELA","lessons":[{"id":"ela_v34_chimp_structure","title":"CURRENT WEEK • My Life with the Chimpanzees: Meaning, Viewpoint & Structure","src":"Sunset Middle School 6th grade newsletter dated October 23, 2026","teach":"Continue the assigned Jane Goodall autobiography excerpt by separating five reading jobs. Main idea states the central point developed across the section. Author point of view describes Goodall’s perspective as narrator. Author purpose explains why she includes a detail. Evidence is the precise detail supporting a claim. Section function explains how one part contributes to the whole. A life event may introduce an interest, an observation may develop it, and a reflection may explain its significance. The newsletter announces a test on this text Monday, November 2, and a study guide posted in Schoology Monday, October 26; that private guide is not represented as recovered here.","model":"MODEL: A section describes careful observation, and a later section explains what the observation taught the narrator. Main idea: sustained observation can deepen understanding. Point of view: curious and reflective. Purpose of the observation detail: show how knowledge developed. Function of the later reflection: explain the event’s importance. Evidence must quote or accurately paraphrase the assigned excerpt after Riley returns to it.","connect":"The same claim-evidence-reasoning chain is used in Science food-web predictions and Social Studies geography explanations. Connect My Life with the Chimpanzees: Meaning, Viewpoint & Structure to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"I can distinguish main idea, point of view, purpose, evidence, and section function and prove each answer from the assigned text.","v":[["main idea","the most important point a section develops"],["point of view","the narrator’s perspective or attitude"],["author’s purpose","why the author includes information"],["section function","how one section contributes to the whole text"],["evidence","a precise detail that supports a claim"]],"w":"After reading the assigned excerpt, write one paragraph stating the main idea, one point-of-view word, the function of one named section, and two verified details with reasoning. Label each part. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject."},{"id":"ela_v34_compound_complex","title":"CURRENT WEEK • Compound & Complex Sentences + Spiral Writing","src":"Sunset Middle School 6th grade newsletter dated October 23, 2026","teach":"A compound sentence joins two independent clauses. It can use a comma plus a coordinating conjunction such as for, and, nor, but, or, yet, or so. A complex sentence contains one independent clause and at least one dependent clause. When a dependent clause comes first, use a comma before the independent clause. Riley’s sentence-type quiz and IXL GG.9 are due Friday, October 30. The weekly spiral is also due Friday and now includes a written component.","model":"MODEL: Compound—“Jane waited quietly, and the chimpanzee returned.” Both sides can stand alone. Complex—“Because Jane waited quietly, the chimpanzee returned.” The opening clause has a subject and verb but cannot stand alone. Do not classify a sentence by length; identify the clauses and their relationship.","connect":"Sentence structure supports clear explanations in every subject. Connect Compound & Complex Sentences + Spiral Writing to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"I can build, punctuate, and explain compound and complex sentences and use them in a written response.","v":[["compound sentence","two independent clauses joined correctly"],["complex sentence","one independent clause plus at least one dependent clause"],["coordinating conjunction","for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so"],["subordinating conjunction","a word such as because, although, when, if, or while that makes a clause dependent"]],"w":"Write four source-related sentences: one simple, one compound, and two complex. Label every independent and dependent clause. Then write a short spiral response using claim, evidence, and reasoning. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject."},{"id":"ela_v29_clause_video","title":"ELA MEMORY LAB • Independent and Dependent Clauses","src":"Independent and dependent clauses practice video • Riley ELA newsletter","link":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uNCbEx2GBaw","teach":"A clause has a subject and a verb. An independent clause expresses a complete thought: “Jane observed the animals.” A dependent clause leaves the thought unfinished: “Because Jane observed the animals.”\n\nRead each clause aloud. Find its subject and verb, then decide whether the thought is complete. After watching the short video, close it and try the questions.","model":"“Jane observed the chimpanzees” is independent. “Because Jane observed the chimpanzees” is dependent; because leaves the reader waiting. Together: “Because Jane observed the chimpanzees, she noticed a pattern.”","connect":"Complete clauses make your explanations clear. In Science, “Because the water froze” needs a main clause: “Because the water froze, the pond’s surface changed.” Connect Independent and Dependent Clauses to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"I can classify clauses and combine them into correctly punctuated sentences.","v":[["independent clause","subject + verb + complete thought"],["dependent clause","subject + verb but incomplete thought"],["subordinating conjunction","word such as because, although, when, or if that begins a dependent clause"]],"w":"CONNECTION: Write one ELA, one Science, and one Social Studies sentence that each joins a dependent clause to an independent clause. Underline both parts and explain the punctuation. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#ELA","cue":"Read → gather evidence → explain the effect → close and retrieve.","task":"Use the evidence organizer or clause sort. Then close the tool and answer a new passage question.","apply":"State a central idea, give two relevant details, and explain what a paragraph contributes. Compare one shared idea across the assigned text and media.","delayed":"Tomorrow, use a different paragraph and explain the idea, evidence, purpose, and one clause without reopening today’s chart."}},{"id":"ela_v29_main_idea_song","title":"ELA MEMORY LAB • Main Idea Song → Evidence Proof","src":"Flocabulary Main Idea video • Riley ELA newsletter and quiz targets","link":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pui4NRRpyao","teach":"The topic names what a text is about. The central idea tells the most important point the text develops. “Animals” is a topic. “Patient observation helps people understand animal behavior” is a central idea.\n\nChoose details that support the whole idea. After listening to the song, close it and explain a paragraph’s central idea using two details.","model":"A topic is “chimpanzees.” A central idea is a complete claim: “Careful observation helps researchers understand chimpanzee behavior.” Details about recorded actions and repeated observations support it.","connect":"Central idea connects to objective summary: keep the central idea and essential details, but remove opinions and minor examples. Connect Main Idea Song → Evidence Proof to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"With the video off, I can state a central idea and choose two details that prove it.","v":[["topic","the broad subject"],["central idea","the most important point developed across a text"],["supporting detail","specific evidence that develops the central idea"],["objective summary","a neutral retelling of the central idea and essential details"]],"w":"CONNECTION: With the video off, read a new paragraph. Write its topic, central idea, two supporting details, an objective one-sentence summary, and explain how central idea supports summary. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#ELA","cue":"Read → gather evidence → explain the effect → close and retrieve.","task":"Use the evidence organizer or clause sort. Then close the tool and answer a new passage question.","apply":"State a central idea, give two relevant details, and explain what a paragraph contributes. Compare one shared idea across the assigned text and media.","delayed":"Tomorrow, use a different paragraph and explain the idea, evidence, purpose, and one clause without reopening today’s chart."}},{"id":"ela_v26_terms_craft","title":"ELA Terms 1–14: Ideas, Craft, Tone & Purpose","src":"Riley ELA binder photographs IMG_0936–IMG_0954 • teacher notes, practice pages, narrative scratch paper, and TNReady Grades 6–8 Narrative Rubric","teach":"Learn these words through examples. Explain what each word means and use it to describe something you read or write. Similar words are not interchangeable: theme is not a one-word topic; tone is not mood; perspective is not automatically point of view; evidence is not reasoning.","model":"MODEL: “The storm glared through the windows.” The word glared gives the storm a human action, so the device is personification. Naming “figurative language” is too broad; the precise term and the evidence must both be identified.","connect":"Use the question’s action word to plan your answer. Explain similarities when asked to compare, differences when asked to contrast, and evidence with reasoning when asked to justify. Connect ELA Terms 1–14: Ideas, Craft, Tone & Purpose to evidence and author craft: identify the exact word or phrase, determine its meaning or feeling in context, and explain how that language changes tone, mood, character, theme, or central idea. The same precision is needed when Science and Social Studies use domain-specific words.","success":"I can recognize, define, distinguish, and apply every term in an unfamiliar passage or prompt.","v":[["main idea / central idea","the most important idea an informational text develops"],["theme","a complete message about life or people developed in a literary text"],["point of view","the position from which a story is narrated and the information the narrator can reveal"],["simile","a comparison using like or as"],["metaphor","a direct comparison that says one thing is another"],["personification","giving human qualities or actions to something nonhuman"],["hyperbole","deliberate exaggeration for emphasis, not a literal claim"],["alliteration","repetition of beginning consonant sounds in nearby words"],["repetition","intentional reuse of a word, phrase, sound, or structure for effect"],["onomatopoeia","a word whose sound imitates the sound it names"],["tone","the author’s or speaker’s attitude toward the subject"],["mood","the feeling or atmosphere a text creates for the reader"],["author’s purpose","the author’s reason for creating a text, such as informing, explaining, persuading, or entertaining"],["convey","to communicate or make an idea, feeling, or information understood"]],"w":"Choose six terms without looking. Define each in your own words, create a correct example, give a non-example, and explain the boundary between two commonly confused terms. CONNECTION: Use one task verb to answer a Science or Social Studies question with relevant evidence. CONNECTION: Use one precise word or phrase as evidence. Explain its meaning in context, its effect, and how that same evidence habit helps in Science or Social Studies."},{"id":"ela_v26_terms_tasks_a","title":"ELA Terms 15–28: Analysis & Evidence Verbs","src":"Riley ELA binder photographs IMG_0936–IMG_0954 • teacher notes, practice pages, narrative scratch paper, and TNReady Grades 6–8 Narrative Rubric","teach":"Learn these words through examples. Explain what each word means and use it to describe something you read or write. Similar words are not interchangeable: theme is not a one-word topic; tone is not mood; perspective is not automatically point of view; evidence is not reasoning.","model":"Practice passage: “The first seedling grew beside a bright window. The second grew in a dim corner. Both received the same amount of water.”\n\nAnalyze: light differs while water stays the same. Compare: both are seedlings receiving equal water. Justify: use these details to explain why light is worth investigating. Evidence supplies the details; reasoning explains why they matter.","connect":"Use the question’s action word to plan your answer. Explain similarities when asked to compare, differences when asked to contrast, and evidence with reasoning when asked to justify. Connect ELA Terms 15–28: Analysis & Evidence Verbs to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"I can recognize, define, distinguish, and apply every term in an unfamiliar passage or prompt.","v":[["analyze","to break something into parts and explain how the parts work or relate"],["compare","to explain meaningful similarities between two or more things"],["contrast","to explain meaningful differences between two or more things"],["infer","to reach a supported conclusion from evidence and reasoning"],["summarize","to state the central idea and essential details concisely and objectively"],["evaluate","to judge quality, effectiveness, or credibility using stated criteria and evidence"],["justify","to support an answer or decision with relevant evidence and reasoning"],["evidence","specific information from a text, source, observation, or data set that supports a claim"],["significant","important enough to affect meaning, events, or outcomes"],["consequence","a result or effect that follows an action, choice, or event"],["factor","one condition or element that helps produce or influence a result"],["impact","the effect or influence something has"],["method","a planned way or technique used to accomplish a task"],["process","a connected series of steps or changes leading to a result"]],"w":"Choose six terms without looking. Define each in your own words, create a correct example, give a non-example, and explain the boundary between two commonly confused terms. CONNECTION: Use one task verb to answer a Science or Social Studies question with relevant evidence. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject."},{"id":"ela_v26_terms_tasks_b","title":"ELA Terms 29–42: Precision Task Words","src":"Riley ELA binder photographs IMG_0936–IMG_0954 • teacher notes, practice pages, narrative scratch paper, and TNReady Grades 6–8 Narrative Rubric","teach":"Learn these words through examples. Explain what each word means and use it to describe something you read or write. Similar words are not interchangeable: theme is not a one-word topic; tone is not mood; perspective is not automatically point of view; evidence is not reasoning.","model":"Practice sentence: “After the trail flooded, the hikers chose a longer route.”\n\nIdentify the change: the flooded trail. Determine its impact: a new route. Distinguish cause from consequence: flooding is the cause; the longer route is a consequence. Formulate a claim: changing conditions affected the hikers’ choice. Support it with the sentence’s details.","connect":"Use the question’s action word to plan your answer. Explain similarities when asked to compare, differences when asked to contrast, and evidence with reasoning when asked to justify. Connect ELA Terms 29–42: Precision Task Words to evidence and author craft: identify the exact word or phrase, determine its meaning or feeling in context, and explain how that language changes tone, mood, character, theme, or central idea. The same precision is needed when Science and Social Studies use domain-specific words.","success":"I can recognize, define, distinguish, and apply every term in an unfamiliar passage or prompt.","v":[["perspective","a person’s viewpoint, position, or way of seeing a subject"],["conclude","to reach a final judgment or supported result after considering evidence"],["determine","to find out or decide through evidence, calculation, or reasoning"],["distinguish","to recognize and explain the difference between similar things"],["identify","to name or locate something accurately"],["contribute","to add to, help cause, or play a part in a larger whole"],["illustrate","to explain or make clear with an example, detail, or visual"],["clarify","to make an idea easier to understand or remove confusion"],["emphasize","to give special importance or attention to something"],["formulate","to create or develop an idea, plan, or answer carefully"],["develop","to build an idea, event, or character with relevant details and explanation"],["relevant","directly connected to the question, claim, or purpose"],["essential","absolutely necessary to understanding or completing the task"],["emerge","to appear, become known, or develop gradually"]],"w":"Choose six terms without looking. Define each in your own words, create a correct example, give a non-example, and explain the boundary between two commonly confused terms. CONNECTION: Use one task verb to answer a Science or Social Studies question with relevant evidence. CONNECTION: Use one precise word or phrase as evidence. Explain its meaning in context, its effect, and how that same evidence habit helps in Science or Social Studies."},{"id":"ela_v26_dialogue","title":"Dialogue Punctuation: Seven Rules","src":"Riley ELA binder photographs IMG_0936–IMG_0954 • teacher notes, practice pages, narrative scratch paper, and TNReady Grades 6–8 Narrative Rubric","teach":"Rule 1: each new speaker gets a new paragraph; keep that speaker’s nearby actions with the dialogue. Rule 2: capitalize the first word spoken, even when the quotation begins later in the sentence. Rule 3: use a comma between dialogue and a dialogue tag when the spoken sentence would otherwise end with a period. Rule 4: commas, periods, and exclamation points go inside closing quotation marks. Rule 5: put a question mark inside when the spoken words are the question; put it outside when the entire surrounding sentence is the question. Rule 6: when a tag interrupts one spoken sentence, use commas and do not capitalize the continued words. Rule 7: avoid giant blocks of talking; weave dialogue into narration with actions, thoughts, setting, and consequences.","model":"MODEL: “I found it,” Maya said, “but I was afraid to tell you.” The comma stays inside the first closing quotation mark, the tag is lowercase, another comma follows the tag, and but is lowercase because the same sentence continues. New speaker: start a new paragraph.","connect":"Connect punctuation to character evidence: what a character says, avoids, or interrupts can reveal motivation and move the conflict. Connect Dialogue Punctuation: Seven Rules to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","success":"I can edit unfamiliar dialogue and independently write a scene using all seven dialogue rules.","v":[["dialogue tag","words such as she said that identify the speaker"],["action beat","a character action near dialogue; it is its own sentence"],["interrupted dialogue","one spoken sentence split by a dialogue tag"],["narration","storytelling outside the characters’ spoken words"]],"w":"Write a 10–12 sentence scene with two speakers. Use separate paragraphs, a dialogue tag, an action beat, one spoken question, one surrounding-sentence question, and an interrupted line. Underline where each of the seven rules appears. CONNECTION: Explain how dialogue supplies evidence about character and affects plot. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_v26_prompt_plan","title":"Attack a TCAP Narrative Prompt + Scratch-Paper Plan","src":"Riley ELA binder photographs IMG_0936–IMG_0954 • teacher notes, practice pages, narrative scratch paper, and TNReady Grades 6–8 Narrative Rubric","teach":"Use this reading routine in order. First, read the entire prompt. As you reread, circle every task word that tells you what to do. Draw an arrow from each task word to the exact content it controls. Under the prompt, list and number the demands in order. Then make a scratch-paper plan: Prompt/checklist; Exposition + Rising Action using known source facts plus new but consistent story information; Climax; Falling Action; Resolution; and one deliberate ending type. A continuation must preserve the assigned narrator, source facts, setting, characterization, and launch situation.","model":"MODEL: “Continue the story from Miss Lesley’s point of view. Use details from Counting on Grace and follow standard English.” Demands: 1 continue—not summarize; 2 Miss Lesley’s point of view; 3 use source details; 4 create a multi-paragraph narrative; 5 use standard conventions. Plan the climax before drafting so events build toward a change instead of wandering.","connect":"Connect prompt attack to academic verbs and evidence across subjects: the answer form must match the verb and every condition. Connect Attack a TCAP Narrative Prompt + Scratch-Paper Plan to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","success":"I can independently decode a dense prompt and create a source-faithful, complete plan before drafting.","v":[["task word","a verb telling the writer what to do"],["constraint","a boundary the response must follow"],["continuation","new events that begin where the source ends and remain consistent"],["scratch-paper plan","a brief plot-and-requirements map used before drafting"]],"w":"Decode one narrative prompt. Write a numbered demand list, then complete all five planning areas. Label every source fact, every new but consistent detail, the climax, and the chosen ending type. CONNECTION: Explain how prompt analysis is the same task-verbs skill used in Science and Social Studies. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_v26_plot_conflict","title":"Plot Mountain, Conflict & Character Change","src":"Riley ELA binder photographs IMG_0936–IMG_0954 • teacher notes, practice pages, narrative scratch paper, and TNReady Grades 6–8 Narrative Rubric","teach":"Plot is the organized sequence of events in a story. Exposition introduces characters, setting, and the central problem. Rising action is the climb where complications, tension, and excitement grow. The climax is the turning point where the main character faces the conflict and a decision or discovery changes the direction. Falling action shows the immediate results as problems begin to settle. Resolution brings the story to an ending. Four common conflicts are character vs. character/group, character vs. nature, character vs. self, and character vs. technology. Conflict is classified by the force creating the central struggle—not simply by what appears in the scene.","model":"MODEL: In “For the Birds,” the small birds exclude the large bird. Their escalating attempts to remove him are rising action; the release that sends the small birds flying is the climax because it reverses the power and determines the outcome. The central conflict is character vs. character/group, not nature merely because the characters are birds.","connect":"Connect plot sequence to cause and effect: rising choices create consequences, the climax changes direction, and the resolution reveals impact. Connect Plot Mountain, Conflict & Character Change to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","success":"I can place unfamiliar events by function, classify the central conflict, and explain character change with evidence.","v":[["exposition","beginning context: characters, setting, and central problem"],["rising action","complications that increase tension"],["climax","the decisive turning point"],["falling action","consequences following the climax"],["resolution","the outcome or ending"],["conflict","the central struggle driving events"]],"w":"Create a plot mountain for a familiar story. For every stage, name a specific event and explain its function. Classify the central conflict and trace one character change from beginning to end. CONNECTION: Turn the plot into a cause-and-effect chain with evidence. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_v26_endings","title":"Seven Story Endings: Identify, Defend & Rewrite","src":"Riley ELA binder photographs IMG_0936–IMG_0954 • teacher notes, practice pages, narrative scratch paper, and TNReady Grades 6–8 Narrative Rubric","teach":"Seven ending types are explicit, implicit, lesson learned, twist, tie back, unresolved, and long view. An ending can show more than one quality, so classify it by its strongest function and defend the choice with precise clues. Explicit answers major questions; implicit requires inference; lesson learned shows change; twist surprises using planted evidence; tie back reconnects to the opening; unresolved leaves major conflict open; long view moves into the future.","model":"MODEL: “Years later, Lina still kept the cracked compass above her desk.” This is primarily long view because it tells what happens later. It also ties back to the compass, but the time jump is the strongest structural effect. A strong answer names the type, cites the clue, and explains the effect.","connect":"Connect ending choice to character change, theme, and reader inference; the ending must grow from the conflict rather than appear as a detached trick. Connect Seven Story Endings: Identify, Defend & Rewrite to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","success":"I can distinguish all seven types, defend classifications with evidence, and deliberately create each type.","v":[["explicit","wraps up and answers the major questions, often stating what happens to major characters"],["implicit","leaves the reader to infer the outcome from clues"],["lesson learned","shows the main character grow, change, or learn and often develops a moral-type theme"],["twist","uses a surprising but story-supported event that changes understanding"],["tie back","connects the ending to clues, language, an object, or an event planted near the beginning"],["unresolved","leaves one or more major conflicts unsolved"],["long view","reveals what happens to a character or characters later in the future"]],"w":"Write seven two-sentence endings for one shared story situation—one of each type. Label each, underline the feature that proves the type, and explain the intended effect. CONNECTION: Explain how the ending completes or deliberately leaves open the plot’s cause-and-effect chain. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_v26_all_summer_end","title":"“All Summer in a Day”: Plot, Ending & Rewrite","src":"Riley ELA binder photographs IMG_0936–IMG_0954 • teacher notes, practice pages, narrative scratch paper, and TNReady Grades 6–8 Narrative Rubric","teach":"At the end of Ray Bradbury’s story, the rain returns, the children remember Margot, walk to the closet, and release her. The ending is unresolved: the physical act of opening the door occurs, but the central emotional and relational questions remain open. Will the children apologize? How will Margot respond? Can the harm be repaired? A defensible answer must identify the ending type, cite precise ending evidence, and explain what remains unresolved. Try a different ending type while keeping the story’s established facts and characters consistent.","model":"MODEL: Claim—unresolved ending. Evidence—the children cannot meet one another’s eyes, walk slowly, and release Margot without the text showing her response. Reasoning—the actions show guilt but leave the central relationship and consequences unanswered. Rewrite as lesson learned: one child admits the harm, acts differently, and the change follows from guilt already shown.","connect":"Connect the ending to inference, theme, character change, and source evidence rather than treating it as an isolated label. Connect “All Summer in a Day”: Plot, Ending & Rewrite to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","success":"I can defend the original ending and create a source-faithful alternative with a deliberate effect.","v":[["source fidelity","keeping established facts, setting, characterization, and events accurate"],["ending evidence","specific final details that reveal an ending’s type and effect"],["redesign","changing the ending’s structure while preserving the story’s established world"]],"w":"Write a 1–2 sentence defense of the original ending. Then rewrite the ending as one different type. Label the new type, preserve source facts, include correctly punctuated dialogue, and explain how the new ending changes theme and character impact. CONNECTION: Trace action → consequence → inference. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_v26_rubric","title":"TN Narrative Rubric: Score, Revise & Prove","src":"Riley ELA binder photographs IMG_0936–IMG_0954 • teacher notes, practice pages, narrative scratch paper, and TNReady Grades 6–8 Narrative Rubric","teach":"Use four areas to revise your narrative. Focus & Organization: answer the exact task, orient the reader with relevant context and point of view, introduce narrator/characters, sequence events naturally, use transitions, and conclude effectively. Development: use relevant dialogue, pacing, description, and reflection to develop experiences, events, and characters and incorporate source details. Language: use precise and sensory language, varied sentence structures, and purposeful transitions. Conventions: control sentence structure, grammar, usage, spelling, capitalization, and punctuation. A vivid response can still score poorly if it violates the prompt.","model":"MODEL: Draft: “I was scared. I went in. I told her.” Diagnosis: focused but underdeveloped. Revision: add the exact turning-point action, one purposeful line of dialogue, sensory detail, and reflection. Then check quotation marks and sentence boundaries. The repair targets Development, Language, and Conventions without changing the prompt.","connect":"Connect rubric revision to error analysis: identify the exact failure, explain why it matters, repair only what is needed, and recheck the whole response. Connect TN Narrative Rubric: Score, Revise & Prove to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","success":"I can draft, score, and revise an independent narrative using evidence for every rubric domain.","v":[["focus and organization","task fulfillment, context, point of view, logical sequence, transitions, and conclusion"],["development","narrative techniques and relevant source details that build events and characters"],["language","precise/sensory wording, sentence variety, and transitions"],["conventions","grammar, usage, spelling, capitalization, sentence structure, and punctuation"]],"w":"Write or revise a multi-paragraph continuation. Then create a four-row audit: one exact piece of evidence for each rubric domain, the likely score level, one weakness, and the targeted revision. CONNECTION: Compare this error-analysis process with correcting a Math or Science response. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_v22_fluency","title":"Silent Reading Fluency: Read, Pause & Find Proof","src":"10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • ELA Unit 2 path • Aimsweb Fall 2026 Student Score Snapshots (Reading 10/5/2026; Math 10/5/2026)","teach":"Read a paragraph in meaningful chunks. Pause at the end and say its main point in your own words.\n\nBefore reading, look at the question so you know what to find. During reading, notice words such as however, because, therefore, and for example. After reading, return to the sentence that supports your answer. Read at a comfortable pace and keep the meaning.","model":"MODEL: Passage—“Dogs in one reading program first learn to sit quietly beside children. Because the dogs do not correct mistakes, hesitant readers often continue longer.” Paragraph job: explain why the setting feels safe. Evidence for the result: “do not correct mistakes” and “continue longer.” A fast guess might choose “dogs learn commands,” but the question asks about readers.","connect":"Connect reading rate to meaning: the goal is efficient evidence use, not speed for its own sake. Connect Silent Reading Fluency: Read, Pause & Find Proof to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"I can read a short passage once for structure, return to proof, and answer accurately without rushing.","v":[["meaningful chunk","a phrase or clause read as one idea"],["transition","a word that signals a relationship between ideas"],["paragraph job","what a paragraph contributes to the whole text"],["evidence return","rereading the exact line that supports an answer"]],"w":"Read a fresh 150–250 word informational passage. Write a five-to-eight-word job note for each paragraph, state the central idea, and cite two exact details. CONNECTION: Explain how the same routine would help with a Science ecosystem passage. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject."},{"id":"ela_v21_unit2_map","title":"Animal Allies: Idea, Purpose, Summary & Paragraph Roles","src":"10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • Williamson County Grade 6 Quarter 2 curriculum map • Savvas myPerspectives Unit 2: Animal Allies • IXL 6-GG.8","teach":"A central idea states the most important point an informational text develops. Author’s purpose explains why the author wrote or included a detail. An objective summary combines the central idea and essential details without personal opinions. Paragraph function explains what one paragraph adds to the whole text.\n\nIn “Reading Buddies,” paragraph 3 explains different reading difficulties. Paragraph 6 gives an example of a girl making progress. These paragraphs have different jobs: one explains a problem; the other illustrates a result.\n\nTo compare a written text with a media source, choose one idea and find evidence in each source. Explain what the words tell you and what the image or sound adds.","model":"Question: What does paragraph 8 add to “Reading Buddies”?\n\nFirst, notice the shift: earlier paragraphs explain benefits for children. Paragraph 8 describes people reading to shelter dogs and cats.\n\nAnswer: Paragraph 8 extends the explanation to benefits for animals. The animals become calmer and more comfortable around people. This detail supports the whole-text idea that people and animals can help each other.","connect":"Connect this ELA evidence chain to Science population claims: data is evidence, while the explanation shows how the evidence supports the claim. Connect Animal Allies: Idea, Purpose, Summary & Paragraph Roles to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"I can distinguish and apply all four informational-reading skills without mixing their definitions.","v":[["central idea","the main point an informational text develops"],["author’s purpose","the author’s reason for writing or including a detail"],["objective summary","a brief unbiased account of the most important ideas"],["paragraph function","the role one paragraph plays within the whole text"]],"w":"Create a four-column organizer for central idea, author’s purpose, objective summary, and paragraph function. Use one original short informational passage and complete every column. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject."},{"id":"ela_v21_reading_buddies","title":"Reading Buddies: Text, Media & Main Idea","src":"10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • Williamson County Grade 6 Quarter 2 curriculum map • Savvas myPerspectives Unit 2: Animal Allies • IXL 6-GG.8","teach":"“Reading Buddies” explains how reading partnerships can help children and animals. Animal listeners can make reading practice less stressful. People reading to shelter animals can help those animals become calmer around humans.\n\nParagraphs 1–2 introduce a boy reading to Theo and explain the program’s background. The passage describes Theo as a five-year-old border collie and says the program began in Utah in 1999.\n\nParagraphs 3–5 explain the problem and why a dog can help. Children may struggle for different reasons. A patient listener can reduce worry: a dog lets a child read at a comfortable pace without laughing or interrupting.\n\nParagraphs 6–7 show a result and explain practice. A shy girl finishes a book after weeks of reading with a dog. The example supports the confidence explanation. Practice also gives readers opportunities to notice and correct mistakes.\n\nParagraphs 8–9 extend the idea to animals. Volunteers read to shelter dogs and cats waiting for adoption. The animals become calmer and more comfortable around people. The conclusion brings both kinds of benefit together.\n\nUse details from both parts when stating the whole-text central idea. The girl’s progress is one example; it does not show that every reader has the same needs.","model":"MODEL: Claim—supportive practice can build reading confidence. Evidence—paragraph 6 contrasts a girl’s earlier avoidance of reading aloud with her later completion of a book. Reasoning—the change supplies a concrete example of the confidence explanation in paragraphs 4–5; it does not prove every child will have the same result. Paragraph 8 has a different job: it extends the focus from benefits for children to benefits for shelter animals. Objective summary: The essay explains how animal listeners can make reading practice less stressful and help children improve confidence and skills. It also describes volunteers reading to shelter animals to help them become calmer around people. PHOTO CHECK: the opening photo shows a child, a dog, and an open book; it helps readers visualize the partnership, but cannot establish the program’s date, a child’s thoughts, or long-term growth. Make a four-column organizer: paragraph → precise detail → idea supported → why it supports the idea. Add a separate row for evidence actually visible or audible in the assigned media; never invent a video scene.","connect":"Connect the essay’s two kinds of benefit to Science by recording who benefits and citing observations. A helpful reading session is not automatically evidence of a long-term ecological symbiosis; context and evidence still matter. Connect Reading Buddies: Text, Media & Main Idea to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"I can support the essay’s two-sided central idea with precise paragraph evidence, explain paragraph roles, retrieve the word-network terms, and compare actual print and media evidence without inventing details.","v":[["medium","the form used to communicate information; a photo shows a moment, not a whole history"],["compare","explain meaningful similarities using evidence from each source"],["contrast","explain meaningful differences and their effects"],["emphasis","the importance or attention a source gives an idea"],["participated","took part; the girl participated in reading sessions, rather than merely hearing about them"],["handlers","people responsible for working with the dogs; not the children’s books"],["well-adjusted","able to cope comfortably; the shelter animals become more comfortable with people, not trained to read"],["anxiety","a feeling of worry or nervousness; not proof that a reader lacks ability"],["confidence","trust in one’s ability to try; not a promise of perfect reading"]],"w":"Create nine paragraph-role cards from the paragraph map, mix them, then rebuild the explanation without looking. Use an evidence organizer to support one claim about children and one about shelter animals. Write an objective summary including both benefits. Add participated, handlers, and well-adjusted to a word network with one new relationship word and original sentences. Compare the opening photo with paragraph 2: identify one visible detail and one fact that requires print. Compare the assigned media only after viewing it: one shared idea, one evidence difference, one medium advantage, and a justified conclusion. CONNECTION: In a new community-center example, volunteers read quietly beside nervous shelter cats. Name the prior ELA claim–evidence–reasoning idea, the new animal-care context, what stays the same, what changes, and what observations would support—but not guarantee—a claim that animals become more comfortable. DELAYED RETRIEVAL: Tomorrow, without the paragraph map, explain the different jobs of paragraphs 3, 6, and 8; retrieve all three network words; then write a new two-sentence summary and identify one claim a photograph alone cannot support. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#ELA","cue":"Read → gather evidence → explain the effect → close and retrieve.","task":"Use the evidence organizer or clause sort. Then close the tool and answer a new passage question.","apply":"State a central idea, give two relevant details, and explain what a paragraph contributes. Compare one shared idea across the assigned text and media.","delayed":"Tomorrow, use a different paragraph and explain the idea, evidence, purpose, and one clause without reopening today’s chart."}},{"id":"ela_v21_chimpanzees","title":"My Life with the Chimpanzees","src":"10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • Williamson County Grade 6 Quarter 2 curriculum map • Savvas myPerspectives Unit 2: Animal Allies • IXL 6-GG.8","teach":"An autobiography is an account the author writes about their own life. In My Life with the Chimpanzees, Jane Goodall describes her experiences with animals.\n\nAs you read, find what she observes, how she responds, and what she learns. To cite evidence for a claim, choose a specific event or statement that supports it. Then explain how the evidence supports your idea.\n\nFor paragraph function, ask what the paragraph does: introduce a goal, describe a challenge, explain a method, give an example, or show a result. A summary keeps the central idea and essential events while leaving out personal opinions.","model":"Practice paragraph: “For three mornings, I watched from the same shaded spot. I wrote down when the birds arrived and what they carried. On the fourth morning, I noticed a repeated route.”\n\nQuestion: What is the job of the sentence about writing notes?\n\nAnswer: It explains the method used to collect evidence. Recording the observations helps the narrator recognize a pattern. This practice paragraph is separate from Goodall’s autobiography.","connect":"Connect Goodall’s observations to Science: distinguish direct observation, inference, and a claim supported by repeated evidence. Connect My Life with the Chimpanzees to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"I can explain an idea, purpose, summary, and paragraph role using evidence from the passage I read.","v":[["autobiography","a person’s account of their own life"],["claim","a statement that can be supported with evidence"],["textual evidence","specific information from the text used as support"],["reasoning","the explanation connecting evidence to a claim"]],"w":"After reading the assigned excerpt, write an objective summary and a separate CER paragraph explaining one author’s purpose. Then explain the function of one paragraph within the whole excerpt. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#ELA","cue":"Read → gather evidence → explain the effect → close and retrieve.","task":"Use the evidence organizer or clause sort. Then close the tool and answer a new passage question.","apply":"State a central idea, give two relevant details, and explain what a paragraph contributes. Compare one shared idea across the assigned text and media.","delayed":"Tomorrow, use a different paragraph and explain the idea, evidence, purpose, and one clause without reopening today’s chart."}},{"id":"ela_v21_clauses","title":"Independent & Dependent Clauses","src":"10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • Williamson County Grade 6 Quarter 2 curriculum map • Savvas myPerspectives Unit 2: Animal Allies • IXL 6-GG.8","teach":"A clause contains a subject and a verb. An independent clause expresses a complete thought and can stand alone. A dependent clause also has a subject and verb but begins or functions in a way that leaves the thought incomplete. Common subordinators include because, although, when, while, if, since, after, before, unless, and even though. Use the stand-alone test, but check meaning—not just length. A phrase lacks a full subject-verb structure. Complex sentences combine one independent clause with at least one dependent clause.","model":"MODEL: “Although the chimpanzee watched carefully, it did not copy the action.” “Although the chimpanzee watched carefully” has subject chimpanzee and verb watched but cannot stand alone because although creates an unfinished relationship. “It did not copy the action” is independent.","connect":"Connect clause logic to cause/effect across subjects: dependent clauses often mark cause, condition, time, or contrast, but they need a complete main claim. Connect Independent & Dependent Clauses to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"I can identify clauses in unfamiliar sentences and correctly build and punctuate complex sentences.","v":[["clause","a word group containing a subject and a verb"],["independent clause","a clause expressing a complete thought"],["dependent clause","a clause that cannot stand alone as a complete sentence"],["subordinator","a word that makes a clause dependent and shows its relationship"]],"w":"Classify the clauses in six original sentences. Include a phrase, two independent clauses, two dependent clauses, and one correctly punctuated complex sentence; explain every classification. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#ELA","cue":"Read → gather evidence → explain the effect → close and retrieve.","task":"Use the evidence organizer or clause sort. Then close the tool and answer a new passage question.","apply":"State a central idea, give two relevant details, and explain what a paragraph contributes. Compare one shared idea across the assigned text and media.","delayed":"Tomorrow, use a different paragraph and explain the idea, evidence, purpose, and one clause without reopening today’s chart."}},{"id":"ela_v25_quiz_review","title":"Passage Skills + Clauses Review","src":"October 9 teacher newsletter • ELA Unit 2 “Animal Allies” • “Reading Buddies” • My Life with the Chimpanzees • independent/dependent clause notes • October 23 quiz","teach":"The quiz requires connected reading skills, not isolated definitions. First determine the central or main idea: the most important idea developed across the text. Then identify the author’s purpose and select details that directly support a claim. An objective summary states the central idea and essential developments without opinion or minor detail. Paragraph function explains what one paragraph contributes to the whole text—such as introducing a problem, providing evidence, explaining a method, showing a result, or marking a turning point. For text-to-media comparison, identify a shared idea, compare the evidence or emphasis, and explain how print, audio, or video affects understanding. Finally, classify clauses by structure and meaning: an independent clause has a subject and verb and expresses a complete thought; a dependent clause has a subject and verb but cannot stand alone, often because of a word such as because, although, when, while, if, or since.","model":"Practice comparison:\n\nWritten account: “After several quiet sessions, Maya volunteered to read the final page.”\n\nImagined video scene: Maya pauses, looks toward her partner, and begins after the partner nods.\n\nBoth suggest that support can help a reader try. The written account reports change across sessions. The scene shows hesitation and encouragement through timing and expression. These are practice examples; use details you actually observe when comparing assigned sources.","connect":"Connect reading evidence to Science observation and Social Studies source analysis while preserving ELA vocabulary and rules. Connect Passage Skills + Clauses Review to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"I can explain my passage answers with evidence and classify unfamiliar clauses.","v":[["central idea","the most important idea developed across a text"],["objective summary","a concise, unbiased statement of the central idea and essential developments"],["paragraph function","the contribution one paragraph makes to the whole text"],["medium","the form used to communicate, such as print, audio, or video"],["independent clause","a subject-verb group that expresses a complete thought"],["dependent clause","a subject-verb group that cannot stand alone"]],"w":"Use one fresh informational passage and its media description. Write the central idea, objective summary, one claim with evidence and reasoning, one paragraph-function explanation, and one text-media comparison. Then classify every clause in two complex sentences. CONNECTION: Use the same evidence-and-reasoning discipline in Social Studies importance questions. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#ELA","cue":"Read → gather evidence → explain the effect → close and retrieve.","task":"Use the evidence organizer or clause sort. Then close the tool and answer a new passage question.","apply":"State a central idea, give two relevant details, and explain what a paragraph contributes. Compare one shared idea across the assigned text and media.","delayed":"Tomorrow, use a different paragraph and explain the idea, evidence, purpose, and one clause without reopening today’s chart."}},{"id":"ela_v19_unit_map","title":"Unit 1 Text & Idea Map","src":"Teacher uploads and 10/2 newsletter • myPerspectives Grade 6 Unit 1 classroom sequence","teach":"Unit 1 connects childhood experience, memory, challenge, identity, and change across poetry, memoir, fiction, and informational text. Read across Brown Girl Dreaming, the Michaela DePrince informational text and Dancer's Dream comparison, Oranges, and All Summer in a Day. Study what each text says, how its genre shapes the telling, and which evidence supports a theme or central idea. Do not treat one remembered detail as the whole message.","model":"For All Summer in a Day, a strong analysis would connect a character choice and its consequence to a defensible theme, then cite a precise event. For an informational text, state a central idea and show how two details develop it. The skill is the same evidence chain, but theme and central idea are not interchangeable labels.","connect":"Connect the Unit 1 experiences to Social Studies migration and Science evidence: source type changes what can be claimed. Connect Unit 1 Text & Idea Map to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"I can identify each classroom text, its genre, its idea, and the evidence that develops that idea.","v":[["theme","a defensible message about life developed through literary details"],["central idea","the main point developed in informational text"],["genre","a category of text with characteristic forms"],["evidence","a specific text detail that supports an inference"]],"w":"Choose two Unit 1 texts. State the theme or central idea of each, cite two precise details per text, and explain one meaningful similarity and difference. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject."},{"id":"ela_v19_analysis","title":"Evidence, Point of View & Structure","src":"Teacher uploads and 10/2 newsletter • Unit 1 study guide: theme, point of view, central idea, compare/contrast","teach":"Point of view controls who tells, what the narrator knows, and how close the reader feels to events. Structure controls the order and relationship of ideas or events. Analyze means break a text into parts and explain how they work. Compare finds meaningful similarities; contrast finds meaningful differences. Convey means communicate or make an idea known.","model":"If a first-person narrator says 'I waited,' the reader knows that speaker's account but not every mind. If a writer shifts from present action to a memory, the structure can reveal a cause or deepen motivation. Name the choice, cite the place it occurs, and explain its effect.","connect":"Connect narration to historical sources: every source has access, limits, and an organizing purpose. Connect Evidence, Point of View & Structure to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","success":"I can explain how perspective and structure influence meaning, not merely label them.","v":[["point of view","the position or perspective from which a text is told"],["structure","the organization and relationship of text parts"],["analyze","examine parts and explain relationships"],["convey","communicate an idea or feeling"]],"w":"Analyze how point of view and one structural choice work together in a Unit 1 text. Use two details and explain the effect of each. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_v19_language","title":"Context, Figurative Language, Tone & Mood","src":"Teacher uploads and 10/2 newsletter • photographed Unit 1 study guide IXL CC.1/CC.2, F.3/F.5, D.2","teach":"Context clues include definition, synonym, antonym/contrast, example, and inference from the whole sentence. Figurative language creates meaning beyond the literal words: simile compares with like/as; metaphor makes a direct comparison; personification gives human traits; imagery appeals to senses. Tone is the author's or speaker's attitude. Mood is the feeling the text creates in the reader. Word choice and imagery are evidence for both.","model":"'The hallway swallowed the last bell and held its breath.' The hallway cannot literally swallow or breathe, so personification makes the setting feel tense and empty. The diction supports an uneasy tone and suspenseful mood. The analysis names the device, meaning, and effect.","connect":"Connect word choice to Science precision and Social Studies source interpretation: a term's meaning depends on context and evidence. Connect Context, Figurative Language, Tone & Mood to evidence and author craft: identify the exact word or phrase, determine its meaning or feeling in context, and explain how that language changes tone, mood, character, theme, or central idea. The same precision is needed when Science and Social Studies use domain-specific words.","success":"I can infer vocabulary and explain how language choices shape tone, mood, and meaning.","v":[["context clue","nearby information used to infer meaning"],["figurative language","nonliteral language used for comparison or effect"],["tone","author's or speaker's attitude"],["mood","feeling created in the reader"]],"w":"Use one Unit 1 passage or teacher-provided excerpt. Explain a context clue, a figurative phrase, the tone, and the mood using exact language as evidence. CONNECTION: Use one precise word or phrase as evidence. Explain its meaning in context, its effect, and how that same evidence habit helps in Science or Social Studies."},{"id":"ela_v19_narrative","title":"Narrative Draft, Revision & Independent Proof","src":"Teacher uploads and 10/2 newsletter • narrative assignment • Tennessee narrative rubric","teach":"A complete narrative establishes a situation and point of view, develops characters and events through pacing, dialogue, description, and reflection, uses transitions to control sequence, and ends in a way that follows from the experience. Dialogue must reveal character, advance action, or develop conflict; it should not fill space. Revision changes meaning and organization before editing fixes conventions.","model":"Weak: 'We talked and then it ended.' Stronger planning: slow the decision moment, include one revealing line of dialogue, add a sensory detail tied to the narrator's emotion, then end with a changed understanding shown through action or reflection. Every detail must earn its place.","connect":"Connect narrative sequence to cause/effect chains in Science and historical chronology in Social Studies. Connect Narrative Draft, Revision & Independent Proof to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","success":"I can produce and defend a coherent narrative using the teacher assignment and rubric criteria.","v":[["pacing","the speed and amount of attention given to events"],["dialogue","spoken words that develop character, action, or conflict"],["revision","substantive improvement of ideas, organization, and development"],["resolution","the outcome that follows from the events"]],"w":"Draft or revise the current narrative. Mark the situation/POV, turning point, one purposeful dialogue exchange, one paced sensory moment, transitions, and a resolution that shows change. Then self-score with the rubric. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_oct2_narrative_map","title":"Narrative Essay Roadmap","src":"10/2 teacher newsletter + 9/25 narrative-writing requirements + TN Grades 6–8 Narrative Rubric","teach":"A narrative essay is a developed experience, not a summary or a list of events. First decode the prompt: who tells the story, what situation must be developed, what source details must stay accurate, and what change or outcome the reader must understand. Then plan a beginning that orients the reader, connected events that build toward a turning point, and an ending that follows from what happened.","model":"Prompt: Write from Lena's point of view after she finds an unopened letter. Model the task before drafting: narrator = Lena; launch event = finding the letter; required continuity = source details; development = choices and consequences; ending = what Lena now understands or decides. A random adventure would not answer this prompt.","connect":"Connect prompt analysis to every subject task word: identify, explain, compare, justify, and evaluate each demand a different kind of answer. Connect Narrative Essay Roadmap to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","success":"I can restate every part of a prompt and produce a focused event plan that answers all of it.","v":[["orientation","the opening context that helps the reader understand who, where, and what is happening"],["sequence","the meaningful order of events"],["turning point","the moment when the conflict or character direction changes"],["reflection","thinking that shows what an experience means"]],"w":"Decode this prompt before drafting: Write a narrative from the point of view of a student who discovers that a friend accepted blame for the student's mistake. List narrator, situation, required conflict, source facts to protect, turning point, and believable ending. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_oct2_scene_development","title":"Build the Scene: Dialogue, Pacing & Description","src":"10/2 teacher newsletter + Unit 1 narrative craft + TN narrative rubric","teach":"Development lets the reader experience the important moment. Slow down at the turning point with purposeful dialogue, actions, thoughts, and sensory details. Speed through routine movement with a transition. Dialogue must reveal character, create conflict, change events, or supply necessary information—and it must be punctuated correctly.","model":"Flat: 'I told her the truth and she was mad.' Developed: 'It was mine,' I said, watching her erase her name from the detention form. She capped the marker slowly. 'You waited until now?' The pause, action, and question reveal hurt while moving the conflict.","connect":"Connect narrative development to ELA evidence: a precise action or line of dialogue is also evidence a reader can use to infer motivation and tone. Connect Build the Scene: Dialogue, Pacing & Description to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","success":"I can choose where to slow down and use dialogue, action, thought, and sensory detail for a clear purpose.","v":[["pacing","how quickly or slowly a narrative moves"],["sensory detail","language connected to sight, sound, smell, taste, or touch"],["action beat","a character action near dialogue; it is a separate sentence, not a speaker tag"]],"w":"Write a 10–14 sentence turning-point scene in which a character admits a mistake. Include a purposeful exchange, one action beat, one sensory detail, a change in pacing, and a reaction that makes the next event believable. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_oct2_revision_lab","title":"Rubric Revision Lab","src":"10/2 teacher newsletter + TN Grades 6–8 Narrative Rubric + classroom MagicSchool feedback process","teach":"Revision is decision-making, not accepting every suggestion. Use four lenses: Focus/Organization, Development, Language, and Conventions. For each suggestion—including AI feedback—locate the exact passage, decide whether the suggestion fits the prompt and improves meaning, revise deliberately, and reread the surrounding paragraph. The writer remains responsible for every choice.","model":"Draft: 'I was scared. I went in. I told her.' Diagnosis: the sequence is clear but the turning point is underdeveloped. Revision: add one precise action, one purposeful line, and one reflection—not random adjectives. Then verify dialogue punctuation and that the confession still answers the prompt.","connect":"Connect revision to Science test corrections and Math error analysis: name the exact problem, explain why it is a problem, and make a targeted repair. Connect Rubric Revision Lab to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"I can cite evidence for a rubric judgment, choose a targeted revision, and explain why the revision improves the response.","v":[["focus","how consistently the writing answers the task"],["development","how fully events, characters, and ideas are built"],["conventions","grammar, usage, capitalization, spelling, sentence structure, and punctuation"]],"w":"Revise one paragraph from your narrative. Paste the before version, name the rubric area you are improving, write the revised version, and explain which exact changes improved it. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject."},{"id":"ela_guide_theme","title":"OFFICIAL GUIDE 1 • Theme • IXL B.1/B.2","src":"Teacher-issued Unit 1 Test Study Guide • received 9/30","teach":"THEME is the complete message a literary text develops about life, people, or a topic. A topic is one or two words, such as courage or jealousy. A plot summary tells what happened. To determine theme, trace the conflict, character choices, consequences, and change; then state a message that the evidence supports.","connect":"Connect Theme • IXL B.1/B.2 to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","v":[["theme","a message or insight developed through a literary text"],["topic","the broad subject of a text"],["evidence","a specific detail that supports an idea"]],"w":"State a complete theme for a story you studied in Unit 1. Give two different details and explain how each one develops that theme. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject."},{"id":"ela_guide_pov","title":"OFFICIAL GUIDE 2 • Point of View • IXL C.2/G.1","src":"Teacher-issued Unit 1 Test Study Guide • received 9/30","teach":"POINT OF VIEW on this guide includes BOTH the author's perspective (IXL C.2) and narrative point of view (IXL G.1). Author's perspective is the position or attitude toward the subject, supported by claims, word choices, and details. Narrative point of view identifies who tells the story and what readers can know. First person uses I/we; second person addresses you; third-person limited follows one character's inner experience; omniscient can reveal multiple minds; objective reports observable action and dialogue. Look at narration rather than pronouns inside dialogue. Explain how the perspective affects what readers know or understand.","connect":"Connect Point of View • IXL C.2/G.1 to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","v":[["first person","a narrator inside the story uses I or we"],["third-person limited","a narrator outside the story follows one character's thoughts"],["objective","reporting observable actions without revealing thoughts"]],"w":"Explain how a scene would change if it shifted from one character's first-person point of view to another character's first-person point of view. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_guide_context","title":"OFFICIAL GUIDE 3 • Context Clues • IXL CC.1/CC.2","src":"Teacher-issued Unit 1 Test Study Guide • received 9/30","teach":"CONTEXT CLUES are words and ideas near an unfamiliar word that help determine its meaning. Look for a definition or restatement, synonym, antonym/contrast, example, or an inference from the whole situation. Substitute the possible meaning back into the sentence and check whether it fits both grammar and meaning.","connect":"Connect Context Clues • IXL CC.1/CC.2 to evidence and author craft: identify the exact word or phrase, determine its meaning or feeling in context, and explain how that language changes tone, mood, character, theme, or central idea. The same precision is needed when Science and Social Studies use domain-specific words.","v":[["restatement","a nearby explanation of the same idea"],["contrast clue","a word or phrase showing an opposite idea"],["inference clue","meaning determined from the whole situation"]],"w":"Write one sentence containing a difficult word and enough context for a reader to infer its meaning. Name the clue type you used. CONNECTION: Use one precise word or phrase as evidence. Explain its meaning in context, its effect, and how that same evidence habit helps in Science or Social Studies."},{"id":"ela_guide_tone","title":"OFFICIAL GUIDE 4 • Tone • IXL D.2","src":"Teacher-issued Unit 1 Test Study Guide • received 9/30","teach":"TONE is the author's or speaker's attitude toward the subject. Determine tone from precise word choice, imagery, details, sentence structure, and what is emphasized or omitted. Use an exact tone word—such as admiring, bitter, anxious, playful, critical, or hopeful—and support it with evidence.","connect":"Connect Tone • IXL D.2 to evidence and author craft: identify the exact word or phrase, determine its meaning or feeling in context, and explain how that language changes tone, mood, character, theme, or central idea. The same precision is needed when Science and Social Studies use domain-specific words.","v":[["tone","the author's or speaker's attitude toward the subject"],["diction","an author's choice of words"],["connotation","the feeling or association carried by a word"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use one precise word or phrase as evidence. Explain its meaning in context, its effect, and how that same evidence habit helps in Science or Social Studies."},{"id":"ela_guide_mood","title":"OFFICIAL GUIDE 5 • Mood","src":"Teacher-issued Unit 1 Test Study Guide • received 9/30 • no IXL code listed","teach":"MOOD is the feeling or atmosphere a text creates for the reader. Setting, imagery, sound, pacing, and word choice work together to create it. Name the feeling precisely and explain which details produce it. Do not confuse mood with tone: mood is the reader's experience; tone is the author or speaker's attitude.","connect":"Connect Mood to evidence and author craft: identify the exact word or phrase, determine its meaning or feeling in context, and explain how that language changes tone, mood, character, theme, or central idea. The same precision is needed when Science and Social Studies use domain-specific words.","v":[["mood","the feeling or atmosphere created for the reader"],["atmosphere","the emotional quality surrounding a scene"],["imagery","language that appeals to the senses"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use one precise word or phrase as evidence. Explain its meaning in context, its effect, and how that same evidence habit helps in Science or Social Studies."},{"id":"ela_guide_central","title":"OFFICIAL GUIDE 6 • Central Idea • IXL A.1/A.2","src":"Teacher-issued Unit 1 Test Study Guide • received 9/30","teach":"CENTRAL IDEA is the most important point an informational text develops. It is broader than one detail but specific enough to capture the text's focus. Ask what the author explains repeatedly, group supporting details, and state the idea objectively. A summary includes the central idea and the most important supporting details without opinions or minor information.","connect":"Connect Central Idea • IXL A.1/A.2 to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","v":[["central idea","the main point an informational text develops"],["supporting detail","a fact, example, or explanation that develops the central idea"],["objective summary","a concise account without personal opinion"]],"w":"State the central idea of a recent informational text and list three supporting details. Explain why each detail is important rather than minor. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject."},{"id":"ela_guide_fig","title":"OFFICIAL GUIDE 7 • Figurative Language • IXL F.3/F.5","src":"Teacher-issued Unit 1 Test Study Guide • received 9/30","teach":"FIGURATIVE LANGUAGE creates meaning beyond literal wording. A simile compares using like or as; a metaphor directly identifies one thing as another; personification gives human qualities to something nonhuman; hyperbole deliberately exaggerates. On the test, do two jobs: identify or interpret the figure, then explain its effect on image, meaning, mood, or tone.","connect":"Connect Figurative Language • IXL F.3/F.5 to evidence and author craft: identify the exact word or phrase, determine its meaning or feeling in context, and explain how that language changes tone, mood, character, theme, or central idea. The same precision is needed when Science and Social Studies use domain-specific words.","v":[["simile","a comparison using like or as"],["metaphor","a direct comparison without like or as"],["personification","giving human qualities to something nonhuman"],["hyperbole","deliberate exaggeration for effect"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use one precise word or phrase as evidence. Explain its meaning in context, its effect, and how that same evidence habit helps in Science or Social Studies."},{"id":"ela_guide_words","title":"OFFICIAL GUIDE 8 • Convey • Analyze • Compare • Contrast","src":"Teacher-issued Unit 1 Test Study Guide • yellow-sheet command words • received 9/30","teach":"Test directions are part of the question. CONVEY means communicate or make an idea known. ANALYZE means explain how parts, choices, or evidence work together and why they matter. COMPARE means explain meaningful similarities. CONTRAST means explain meaningful differences. A prompt may require both compare and contrast, so answer every command.","connect":"Connect Convey • Analyze • Compare • Contrast to evidence and author craft: identify the exact word or phrase, determine its meaning or feeling in context, and explain how that language changes tone, mood, character, theme, or central idea. The same precision is needed when Science and Social Studies use domain-specific words.","v":[["convey","communicate or make an idea, feeling, or information known"],["analyze","examine parts and explain relationships, effects, or significance"],["compare","explain meaningful similarities"],["contrast","explain meaningful differences"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use one precise word or phrase as evidence. Explain its meaning in context, its effect, and how that same evidence habit helps in Science or Social Studies."},{"id":"ela_wk0928_prompt","title":"Decode the Writing Prompt","src":"9/25 teacher newsletter + TN Grade 6 narrative-writing/TCAP alignment","teach":"Before writing, identify exactly what the prompt requires: the situation or stimulus, the writing mode, the narrator or point of view if specified, the experience or event to develop, and any source details that must be used. A strong response answers the whole task rather than writing a generic story. Plan before drafting.","connect":"Connect Decode the Writing Prompt to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","v":[["task","what the prompt requires you to do"],["stimulus","the text or information that a writing prompt is based on"],["point of view","the position or perspective from which a narrative is told"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject."},{"id":"ela_wk0928_rubric","title":"TN Narrative Rubric & Anchor-Paper Thinking","src":"9/25 teacher newsletter + Tennessee Grades 6–8 Narrative Rubric","teach":"Tennessee narrative writing is evaluated holistically across focus/organization, development, language, and conventions. High-level writing establishes a relevant context, organizes events logically, uses narrative techniques such as dialogue, pacing, description and reflection to develop events or characters, uses precise language and transitions, provides an effective conclusion, and demonstrates strong command of conventions. When scoring an anchor paper, cite specific evidence for the score rather than relying on whether you simply liked the story.","connect":"Connect TN Narrative Rubric & Anchor-Paper Thinking to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","v":[["holistic","judging the response as a whole using the rubric"],["development","how fully and effectively the writer builds events, experiences, or characters"],["conventions","standard grammar, usage, capitalization, spelling, and punctuation"]],"w":"Read one of your own recent narrative paragraphs. Score it using four headings: Focus/Organization, Development, Language, and Conventions. For each heading, cite one specific piece of evidence from your writing and name one revision that would improve it. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_wk0928_endings","title":"Effective Narrative Endings","src":"9/25 teacher newsletter + TN narrative-writing alignment","teach":"An effective ending follows from the narrated events. It may resolve the immediate conflict, show a realization or reflection, return to an important image or idea from the opening, or end with a meaningful action or line of dialogue. The ending should feel earned. Avoid introducing a new major conflict, abruptly stopping, or attaching a moral that the story did not develop.","connect":"Connect Effective Narrative Endings to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","v":[["resolution","the way a conflict or problem is settled"],["reflection","thought that shows what an experience means to the narrator or character"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_wk0928_dialogue_mastery","title":"Dialogue Mastery Lab","src":"Holt/McDougal dialogue source match + TN conventions + Friday quiz remediation","teach":"Dialogue is both craft and convention. Spoken words belong inside quotation marks. A statement followed by a speaker tag normally uses a comma before the closing quotation mark: “I know,” Maya said. A tag before dialogue normally takes a comma: Maya said, “I know.” A question mark or exclamation point that belongs to the spoken words stays inside the quotation marks. A new speaker starts a new paragraph. An action beat is a complete action, not a speaker tag, so it is punctuated as its own sentence. Dialogue should also reveal character, create or increase conflict, move action, or provide meaningful information.","connect":"Connect Dialogue Mastery Lab to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","v":[["speaker tag","words such as 'Maya said' that identify the speaker"],["action beat","a character action placed near dialogue; it is not grammatically a speaker tag"],["interrupted dialogue","speech broken by a speaker tag or another interruption"]],"w":"Write an 8-line scene between two characters after one realizes the other hid an important object. Use at least one tag before dialogue, one tag after dialogue, one question, one exclamation, one action beat, and one interrupted quotation. Start a new paragraph every time the speaker changes. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_world","title":"Story World & Conflict","src":"Newsletter + Holt/McDougal All Summer in a Day","teach":"Bradbury imagines a Venus where rain dominates life and the sun appears only briefly after a very long interval. Margot remembers the sun because she lived on Earth. Most classmates do not. Her real memory makes her different, and that difference helps create jealousy, disbelief, exclusion, and the central conflict.","connect":"Connect Story World & Conflict to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","v":[["conflict","a struggle between opposing forces"],["infer","figure out an idea from evidence even when it is not stated directly"]],"w":"Why does Margot’s memory of the sun matter to the conflict? Use at least two story details. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_setting","title":"Setting, Mood & Imagery","src":"Holt/McDougal + Grade 6 source-matched lesson","teach":"Setting is more than “Venus.” Bradbury’s repeated rain, darkness, confinement, and brief sunlight shape the emotional experience. Mood is the feeling created in the reader. Imagery is sensory language. A strong answer names the detail and explains its effect.","connect":"Connect Setting, Mood & Imagery to evidence and author craft: identify the exact word or phrase, determine its meaning or feeling in context, and explain how that language changes tone, mood, character, theme, or central idea. The same precision is needed when Science and Social Studies use domain-specific words.","v":[["mood","the feeling a text creates in the reader"],["imagery","language that helps the reader imagine sensory experience"],["oppressive","uncomfortably controlling, heavy, or difficult to escape"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use one precise word or phrase as evidence. Explain its meaning in context, its effect, and how that same evidence habit helps in Science or Social Studies."},{"id":"ela_fig","title":"Figurative Language & Connotation","src":"Newsletter + Holt/McDougal source match","teach":"Metaphor compares without like/as. Simile compares using like/as. Personification gives human qualities to something nonhuman. Hyperbole exaggerates deliberately. Connotation is the feeling or association a word carries beyond its dictionary meaning. The goal is not just naming the device; explain its effect.","connect":"Connect Figurative Language & Connotation to evidence and author craft: identify the exact word or phrase, determine its meaning or feeling in context, and explain how that language changes tone, mood, character, theme, or central idea. The same precision is needed when Science and Social Studies use domain-specific words.","v":[["connotation","the feelings or associations connected to a word"],["personification","giving human qualities to something nonhuman"],["hyperbole","deliberate exaggeration for effect"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use one precise word or phrase as evidence. Explain its meaning in context, its effect, and how that same evidence habit helps in Science or Social Studies."},{"id":"ela_character","title":"Character & Motivation","src":"All Summer in a Day close-reading/source work","teach":"Character analysis uses what someone says, does, thinks, remembers, notices, and how the person changes. Motivation means the reason behind an action. Margot’s longing connects to memory and isolation. The children’s actions can be analyzed through jealousy, group pressure, exclusion, and later remorse.","connect":"Connect Character & Motivation to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","v":[["motivation","the reason behind a character’s action"],["remorse","deep regret or guilt about something done"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_theme","title":"Conflict, Theme & Ending","src":"Holt/McDougal after-reading + CT Grade 6 lesson","teach":"Theme is a complete message, not a one-word topic. Trace conflict → actions → consequences → change → message. The ending matters because the children’s behavior after the rain returns requires the reader to infer what they now understand about Margot’s loss.","connect":"Connect Conflict, Theme & Ending to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","v":[["theme","a message or insight developed through a text"],["consequence","a result that follows an action"]],"w":"State one defensible theme and support it with two different events from the story. Explain how each event supports the theme. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_evidence","title":"Claim • Evidence • Reasoning","src":"Grade 6 evidence-based source work","teach":"CLAIM = what you are arguing. EVIDENCE = the specific story detail that supports it. REASONING = how that detail proves the claim. A quotation or event alone is not the explanation.","connect":"Connect Evidence • Reasoning to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","v":[["evidence","a relevant detail used to support a claim"],["reasoning","the explanation connecting evidence to a claim"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject."},{"id":"ela_narrative","title":"Narrative Orientation & Organization","src":"Current newsletter narrative essay elements","teach":"Orient the reader by establishing enough of who, where, when, and situation to enter the story without an information dump. Events should follow logically. Transitions clarify movement. Pacing slows important moments and moves faster through less important ones.","connect":"Connect Narrative Orientation & Organization to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","v":[["orient","help the reader understand the basic situation and context"],["pacing","how quickly or slowly a narrative moves through events"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_dialogue","title":"Purposeful Dialogue","src":"Newsletter + Holt/McDougal pp. 74–77 source match","teach":"Dialogue can reveal character, develop a conflict, or move the action forward. Ask what changes because of the spoken words.\n\nWhen continuing All Summer in a Day after Margot is released, choose words that fit what the characters have experienced. A hesitant apology or an unanswered question can reveal tension without naming every emotion.","connect":"Connect Purposeful Dialogue to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","v":[["dialogue","spoken words between characters in a text"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_punct","title":"Dialogue Punctuation","src":"Holt/McDougal grammar-in-context source match","teach":"Spoken words go inside quotation marks. Tag after statement: “We should go,” said Eli. Tag before dialogue: Eli said, “We should go.” New speaker usually starts a new paragraph. Question/exclamation marks stay with spoken words when they belong to the speech.","connect":"Connect Dialogue Punctuation to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","v":[["speaker tag","words that identify who is speaking, such as “said Eli”"]],"w":"Write 4–6 lines of dialogue beginning when Margot is released. Reveal guilt or discomfort without using the sentence “I feel guilty.” Use correct dialogue punctuation. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_taskwords","title":"Academic Task Words","src":"Grade 6 ELA task language + classroom response demands","teach":"ANALYZE = explain relationships, effects, or significance. INFER = use evidence to reach an unstated conclusion. JUSTIFY = support a conclusion with evidence/reasoning. SUMMARIZE = give the important ideas/events without unnecessary detail or opinion. COMPARE = explain similarities; CONTRAST = explain differences.","connect":"Connect Academic Task Words to evidence and author craft: identify the exact word or phrase, determine its meaning or feeling in context, and explain how that language changes tone, mood, character, theme, or central idea. The same precision is needed when Science and Social Studies use domain-specific words.","v":[["analyze","explain relationships, effects, or significance"],["justify","support a conclusion with evidence and reasoning"],["infer","reach a conclusion from evidence"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use one precise word or phrase as evidence. Explain its meaning in context, its effect, and how that same evidence habit helps in Science or Social Studies."},{"id":"ela_source_close","title":"All Summer in a Day • Close Reading Challenge","src":"Holt/McDougal Grade 6 All Summer in a Day • classroom source-match targets","teach":"Close reading connects a specific detail with what it shows. In All Summer in a Day, the rain, Margot’s memories, the children’s choices, and the ending work together.\n\nStart with one detail. Explain what it suggests about a character or conflict. Then show how it supports a larger idea. Retelling an event is the first step; explaining why it matters completes the answer.","connect":"Connect Close Reading Challenge to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","v":[["close reading","careful reading that uses specific details to explain meaning"],["text evidence","a specific detail from the text that supports an idea"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject."},{"id":"ela_inference_depth","title":"Inference • Evidence • Character Change","src":"Holt/McDougal pp. 74–77 source-match • Grade 6 post-reading/inference work","teach":"An inference combines evidence with reasoning. Track what a character does before and after an important event. Explain what the change suggests.\n\nFor example, a character who once laughed at someone but later avoids their eyes may feel ashamed. The action supports that inference, even when the author never uses the word ashamed.","connect":"Connect Evidence • Character Change to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","v":[["motivation","a reason that helps explain why a character acts"],["character development","how a character is revealed or changes through actions, thoughts, dialogue, and events"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_fig_source","title":"Figurative Language • Effect, Not Labels","src":"Classroom figurative-language focus + Holt/McDougal All Summer in a Day","teach":"Figurative language creates an image or meaning beyond the literal words. First identify the device. Then explain its effect.\n\n“The rain hammered the roof” makes the rain seem forceful. The verb hammered creates a strong sound image and may make the scene feel harsh. Connect your explanation to the exact words in the passage.","connect":"Connect Effect, Not Labels to evidence and author craft: identify the exact word or phrase, determine its meaning or feeling in context, and explain how that language changes tone, mood, character, theme, or central idea. The same precision is needed when Science and Social Studies use domain-specific words.","v":[["effect","the result a word choice or technique has on meaning or the reader"],["imagery","language that creates sensory pictures or impressions"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use one precise word or phrase as evidence. Explain its meaning in context, its effect, and how that same evidence habit helps in Science or Social Studies."},{"id":"ela_dialogue_quiz","title":"Dialogue Punctuation • Quiz Practice","src":"Current classroom dialogue punctuation quiz/IXL target • Holt/McDougal grammar-in-context work","teach":"Put spoken words inside quotation marks. A statement followed by a speaker tag uses a comma inside the closing quotation mark: “I found it,” Nia said.\n\nA tag before speech takes a comma: Nia said, “I found it.” A new speaker starts a new paragraph. Keep a question mark inside the quotation marks when the spoken words form the question.","connect":"Connect Quiz Practice to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","v":[["speaker tag","words that identify who is speaking, such as she said"],["dialogue","the exact spoken words of characters"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_narrative_craft_q","title":"Narrative Writing • Character, Pacing & Ending","src":"Current classroom Narrative Essay Elements • character development • endings • purposeful dialogue","teach":"A narrative introduces a situation and develops connected events. Slow down an important moment with action, dialogue, thoughts, and sensory details. Use transitions to move through less important events.\n\nEnd with an action, result, or reflection that grows from the story. For each writing choice, ask: What does this help the reader understand?","connect":"Connect Character, Pacing & Ending to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","v":[["orientation","the opening information that helps a reader understand the situation, setting, and characters"],["pacing","how quickly or slowly a narrative moves through events"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_mixed_checkpoint","title":"Reading & Writing Checkpoint","src":"Current classroom ELA + Holt/McDougal source-match • comprehension, inference, analysis, dialogue, narrative craft","teach":"Read each question carefully. Decide whether it asks you to infer, choose evidence, explain an effect, or edit a sentence. Use the passage or sentence to support your answer.","connect":"Connect Reading & Writing Checkpoint to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","v":[],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject."},{"id":"ela_ixl_kmu_exact","title":"IXL KMU • Figures of Speech: Meaning & Tone","src":"Exact assigned IXL skill KMU • Analyze the effects of figures of speech on meaning and tone","teach":"Read the whole sentence before identifying a figure of speech. Ask what the comparison, image, or exaggeration suggests.\n\n“The news hit like a cold wave” is a simile. It suggests an overwhelming, unpleasant reaction. Explaining that reaction tells more than naming the device alone.","connect":"Connect Figures of Speech: Meaning & Tone to evidence and author craft: identify the exact word or phrase, determine its meaning or feeling in context, and explain how that language changes tone, mood, character, theme, or central idea. The same precision is needed when Science and Social Studies use domain-specific words.","v":[["tone","the attitude or feeling created by the writer’s language"],["effect","what a language choice causes the reader to understand, picture, or feel"],["hyperbole","deliberate exaggeration used for emphasis or effect"],["personification","giving human actions or qualities to something nonhuman"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use one precise word or phrase as evidence. Explain its meaning in context, its effect, and how that same evidence habit helps in Science or Social Studies."},{"id":"ela_ixl_fnt_exact","title":"IXL FNT • Formatting Quotations & Dialogue","src":"Exact assigned IXL skill FNT • Formatting quotations and dialogue","teach":"When editing dialogue, check the spoken words, the speaker tag, capitalization, and punctuation.\n\nCorrect: “Are you ready?” Mina asked. The question mark belongs to Mina’s spoken question, so it stays inside the quotation marks. The tag continues the sentence, so asked begins with a lowercase letter.","connect":"Connect Formatting Quotations & Dialogue to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","v":[["direct quotation","the exact words spoken or written by a person or source"],["speaker tag","words such as she said or he asked that identify the speaker"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude."},{"id":"ela_mixed_practice_2","title":"★ RANDOM MIXED PRACTICE 2 • ELA • 15 New Questions","src":"Current classroom ELA • Holt/McDougal All Summer in a Day • IXL KMU + FNT","teach":"NEW MIXED RETRIEVAL. The 15 questions appear in a different random order each time this set is started. Skills are intentionally mixed: figurative-language effects (KMU), formatting quotations/dialogue (FNT), inference, evidence, character, setting/mood, theme, and narrative craft. Answer choices are also shuffled.","connect":"Connect ELA • 15 New Questions to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","v":[],"w":"Write one paragraph analyzing how a setting can affect both a character's choices and the mood. Use a specific example from All Summer in a Day or another class text. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject."},{"id":"ela_v31_goodall_intro","title":"Jane Goodall: Curiosity, Perspective & Evidence","src":"Teacher-assigned Intro to Jane Goodall • Schoology Animal Allies • five activity prompts reviewed October 21, 2026 • original parallel practice","link":"https://wcschools.schoology.com/external_tool/3573938304/launch","teach":"A person’s perspective is a way of seeing or responding to something. Inquisitive means eager to investigate and ask questions. Astounded means greatly surprised. Inquisitive describes curiosity; astounded describes a response to something unexpected. They are not interchangeable. Indifferent and apathetic mean showing little interest or concern; jaded suggests interest or enthusiasm worn down by experience. Intrigued means interested enough to want to learn more. A POV claim needs an observable action or specific words as evidence, followed by reasoning. Do not infer a person’s entire viewpoint from one smile.\n\nThe introduction asks you to activate prior knowledge, explain a reaction to a discovery, explain interest in Gombe, identify a viewpoint, and connect Goodall’s story to how humans and animals help each other. Respect the different response lengths: 1–2 sentences for prior knowledge, 2–3 for interest in Gombe, and 3–4 for the unit connection. Sentence count is a minimum task constraint, not a substitute for evidence.\n\nKeep sources separate: a video introduction builds background; an autobiography presents an author’s account of her own experiences. When answering about My Life with the Chimpanzees, use the assigned excerpt, not an imagined scene or a general biography. Distinguish learning from animals through observation from an intentional act of help by an animal. A claim that people help animals requires evidence of care, protection, or conservation—not merely proof that a person watched them.","model":"ORIGINAL PRACTICE SCENE, not a transcript: A researcher notices a bird use a twig to reach food. She says she did not expect it, records what happened, and returns to watch again. Claim 1: her reaction is astounded. Evidence: she reports surprise. Claim 2: her approach is inquisitive. Evidence: she records observations and returns with questions. Reasoning: continued investigation supports curiosity; surprise alone would not. For a 3–4-sentence unit response, state the connection, supply an actual source detail, explain what humans learn, and identify what evidence is needed before claiming humans benefit the animals.","connect":"Connect perspective analysis to Science observation versus inference: words and actions are evidence, while claims about motives require reasoning and limits. Connect Jane Goodall: Curiosity, Perspective & Evidence to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"I can distinguish curiosity from surprise, support a viewpoint with actual source evidence, and fulfill every part of a short constructed-response task.","v":[["inquisitive","curious and eager to investigate; repeated questions fit, boredom does not"],["astounded","greatly surprised; an unexpected observation may cause it"],["intrigued","interested and wanting to learn more"],["apathetic","showing little interest or concern; a contrast to inquisitive"],["jaded","no longer enthusiastic after repeated experience; not simply tired for one afternoon"],["perspective","a way of understanding or responding; support it with source evidence"]],"w":"Return to the actual assigned introduction, not the original bird practice scene. Answer the prior-knowledge task in 1–2 sentences; explain interest in Gombe in 2–3 sentences with a source detail; and answer the animal–human relationship question in 3–4 sentences. Underline the detail and circle the reasoning. CONNECTION: Compare this ELA evidence routine with a new Science observer studying ants. Name the prior and new ideas, what stays the same, what changes, and the evidence needed for a valid claim. DELAYED RETRIEVAL: Next session, distinguish inquisitive, astounded, apathetic, and jaded without the vocabulary list; analyze a new observer’s behavior and explain one unsupported inference. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject."},{"id":"ela_v33_reading_challenge","title":"Quiz Practice A: Evidence, Summary & Media","src":"October 23 teacher quiz scope; uploaded Reading Buddies paragraphs 1–9; original observation passages and imagined media descriptions for transfer; Purdue OWL clause guidance; ReadWriteThink comparison organizer. Assigned My Life with the Chimpanzees excerpt remains unrecovered; no invented quotation or event is attributed to it.","teach":"Start with the question: identify the central idea, choose evidence, explain a paragraph’s job, or compare media. A topic names a subject; a central idea states what the passage develops about it. An objective summary includes the main developments without your reaction.\n\nEvidence must fit the claim. One scene can show a visible action, but it cannot establish a six-week trend. An author can explain both an observation and a limit on what that observation proves. For paragraph function, explain what would be lost if the paragraph disappeared.\n\nFor an autobiography such as My Life with the Chimpanzees, distinguish the author’s lived experience from your background knowledge. The original heron account in this practice lets you apply those reading skills to a fresh first-person text. It is a separate passage.\n\nUse a three-column comparison: print evidence / shared idea / media evidence. Then add how each form affects your understanding. Close the organizer before answering a fresh comparison.","model":"Separate worked example: A report says a student volunteered after a month of practice. A photo shows the student holding an open book. The report supports a change over time; the photo shows one moment. Both concern reading, but only the report supplies the earlier-to-later comparison. A careful claim stays within the evidence.","connect":"ELA and Science both require evidence that fits the claim. A first-person account and a population study use different evidence, but neither makes an unobserved cause certain. Connect Quiz Practice A: Evidence, Summary & Media to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"I can explain the evidence, the paragraph’s contribution, and the limit on a conclusion. I can compare print and media without inventing details.","v":[["relevant evidence","a detail that directly supports the particular claim"],["paragraph function","the job a paragraph does in developing the whole text"],["objective summary","a brief account of the main ideas without personal judgment"],["limitation","a boundary on what evidence can establish"],["medium","a communication form, such as print, audio, or video"]],"w":"REFERENCE PASSAGES FOR WRITING\n\nORIGINAL PRACTICE ACCOUNT — This is not an excerpt from My Life with the Chimpanzees.\n\n(1) I expected to see the herons at sunrise, but the pond was empty. For two mornings, I left after ten minutes.\n(2) On the third morning, I stayed farther from the water and recorded arrival times. I continued this routine for a week. The herons usually arrived after the shaded bank became sunny.\n(3) My notes suggested when to return, but they did not establish why the birds chose that time. I needed more observations before explaining their behavior.\n\nORIGINAL PRACTICE PAIR\n\nArticle: For six weeks, volunteers read beside shelter dogs. Staff recorded how often the dogs approached visitors. Several dogs approached more often by the final week. The staff said other changes in care might also have contributed.\nVideo description: A short clip shows one dog approaching a seated reader. The viewer hears a quiet reading voice and sees the dog settle nearby. No earlier sessions or visitor counts appear.\n\nComplete four short tasks in your own words.\n\n1. Reading Buddies: Write a two-sentence objective summary that includes benefits for children and shelter animals.\n\n2. Original heron account: Make one claim about the narrator’s approach. Give two precise details and explain how each supports the claim.\n\n3. Original shelter media pair: Write 3–4 sentences identifying one shared idea, one detail from each source, and how the forms affect what you can conclude.\n\n4. Paragraph function: Explain how paragraph 3 of the heron account changes the conclusion a reader should draw from paragraph 2.\n\nConnection: Apply the distinction between observation and explanation to a Science population graph. Identify the prior reading idea, the new setting, what stays the same, what changes, and what evidence is needed.\n\nDelayed retrieval: Tomorrow, close these passages. Explain the jobs of Reading Buddies paragraphs 6 and 8, then classify one new claim as observed or inferred. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject."},{"id":"extra_v35_ela_a","title":"Reading, evidence and sentences · practice A","src":"October 23 teacher newsletter scope; original extra practice, form A. Assigned private ELA guide and math practice test remain unverified.","teach":"Read the labeled study retelling beside each reading question. It is supplemental, not the assigned autobiography. For sentence questions, locate subject and verb, decide whether each clause stands alone, and then check how clauses join.","model":"“Although the rain stopped, the trail stayed wet.” Although makes the first clause dependent, while the second can stand alone: complex. “The rain stopped, but the trail stayed wet” has two independent clauses: compound.","connect":"Use ELA evidence and reasoning to explain the geographic or mathematical relationship. Connect Reading, evidence and sentences · practice A to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"Complete first responses, repair missed skills, and return later for the other form. Writing needs review before readiness can be determined.","v":[],"w":"CONNECTION: Complete the written items in focused practice. Connect the prior idea to the new context; say what stays the same, what changes and which source detail or calculation supports your conclusion. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject.","learningTools":{"cue":"Work one item at a time.","task":"Open form A including written work and visual questions.","url":"exam-practice.html?set=extra_v35_ela_a","apply":"Save your explanation before viewing the model response.","delayed":"Use the other form in a later session without notes."}},{"id":"extra_v35_ela_b","title":"Reading, evidence and sentences · practice B","src":"October 23 teacher newsletter scope; original extra practice, form B. Assigned private ELA guide and math practice test remain unverified.","teach":"Read the labeled study retelling beside each reading question. It is supplemental, not the assigned autobiography. For sentence questions, locate subject and verb, decide whether each clause stands alone, and then check how clauses join.","model":"“Although the rain stopped, the trail stayed wet.” Although makes the first clause dependent, while the second can stand alone: complex. “The rain stopped, but the trail stayed wet” has two independent clauses: compound.","connect":"Use ELA evidence and reasoning to explain the geographic or mathematical relationship. Connect Reading, evidence and sentences · practice B to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"Complete first responses, repair missed skills, and return later for the other form. Writing needs review before readiness can be determined.","v":[],"w":"CONNECTION: Complete the written items in focused practice. Connect the prior idea to the new context; say what stays the same, what changes and which source detail or calculation supports your conclusion. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject.","learningTools":{"cue":"Work one item at a time.","task":"Open form B including written work and visual questions.","url":"exam-practice.html?set=extra_v35_ela_b","apply":"Save your explanation before viewing the model response.","delayed":"Use the other form in a later session without notes."}},{"id":"ela_v37_assigned_close_read","title":"Goodall: repetition, contrast and punctuation","src":"Assigned Savvas Unit 2 memoir extract, My Life With the Chimpanzees, pp.101-110, paragraphs 1-61; complete PDF recovered October 28, 2026. Original paraphrase-based study checks. Separate teacher study guide remains inaccessible.","teach":"A memoir recounts the author’s own experiences. First-person narration gives direct access to Goodall’s feelings but does not independently prove what an animal thinks. Structure and word choices help communicate her gradual progress. In paragraph 8, repeated reports of not finding chimps stretch the sense of waiting. In paragraph 17, the slower acceptance by chimps is contrasted with baboons becoming comfortable. Paragraph 32 uses a dash and exclamation point to emphasize a surprising change. Paragraphs 48-49 describe individual appearance, personality and family relationships instead of treating every chimp as identical. When explaining an author’s choice, name the technique, cite a paragraph detail, then explain its effect.","model":"Original paraphrase of paragraphs 8-9 (p.102): brief sightings are followed by several fruitless days; even a later long-distance viewing does not reveal enough detail. Model: repetition emphasizes sustained frustration. This helps explain why later close observation matters. A summary says what happened; analysis explains why the author tells it this way.","connect":"Connect evidence-based ELA analysis to the science distinction between an observation and an inference. Connect Goodall: repetition, contrast and punctuation to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"Save first answers, explain every miss, complete fresh repair, then use the new-format delayed task after at least 24 hours. Writing needs review; practice completion is not mastery.","v":[["memoir","A nonfiction account of experiences remembered by its author."],["repetition","Repeating a word, phrase or pattern for an effect."],["contrast","Placing differences together to clarify them."],["impetuous","Acting suddenly with little thought."],["dominate","Rule or control."],["irritable","Easily annoyed."],["miserable","Very unhappy or uncomfortable."],["vanished","Disappeared."],["threateningly","In an alarming or frightening manner."]],"w":"CONNECTION: With the assigned class PDF open, mark evidence in paragraphs 8,17,32,48-49 and explain each author choice. This is supported practice, not closed-note evidence. Then close it and analyze repetition/contrast in a new field-journal example you create. Compare the role of evidence in ELA and science: what stays, what changes, and which details support the connection? CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject.","learningTools":{"cue":"Read the quantities and labels before choosing a method.","task":"Open matching practice, diagrams, printable student copy and written transfer. These repeat the same lesson checks; do not count a repeated answer as fresh evidence.","apply":"CONNECTION: With the assigned class PDF open, mark evidence in paragraphs 8,17,32,48-49 and explain each author choice. This is supported practice, not closed-note evidence. Then close it and analyze repetition/contrast in a new field-journal example you create. Compare the role of evidence in ELA and science: what stays, what changes, and which details support the connection?","delayed":"After at least 24 hours, make a new example with different quantities or organisms. Explain it without notes; have an adult review it.","url":"exam-practice.html?set=ela_v37_assigned_close_read"}},{"id":"ela_v37_assigned_arc","title":"Goodall: follow the whole memoir arc","src":"Assigned Savvas Unit 2 memoir extract, My Life With the Chimpanzees, pp.101-110, paragraphs 1-61; complete PDF recovered October 28, 2026. Original paraphrase-based study checks. Separate teacher study guide remains inaccessible.","teach":"Track how the research develops rather than remembering only tool use. Paragraphs 1-7 establish arrival, camp and limited resources. Paragraphs 8-17 show setbacks, changed observation methods and slow acceptance. Paragraphs 18-30 develop forest risks, daily routines and her mother’s help. Paragraphs 31-39 show David Greybeard’s visits and the tool observation. Paragraphs 40-46 combine rainy-season difficulties, a threatening encounter and careful daily records. Paragraphs 47-59 develop individual personalities, family learning, communication and social rank. Paragraphs 60-61 explain expansion from notes to recordings, assistants and a research center. Chronology organizes the overall account, while local passages use comparison, cause/effect and description.","model":"Original paraphrase, paragraphs 38-39 (p.106): David inserts plant material into a termite mound; later he removes a twig’s leaves before using it. The first action shows tool use; the modification supports toolmaking. Paragraph 39 explains the scientific significance of the observation in the historical context. Keep a distinction between behavior, interpretation and significance.","connect":"Scientific evidence and ELA structure both require relating a detail to the larger explanation. Connect Goodall: follow the whole memoir arc to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"Save first answers, explain every miss, complete fresh repair, then use the new-format delayed task after at least 24 hours. Writing needs review; practice completion is not mastery.","v":[["chronology","Organization by time order."],["significance","Why a detail or event matters."],["evidence","Details that support an explanation."]],"w":"CONNECTION: Create a seven-stage timeline using paragraph ranges 1-7,8-17,18-30,31-39,40-46,47-59,60-61. Cite one exact paragraph detail per stage using the class PDF; do not copy long passages. Then close it and explain how systematic recording connects to science: prior/new ideas, similarities, differences and evidence. Delayed task: reconstruct the arc without notes after 24 hours. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject.","learningTools":{"cue":"Read the quantities and labels before choosing a method.","task":"Open matching practice, diagrams, printable student copy and written transfer. These repeat the same lesson checks; do not count a repeated answer as fresh evidence.","apply":"CONNECTION: Create a seven-stage timeline using paragraph ranges 1-7,8-17,18-30,31-39,40-46,47-59,60-61. Cite one exact paragraph detail per stage using the class PDF; do not copy long passages. Then close it and explain how systematic recording connects to science: prior/new ideas, similarities, differences and evidence. Delayed task: reconstruct the arc without notes after 24 hours.","delayed":"After at least 24 hours, make a new example with different quantities or organisms. Explain it without notes; have an adult review it.","url":"exam-practice.html?set=ela_v37_assigned_arc"}},{"id":"ela_v38_spiral_writing","title":"Spiral writing coach: dialogue, point of view and revision","src":"Released October 19-23 Schoology spiral directions and 14-point writing rubric, read October 28, 2026. New original practice scenes. This week’s prompt and rubric are not visible before an attempt, so current scope is not asserted.","teach":"Start with the prompt, not the first sentence. Circle WHO tells the story, WHAT section to rewrite and WHAT must stay.\n\nDIALOGUE is speech said aloud. ACTION tells what happens. INNER THOUGHT tells what the narrator thinks. A sound effect such as Click! is not a character speaking. Include useful speech together with action and thought; avoid a page made only of quotations.\n\nFor a statement plus speech tag: “Hold the edge,” Zoe said. Put the comma inside the closing quotation mark. For a spoken question: “Is it ready?” Zoe asked. Do not add a comma after the question mark. A tag describes speaking, such as said or asked. An action such as smiled needs its own sentence: “It is ready.” Zoe smiled. Start a new paragraph when the speaker changes.\n\nIn a first-person rewrite, I always names the assigned narrator. Keep the original main events and order; change which character’s experience we follow. Give that narrator fitting thoughts, feelings, observations and dialogue. Do not turn another character’s unspoken thoughts into facts.\n\nCheck the actual rubric before submitting. Our rehearsal uses four checks from the released class rubric: point of view, appropriate dialogue and punctuation, clear source-consistent plot, and one or two fitting pieces of figurative language. Figurative language does not replace spoken dialogue.","model":"Different practice scene: A school-play curtain catches. Nia holds the ladder while Leo frees the fabric.\n\nRewrite from Nia’s view: I planted both hands on the ladder. My stomach fluttered like a trapped moth. “I have the ladder,” I told Leo.\n\nHe eased the curtain off the hook. “It is free!” he called.\n\nI kept holding until he reached the floor.\n\nWhy it works: I is Nia throughout. The ladder and curtain events stay in order. Two short spoken exchanges fit the action. The comparison reveals Nia’s feeling without claiming Leo’s private thoughts. The new speaker begins a new paragraph in a finished draft.","connect":"Use the science distinction between observable behavior and inferred thinking. Changing narrator does not give access to every person’s thoughts. Connect Spiral writing coach: dialogue, point of view and revision to cause/effect and point of view. A writer's choices control what the reader knows, how events unfold, and why a character changes. Use the same sequence thinking to explain a historical migration or a Science system without inventing evidence.","success":"Finish the independent language checks and the new written draft. Have an adult review dialogue separately from point of view and plot. Corrected work preserves the original score; use a new scene for later independent application.","v":[["dialogue","Words a character says aloud."],["dialogue tag","Words that identify who speaks, such as Zoe asked."],["action beat","A sentence showing a character’s movement or behavior."],["inner thought","What the point-of-view narrator thinks silently."],["perspective","The viewpoint through which events are experienced."],["figurative language","Language such as a simile or metaphor that describes through comparison."],["comma splice","Two complete clauses incorrectly joined by a comma alone."]],"w":"CONNECTION: Open the matching spiral-coach activity and complete the planning, drafting and revision steps using its original source scene. Include two useful spoken lines, consistent assigned first-person narration, the same main events and one fitting comparison. Then compare changing narrators in ELA with describing a science investigation from a partner’s view: what stays, what changes and what evidence constrains both? Written work needs review. CONNECTION: Explain one cause-and-effect chain in the lesson, then show how changing point of view or evidence would change what a reader can conclude.","learningTools":{"url":"exam-practice.html?set=ela_v38_spiral_writing","cue":"WHO → SAME EVENTS → SPEECH → ACTION/THOUGHT → CHECK.","task":"Use the short steps to plan, write and revise. The focused multiple-choice questions mirror this lesson; repeated items are not fresh evidence.","apply":"Write the new Zoe-perspective scene and check spoken dialogue separately from sound effects.","delayed":"After at least 24 hours, rewrite the new comic-club scene without reopening the model. Parent reviews a separate draft; do not count an early or supported response as delayed mastery."}},{"id":"ela_v39_friday_sentences","title":"Friday sentence quiz · fresh clause and comma practice","src":"October 30 compound/complex quiz and IXL GG9 in teacher newsletter; uploaded Compare + Contrast grammar scavenger hunt. Original practice, not actual test questions. Rule verification: Purdue OWL Extended Rules for Commas and Sentence Punctuation Patterns, October 29.","teach":"Friday October 30 focus: compound and complex sentences, independent/dependent clauses and comma placement; teacher newsletter and worksheet scope recovered October 28. Exact quiz questions are not available. IXL GG9 remains the assigned companion.\n\nCOUNT COMPLETE THOUGHTS. Independent: subject + verb + complete thought. Dependent: subject + verb but an unfinished thought, often starting because, although, when, before or after. A phrase does not have its own subject-verb pair.\n\nSIMPLE: one independent clause, even with two actions. COMPOUND: two independent clauses, no dependent clause. Use comma + FANBOYS (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so), or a semicolon between related complete clauses. COMPLEX: one independent plus at least one dependent clause. Opening dependent clause, then comma + main clause. Main clause followed by an essential because/when clause usually needs no comma.\n\nTRAPS: and can join two verbs without making a compound sentence. A comma alone cannot join two complete sentences. A because fragment still needs a main clause. Choose conjunctions for meaning: because gives a reason; although shows contrast. Compound-complex contains two independent clauses AND a dependent clause; it is an extension, not a verified separate quiz requirement.","model":"Mina cut the cloth and pinned the sleeve. → SIMPLE: Mina performs both actions.\nMina cut the cloth, and Eli pinned the sleeve. → COMPOUND: each side has a subject, verb and complete thought.\nWhen Mina cut the cloth, Eli pinned the sleeve. → COMPLEX: When Mina cut the cloth cannot stand alone.\nEli pinned the sleeve when Mina finished cutting. → COMPLEX: the essential time clause follows, so no comma is needed here.","connect":"Use the same clause test when explaining cause and effect in science or social studies. Grammatical shape and factual accuracy are separate checks. Connect Friday sentence quiz · fresh clause and comma practice to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"Aim for at least 90% independent selected responses plus correct written application. Review every miss and use fresh practice. No readiness claim from completion alone.","v":[["independent clause","A subject-verb group expressing a complete thought."],["dependent clause","A subject-verb group that cannot stand alone as a complete sentence."],["compound predicate","Two or more actions sharing a subject; this alone does not create a compound sentence."],["comma splice","Two independent clauses incorrectly joined by a comma alone."]],"w":"Fresh transfer: Write three connected sentences about preparing a fashion display: one simple, one compound and one complex. Label every clause and justify each comma. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject.","learningTools":{"url":"exam-practice.html?set=ela_v39_friday_sentences","cue":"SUBJECT → VERB → COMPLETE? → COUNT → PUNCTUATE","task":"Complete the fresh set without notes after reading the model.","apply":"Fresh transfer: Write three connected sentences about preparing a fashion display: one simple, one compound and one complex. Label every clause and justify each comma.","delayed":"Use a new topic and new sentences after at least 24 hours; review writing separately."}},{"id":"ela_v40_sentence_types","title":"Sentence Types · four structures quiz rehearsal","src":"Parent clarification October 29: sentence-types quiz October 30. Uploaded grammar worksheet; original rehearsal items. Purdue OWL sentence structure and punctuation rules. Actual test questions not recovered.","teach":"Sentence types are determined by CLAUSES, not length, punctuation alone, or the number of verbs.\n\nINDEPENDENT CLAUSE: a subject and verb expressing a complete thought. DEPENDENT CLAUSE: a subject and verb that cannot stand alone, often introduced by because, although, when, after, if or while. A phrase does not contain its own subject-verb pair.\n\nSIMPLE = 1 independent + 0 dependent. A compound subject or several actions can still make a simple sentence.\nCOMPOUND = 2 or more independent + 0 dependent. Both complete clauses may be joined by comma + FANBOYS or a semicolon.\nCOMPLEX = 1 independent + 1 or more dependent. A dependent clause can come first or last.\nCOMPOUND-COMPLEX = 2 or more independent + 1 or more dependent.\n\nMETHOD: Find subjects and verbs. Put brackets around each clause. Test which clauses stand alone. Count independent and dependent clauses. Match the counts to a type. Do not count and unless it joins complete clauses.\n\nPunctuation supports the structure: comma before FANBOYS between independent clauses; comma after an opening dependent clause. These are sentence STRUCTURES. Declarative, interrogative, imperative and exclamatory name sentence PURPOSES, a different classification.","model":"SIMPLE: Eva sketched and painted. [Eva sketched and painted] = 1 independent.\nCOMPOUND: Eva sketched, and Sam painted. [Eva sketched] + [Sam painted] = 2 independent.\nCOMPLEX: While Eva sketched, Sam painted. [While Eva sketched] = dependent; [Sam painted] = independent.\nCOMPOUND-COMPLEX: While Eva sketched, Sam painted, and I cut the paper. 1 dependent + 2 independent.\nNotice how adding a separate subject changes the clause count; adding while makes a clause dependent.","connect":"Classify structure by clauses even when the topic changes to science or history. Connect Sentence Types · four structures quiz rehearsal to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"At least 90% on independent selected responses plus separately reviewed writing. Corrected responses do not replace first scores.","v":[["independent clause","A subject-verb group expressing a complete thought."],["dependent clause","A subject-verb group that cannot stand alone as a complete sentence."],["compound predicate","Two or more actions sharing a subject; this alone does not create a compound sentence."],["comma splice","Two independent clauses incorrectly joined by a comma alone."]],"w":"Write FOUR sentences about an art show: one simple, one compound, one complex, and one compound-complex. Label and count every clause. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject.","learningTools":{"url":"exam-practice.html?set=ela_v40_sentence_types","cue":"FIND → BRACKET → TEST → COUNT → CLASSIFY","task":"Four sentence structures with equal practice.","apply":"Write FOUR sentences about an art show: one simple, one compound, one complex, and one compound-complex. Label and count every clause.","delayed":"Use new sentences on a different topic after at least 24 hours."}},{"id":"ela_v41_start_here","title":"START HERE — New Sentence Types Practice","src":"Sentence-types quiz scope clarified by parent October 29; original examples. Purdue OWL clause and sentence-structure rules. No actual test questions.","teach":"START HERE — NEW SENTENCE TYPES PRACTICE\nNew examples: shelter volunteers, fashion displays and studio events.\n\nFirst identify complete thoughts. SIMPLE: 1 independent clause, 0 dependent. COMPOUND: 2 or more independent, 0 dependent. COMPLEX: 1 independent, 1 or more dependent. COMPOUND-COMPLEX: 2 or more independent, 1 or more dependent.\n\nA dependent clause has a subject and verb but cannot stand alone, often because of when, after, although, while or because. A phrase lacks its own subject-verb pair. Several actions or subjects do not automatically create several clauses.\n\nItems 1–20 are scored automatically for sentence structure, clause counts and matching a writing plan. Items 21–24 are your own writing: save a draft, reread it and make your own changes before saving your final response. Your writing is not underlined, autocorrected or given spelling, grammar or punctuation corrections by this activity. Free writing is saved for review, not automatically assigned a Tennessee essay score.","model":"Different model: While Zoe read, Mia drew, and I arranged the books.\n[While Zoe read] depends on another thought. [Mia drew] and [I arranged the books] can stand alone. Count: 2 independent + 1 dependent = compound-complex.\nBefore answering, find subjects and verbs and test each whole clause.","connect":"Use the same clause-count method with any topic. Connect START HERE — New Sentence Types Practice to claim–evidence–reasoning. State the idea, cite a specific detail, and explain how the detail supports theme, central idea, inference, structure, or perspective. This same reasoning structure is required in Science explanations and Social Studies source analysis.","success":"Objective answers are scored automatically; original writing needs review. Preserve first attempts.","v":[["independent clause","A subject-verb group expressing a complete thought."],["dependent clause","A subject-verb group that cannot stand alone as a complete sentence."],["compound predicate","Two or more actions sharing a subject; this alone does not create a compound sentence."],["comma splice","Two independent clauses incorrectly joined by a comma alone."]],"w":"Open the START HERE activity for four separate writing tasks. Reread and revise each yourself, then bracket and label the clauses. No extra cross-subject response is required for this quiz rehearsal. CONNECTION: Make a claim about this lesson, support it with two details, and explain how each detail supports the claim. Then name where the same reasoning structure appears in another subject.","learningTools":{"url":"exam-practice.html?set=ela_v41_start_here","cue":"BRACKET → COUNT → CLASSIFY","task":"20 automatic checks and 4 self-edited writing responses.","apply":"Write and label one sentence of each structure.","delayed":"Try fresh examples later without reopening the model."}}]},"MATH":{"name":"Math","lessons":[{"id":"math_v28_big8_source","title":"MATH BINDER • The Big Eight From Memory","src":"Riley Math 6th in 6th binder • The Big Eight • Integers, Rational Numbers and Coordinate Graphing Unit Notes","teach":"Memorize the eight fraction–decimal–percent families exactly. A bar or ellipsis marks repetition. Use relationships: 2/3 is twice 1/3; 1/5 is twice 1/10; 1/8 is half of 1/4. For denominators 9, 99, and 999, the numerator block repeats.","model":"1/8=0.125=12.5%; 3/8=3×0.125=0.375=37.5%. For 17/99, 17 repeats: 0.171717….","connect":"Automatic equivalents support comparison and percent reasoning. Connect The Big Eight From Memory to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","success":"I can write all 24 forms with no notes twice on different days.","v":[["equivalent forms","different representations with the same value"],["repeating decimal","decimal with a repeating digit or block"],["exact","equal without rounding"]],"w":"NO-PEEK: Rebuild the eight-row table. Then convert 2/9, 7/9, 17/99, 7/99, and 127/999. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#MATH","cue":"Predict → model → explain → rebuild without looking.","task":"Use the number line, a blank coordinate grid, or Big Eight recall cards. Read the scale before plotting.","apply":"Compare -12/25 and -0.51, plot (-2.5,1.5), reflect it across each axis, then explain one distance as an absolute difference.","delayed":"Tomorrow, rebuild all 24 Big Eight forms on blank paper and solve fresh fraction-coordinate examples."}},{"id":"math_v29_coordinate_song","title":"MATH MEMORY LAB • Four-Quadrant Coordinate Song","src":"NUMBEROCK Coordinate Plane Song • Riley Math 6th-in-6th binder","link":"https://numberock.com/lessons/coordinate-plane-4-quadrants/","teach":"Use the song to lock in x first, y second and the four quadrant sign patterns. Turn it off before solving. The song does not replace Riley’s binder work with fractional and decimal coordinates, points on axes, reflections, absolute value, or horizontal and vertical distance.","model":"For (−2.5, 1.5), move left 2.5 for x and up 1.5 for y, so the point is in Quadrant II. Across the y-axis it becomes (2.5,1.5).","connect":"The signs used to compare rational numbers also determine direction and quadrant on the coordinate plane. Connect Four-Quadrant Coordinate Song to geography and data displays. Signs, ordered pairs, distance, and scale describe position and change; always name the reference point, axes, direction, and units before interpreting a map or model.","success":"Without replaying, I can plot rational coordinates, name location, reflect a point, and find axis-aligned distance.","v":[["ordered pair","(x,y), horizontal then vertical"],["quadrant","one of four regions made by the axes"],["reflection","a flip across a mirror axis"],["axis","a reference number line on the coordinate plane"]],"w":"CONNECTION: With the video off, plot four rational-coordinate points in four quadrants, reflect two across named axes, and find one horizontal and one vertical distance. Explain how signs and absolute value connect. CONNECTION: Create a coordinate or number-line situation connected to a map. Solve it, name the reference point and units, and explain what the numbers mean in the real situation.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#MATH","cue":"Predict → model → explain → rebuild without looking.","task":"Use the number line, a blank coordinate grid, or Big Eight recall cards. Read the scale before plotting.","apply":"Compare -12/25 and -0.51, plot (-2.5,1.5), reflect it across each axis, then explain one distance as an absolute difference.","delayed":"Tomorrow, rebuild all 24 Big Eight forms on blank paper and solve fresh fraction-coordinate examples."}},{"id":"math_v28_integer_rational","title":"MATH BINDER • Integers, Rational Numbers, Absolute Value and Order","src":"Riley Math 6th in 6th binder • The Big Eight • Integers, Rational Numbers and Coordinate Graphing Unit Notes","teach":"Integers are zero, positive whole numbers, and negative whole numbers. Deposit/increase/rise/credit/above can be positive; withdrawal/decrease/debt/loss/depth/below can be negative. Zero can mean sea level, starting point, ground level, or no change. Absolute value is distance from zero. Compare mixed rational forms by converting them to a common form and using number-line position. Number-line graphing requires bounding integers, equal intervals, and accurate placement.","model":"−12/25=−0.48. Because −0.48 is right of −0.51, −12/25>−0.51.","connect":"The sign gives direction; absolute value gives magnitude. Connect Integers, Rational Numbers, Absolute Value and Order to geography and data displays. Signs, ordered pairs, distance, and scale describe position and change; always name the reference point, axes, direction, and units before interpreting a map or model.","success":"I can define, graph, compare, and order positive and negative rational numbers.","v":[["integer","zero, a positive whole number, or a negative whole number"],["rational number","number expressible as a fraction of integers"],["absolute value","distance from zero"],["magnitude","size without direction"]],"w":"Graph {−1/5, −0.7, 2 3/5, −1.8}, then order a second mixed set and justify every conversion. CONNECTION: Create a coordinate or number-line situation connected to a map. Solve it, name the reference point and units, and explain what the numbers mean in the real situation.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#MATH","cue":"Predict → model → explain → rebuild without looking.","task":"Use the number line, a blank coordinate grid, or Big Eight recall cards. Read the scale before plotting.","apply":"Compare -12/25 and -0.51, plot (-2.5,1.5), reflect it across each axis, then explain one distance as an absolute difference.","delayed":"Tomorrow, rebuild all 24 Big Eight forms on blank paper and solve fresh fraction-coordinate examples."}},{"id":"math_v28_coordinate_graph","title":"MATH BINDER • Coordinate Plane, Quadrants, Axes and Reflections","src":"Riley Math 6th in 6th binder • The Big Eight • Integers, Rational Numbers and Coordinate Graphing Unit Notes","teach":"Read (x,y): horizontal x first, vertical y second. Quadrants are I (+,+), II (−,+), III (−,−), and IV (+,−). Points on axes are not in quadrants: if x=0, the point is on the y-axis; if y=0, it is on the x-axis. Across the x-axis, y changes sign. Across the y-axis, x changes sign. To identify reflection axis, find which coordinate changed sign. Fractional and decimal coordinates require reading the grid scale before plotting.","model":"P(−1 3/4,2 3/4) across the x-axis becomes P′(−1 3/4,−2 3/4).","connect":"Use signs, scale, and the original/image relationship as evidence. Connect Coordinate Plane, Quadrants, Axes and Reflections to geography and data displays. Signs, ordered pairs, distance, and scale describe position and change; always name the reference point, axes, direction, and units before interpreting a map or model.","success":"I can graph rational-coordinate points, reflect them, and identify the mirror axis.","v":[["origin","(0,0)"],["quadrant","one of four regions formed by the axes"],["reflection","flip across a mirror line"],["image","result after a transformation"]],"w":"Graph six rational-coordinate points, including both axes. Reflect three and explain each sign change. CONNECTION: Create a coordinate or number-line situation connected to a map. Solve it, name the reference point and units, and explain what the numbers mean in the real situation.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#MATH","cue":"Predict → model → explain → rebuild without looking.","task":"Use the number line, a blank coordinate grid, or Big Eight recall cards. Read the scale before plotting.","apply":"Compare -12/25 and -0.51, plot (-2.5,1.5), reflect it across each axis, then explain one distance as an absolute difference.","delayed":"Tomorrow, rebuild all 24 Big Eight forms on blank paper and solve fresh fraction-coordinate examples."}},{"id":"math_v28_distance","title":"MATH BINDER • Horizontal and Vertical Distance","src":"Riley Math 6th in 6th binder • The Big Eight • Integers, Rational Numbers and Coordinate Graphing Unit Notes","teach":"Horizontal points share y; find the absolute difference of x. Vertical points share x; find the absolute difference of y. When no grid is shown, ignore the matching coordinate and find the separation between the other two values. Distance is never negative.","model":"D(−1,−1/2) and C(−1,−2) share x. Distance=|−1/2−(−2)|=1 1/2 units.","connect":"Coordinate distance is absolute value applied to position. Connect Horizontal and Vertical Distance to geography and data displays. Signs, ordered pairs, distance, and scale describe position and change; always name the reference point, axes, direction, and units before interpreting a map or model.","success":"I can calculate exact horizontal and vertical distances with fractions and decimals.","v":[["horizontal distance","absolute difference of x-values when y matches"],["vertical distance","absolute difference of y-values when x matches"]],"w":"Create and solve two horizontal and two vertical distance problems using fractions or decimals. CONNECTION: Create a coordinate or number-line situation connected to a map. Solve it, name the reference point and units, and explain what the numbers mean in the real situation.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#MATH","cue":"Predict → model → explain → rebuild without looking.","task":"Use the number line, a blank coordinate grid, or Big Eight recall cards. Read the scale before plotting.","apply":"Compare -12/25 and -0.51, plot (-2.5,1.5), reflect it across each axis, then explain one distance as an absolute difference.","delayed":"Tomorrow, rebuild all 24 Big Eight forms on blank paper and solve fresh fraction-coordinate examples."}},{"id":"math_v23_big8_memory","title":"DO FIRST • MATH 6TH IN 6TH • Memorize the Big 8","src":"Parent-applied highlights on the 10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • teacher-required Big 8 floating pop quizzes • Math 6th in 6th","teach":"These eight fact families must be recalled automatically—not merely recognized: 1/2 = 0.5 = 50%; 1/3 = 0.333… = 33⅓%; 1/4 = 0.25 = 25%; 1/5 = 0.2 = 20%; 1/8 = 0.125 = 12.5%; 1/9 = 0.111… = 11⅑%; 1/10 = 0.1 = 10%; and 2/3 = 0.666… = 66⅔%. Learn them in anchors: halves and tenths first; quarters and fifths next; eighths from halving 1/4; thirds and ninths as repeating families. Use cover–recall–check: study one row, cover it, say and write both missing forms, uncover and correct, then mix the direction. A correct choice seen on screen is recognition; mastery requires no-peek production from memory.","model":"MODEL: Start with 1/4 = 0.25 = 25%. Cover the decimal and percent. Say “one fourth, twenty-five hundredths, twenty-five percent,” then write 0.25 and 25%. Reverse it: when shown 25%, produce 1/4 and 0.25. Finally connect 1/8 by halving 1/4: half of 0.25 is 0.125, and half of 25% is 12.5%.","connect":"Connect the fact families to comparison, percent problems, coordinate values, and Science proportions so recall supports application. Connect MATH 6TH IN 6TH • Memorize the Big 8 to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","success":"Mastery means 24/24 forms produced with no notes or choices on two checks separated by at least one day.","v":[["fact family","equivalent fraction, decimal, and percent forms"],["automatic recall","producing a fact accurately without choices or notes"],["terminating decimal","a decimal that ends"],["repeating decimal","a decimal with a digit or block repeating forever"],["cover–recall–check","cover the answer, produce it, reveal it, and repair any error"]],"w":"NO-PEEK PRODUCTION: On blank paper, write all eight fractions in a shuffled order. Add each decimal and percent without notes or choices. Check only after all 24 entries are complete. Circle every miss, restudy that family, and reproduce the entire table correctly. CONNECTION: Use 1/8, 1/4, and 1/2 to describe three portions of one Science population. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#MATH","cue":"Predict → model → explain → rebuild without looking.","task":"Use the number line, a blank coordinate grid, or Big Eight recall cards. Read the scale before plotting.","apply":"Compare -12/25 and -0.51, plot (-2.5,1.5), reflect it across each axis, then explain one distance as an absolute difference.","delayed":"Tomorrow, rebuild all 24 Big Eight forms on blank paper and solve fresh fraction-coordinate examples."}},{"id":"math_v24_reveal_module4_vocab","title":"CURRENT SOURCE CHECK • REVEAL MATH MODULE 4 VOCABULARY","src":"Williamson County Schools adopted curriculum: Tennessee Reveal Math, Course 1, Module 4 “Integers, Rational Numbers, and the Coordinate Plane” family letter • 10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • Math 6th in 6th","teach":"Learn the exact Module 4 vocabulary before using it. An integer is zero, a positive whole number, or a negative whole number. A positive integer is greater than zero; a negative integer is less than zero. Opposites are the same distance from zero on different sides. Absolute value is distance from zero, so it is never negative. A rational number can be written as a fraction of integers with a nonzero denominator. On the coordinate plane, the x-axis is horizontal, the y-axis is vertical, and the axes divide the plane into four quadrants. A reflection flips a figure across a line while preserving size, shape, and distance from that line.","model":"MODEL: −4 is a negative integer and rational number because −4=−4/1. Its opposite is 4, a positive integer, and |−4|=4. Point (−4,3) lies in Quadrant II. Reflecting it across the y-axis changes only the x-coordinate, giving (4,3).","connect":"Connect exact vocabulary to the Big 8, signed-number comparison, coordinate graphing, and Science quantities. Connect REVEAL MATH MODULE 4 VOCABULARY to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","success":"I can define, distinguish, model, and apply all eight adopted Module 4 terms without relying on answer choices.","v":[["absolute value","the distance a number is from zero"],["integer","zero, a positive whole number, or a negative whole number"],["negative integer","an integer less than zero"],["opposite","a number the same distance from zero on the other side"],["positive integer","an integer greater than zero"],["quadrants","the four regions formed by the x-axis and y-axis"],["rational number","a number expressible as a fraction of integers with a nonzero denominator"],["reflection","a flip across a line that preserves size, shape, and distance"]],"w":"Create a four-column vocabulary map: term, exact meaning, example, and nonexample. Then graph one point in each quadrant, reflect two points, and explain every sign change. CONNECTION: Use positive and negative integers to represent increase and decrease in a Science population. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"math_v22_compare","title":"CURRENT OCT. 17 • MATH 6TH IN 6TH • Compare Across Number Forms","src":"10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • Math 6th in 6th path • number comparison fluency • mental computation fluency • Aimsweb Fall 2026 Student Score Snapshots (Reading 10/5/2026; Math 10/5/2026)","teach":"Riley stays in the regular Math 6th in 6th course. Her strongest benchmark area is Concepts & Applications; the improvement target is efficient comparison across whole numbers, fractions, decimals, and signed numbers. Use one common representation: convert fractions to decimals when easy, use benchmarks such as 0, 1/2, and 1, or place values on a number line. For negatives, farther left means less. Compare values—not the number of digits or the size of a denominator.","model":"MODEL: Compare −0.6 and −5/8. Convert −5/8 to −0.625. Because −0.625 lies left of −0.6, −5/8\u003c−0.6. The digits 625 being “larger” does not make a negative value greater.","connect":"Connect the Big 8 facts and current integer unit to benchmark number-comparison fluency. Connect MATH 6TH IN 6TH • Compare Across Number Forms to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","success":"I can compare mixed rational-number forms accurately and explain the representation I chose.","v":[["magnitude","size or distance from zero"],["benchmark fraction","a familiar value such as 0, 1/2, or 1 used for comparison"],["equivalent forms","fraction, decimal, or percent forms with the same value"],["triad","three values compared to identify relative size"]],"w":"Create two comparison triads: one using fractions/decimals/percents and one using negative rational numbers. Order each triad and justify the order in two different ways. CONNECTION: Link one value to a population proportion. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"math_v22_mental","title":"CURRENT OCT. 17 • MATH 6TH IN 6TH • Mental Computation & Order of Operations","src":"10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • Math 6th in 6th path • number comparison fluency • mental computation fluency • Aimsweb Fall 2026 Student Score Snapshots (Reading 10/5/2026; Math 10/5/2026)","teach":"Mental computation should be accurate before it is fast. Break numbers apart, use compatible numbers, and check signs and operation order. For fractions and decimals, estimate first. Use grouping symbols, then multiplication/division from left to right, then addition/subtraction from left to right. A negative sign is part of the number. Daily retrieval will mix whole numbers, fractions, decimals, and current integer ideas so Riley must choose a method.","model":"MODEL: 18×25 = (20−2)×25 = 500−50 = 450. For 6−2(−3), multiply first: 2(−3)=−6, then 6−(−6)=12. A reasonableness check confirms that subtracting a negative increases the result.","connect":"Connect benchmark fluency to the current integer, rational-number, percent, and coordinate work. Connect MATH 6TH IN 6TH • Mental Computation & Order of Operations to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","success":"I can choose an efficient mental strategy, follow operation order, and verify the result.","v":[["compatible numbers","numbers that are easy to compute together"],["distributive property","breaking a product across addition or subtraction"],["order of operations","agreed sequence for evaluating expressions"],["estimate","a nearby value used to judge reasonableness"]],"w":"Solve six mixed mental-computation items. For each, name the strategy, show one key step, and estimate to verify reasonableness. CONNECTION: Explain how computation supports—not replaces—conceptual problem solving. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"math_v21_integers","title":"CURRENT OCT. 9 • MATH 1 • Integer Vocabulary, Opposites & Order","src":"10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • Schoology vocabulary folder referenced but not publicly accessible • Tennessee Grade 6 NS.C.5–8 support","teach":"An integer is a positive whole number, its negative opposite, or zero. Opposites are the same distance from zero on different sides, so their sum is zero. Absolute value is distance from zero and is never negative. On a number line, numbers farther right are greater. A negative number with larger absolute value is farther left and therefore smaller. Use contexts such as elevation, temperature, debt/credit, and change, but do not confuse the sign with the size of the quantity.","model":"MODEL: Compare −8 and −3. On the number line, −8 is left of −3, so −8\u003c−3. Their absolute values are 8 and 3, but absolute value measures distance, not which signed number is greater. Opposite of −8 is 8; |−8|=8.","connect":"Connect signed values to Science population change: a negative change can represent decrease, while magnitude describes how large the change is. Connect MATH 1 • Integer Vocabulary, Opposites & Order to geography and data displays. Signs, ordered pairs, distance, and scale describe position and change; always name the reference point, axes, direction, and units before interpreting a map or model.","success":"I can define, locate, compare, and interpret integers in unfamiliar contexts.","v":[["integer","a positive whole number, negative whole number, or zero"],["opposites","numbers the same distance from zero on opposite sides"],["absolute value","distance from zero"],["inequality","a comparison using \u003c, >, ≤, or ≥"]],"w":"Create a number-line story using at least four integers. Order them, identify two opposites, calculate absolute values, and explain one common mistake. CONNECTION: Create a coordinate or number-line situation connected to a map. Solve it, name the reference point and units, and explain what the numbers mean in the real situation."},{"id":"math_v21_coordinates","title":"CURRENT OCT. 9 • MATH 2 • Rational Numbers & Coordinate Graphing","src":"10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • Schoology vocabulary folder referenced but not publicly accessible • Tennessee Grade 6 NS.C.5–8 support","teach":"A rational number can be written as a fraction of integers and located on a number line. The coordinate plane has four quadrants. An ordered pair is (x,y): move horizontally for x, then vertically for y. Quadrant I (+,+), II (−,+), III (−,−), IV (+,−). Reflection across the x-axis changes the sign of y; reflection across the y-axis changes the sign of x; reflection across both axes changes both signs. Points sharing x lie vertically; points sharing y lie horizontally. Their distance is the absolute difference of the changing coordinates.","model":"MODEL: Point A=(−3,4) is in Quadrant II. Across the x-axis it becomes (−3,−4); across the y-axis it becomes (3,4). Point B=(−3,−2) shares x with A, so the vertical distance is |4−(−2)|=6.","connect":"Connect coordinate graphs to map reasoning: both use reference axes, direction, scale, and exact location evidence. Connect MATH 2 • Rational Numbers & Coordinate Graphing to geography and data displays. Signs, ordered pairs, distance, and scale describe position and change; always name the reference point, axes, direction, and units before interpreting a map or model.","success":"I can graph, reflect, compare, and find axis-aligned distances with rational coordinates.","v":[["coordinate plane","two perpendicular number lines used to locate points"],["ordered pair","coordinates written (x,y)"],["quadrant","one of four regions formed by the axes"],["reflection","a flip across a line that preserves distance"]],"w":"Draw and label four points, one in each quadrant. Reflect two points across an axis and calculate a horizontal or vertical distance; explain every sign change. CONNECTION: Create a coordinate or number-line situation connected to a map. Solve it, name the reference point and units, and explain what the numbers mean in the real situation."},{"id":"math_v21_big8","title":"CURRENT OCT. 9 • MATH 3 • Big 8 Fraction–Decimal–Percent Fluency","src":"10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • Schoology vocabulary folder referenced but not publicly accessible • Tennessee Grade 6 NS.C.5–8 support","teach":"The newsletter names eight conversions for pop quizzes: 1/2=.5=50%; 1/3=.333…=33⅓%; 1/4=.25=25%; 1/5=.2=20%; 1/8=.125=12.5%; 1/9=.111…=11⅑%; 1/10=.1=10%; and 2/3=.666…=66⅔%. A bar over a digit means it repeats forever. For denominators 9, 99, or 999, the numerator block repeats: 4/9=.444…, 37/99=.373737…, 125/999=.125125125…. Always distinguish an exact repeating value from a rounded decimal.","model":"MODEL: 2/3=0.666… exactly. As a percent, multiply by 100: 66.666…%=66⅔% exactly. If a problem requests the nearest tenth of a percent, report 66.7% and label it rounded. For 37/99, the repeating block is 37.","connect":"Connect equivalent forms to percent data in Science: the fraction, decimal, and percent must describe the same population proportion. Connect MATH 3 • Big 8 Fraction–Decimal–Percent Fluency to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","success":"I can produce all eight equivalents quickly and explain repeating-decimal notation accurately.","v":[["terminating decimal","a decimal that ends"],["repeating decimal","a decimal with a digit or block that repeats forever"],["equivalent forms","different representations of the same value"],["rounded value","an approximation at a named place"]],"w":"Build the Big 8 table from memory. Then explain the denominator-9 pattern using 4/9, 37/99, and 125/999, labeling exact and rounded forms. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"math_v21_rational_apply","title":"CURRENT OCT. 9 • MATH 4 • Rational-Number Applications & Error Analysis","src":"10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • Schoology vocabulary folder referenced but not publicly accessible • Tennessee Grade 6 NS.C.5–8 support","teach":"Choose the representation that makes the problem easiest. Use a number line for order and change, coordinates for position, absolute value for distance, and fraction-decimal-percent equivalence for comparison. Never treat absolute value as “make it positive” without explaining distance. Never swap x and y. When comparing a fraction and decimal, convert both to the same form or locate both on a number line. Preserve the first incorrect step during error analysis, name the rule violated, then solve a parallel example.","model":"MODEL: Order −0.6, −1/2, 0.25. Convert −1/2 to −0.5. Then −0.6\u003c−0.5\u003c0.25. A student who says −0.6 is greater because 6>5 ignored signed position. The repair is to use the number line.","connect":"Connect rational-number choices to ELA task verbs: “compare,” “locate,” “reflect,” and “convert” demand different operations. Connect MATH 4 • Rational-Number Applications & Error Analysis to geography and data displays. Signs, ordered pairs, distance, and scale describe position and change; always name the reference point, axes, direction, and units before interpreting a map or model.","success":"I can choose and defend the correct representation instead of applying one rule mechanically.","v":[["rational number","a number expressible as a ratio of integers"],["magnitude","size or distance regardless of direction"],["origin","the point (0,0)"],["error analysis","identifying the first wrong step, repairing the rule, and reapplying it"]],"w":"Solve a mixed set containing ordering, coordinate distance, reflection, and fraction-decimal-percent conversion. For one deliberate error, identify and repair the first wrong step. CONNECTION: Create a coordinate or number-line situation connected to a map. Solve it, name the reference point and units, and explain what the numbers mean in the real situation."},{"id":"math_v19_percent_meaning","title":"CURRENT SOURCE PATH 1 • Percent Means Per 100","src":"Teacher uploads and 10/2 newsletter • Percentage Unit Test scope • Tennessee Grade 6 ratio/percent expectations","teach":"Percent means per 100. Represent a percent with a 100-grid, ratio p:100, fraction p/100, or decimal p÷100. Percentages below 1% describe less than one per hundred; percentages above 100% describe more than one whole. Always name the reference whole.","model":"0.5%=0.5/100=0.005. On a 100-grid it is half of one small square. 125%=125/100=1.25, or one whole and one fourth. The symbols change, but the quantity does not.","connect":"Connect percent representations to Science resource data: the base and units control the meaning. Connect Percent Means Per 100 to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","success":"I can represent and interpret percents below 1, between 1 and 100, and above 100.","v":[["percent","a rate per 100"],["reference whole","the amount defined as 100%"],["equivalent","having the same value"],["benchmark percent","a useful reference such as 1%, 10%, 25%, 50%, or 100%"]],"w":"Create representations for 0.25%, 18%, 100%, and 140%. For each, name a possible reference whole and explain the quantity. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"math_v19_three_unknowns","title":"CURRENT SOURCE PATH 2 • Find the Part, Percent, or Whole","src":"Teacher uploads and 10/2 newsletter • Percentage Unit Test scope","teach":"Use part = percent rate × whole, with the percent written as a decimal. If the part is unknown, multiply. If the percent is unknown, divide part by whole and convert to percent. If the whole is unknown, divide part by the decimal rate. Estimate with benchmarks before calculating.","model":"Part: 35% of 80=0.35×80=28. Percent: 18 is what percent of 60? 18÷60=0.30=30%. Whole: 24 is 15% of what? 24÷0.15=160. Each answer passes a benchmark check.","connect":"Connect unknown selection to ELA prompt decoding: identify what is given and what the question asks before operating. Connect Find the Part, Percent, or Whole to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","success":"I can identify the unknown, select the correct equation, calculate, and check reasonableness.","v":[["part","the compared amount"],["whole","the reference amount equal to 100%"],["rate","the percent written as a rate or decimal"],["estimate","a reasonable approximate value used to check work"]],"w":"Write and solve one fresh problem of each type: unknown part, unknown percent, and unknown whole. Include an estimate and label the reference whole. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"math_v19_applications","title":"CURRENT SOURCE PATH 3 • Percent Applications & Error Analysis","src":"Teacher uploads and 10/2 newsletter • Percentage Unit Test review/notes/reflection","teach":"For discount, tax, tip, markup, commission, and percent change, first identify the original or reference amount. A discount is subtracted; tax and tip are added. Percent change uses change÷original×100%. Show units and distinguish the amount of change from the final amount.","model":"A $48 item is 25% off: discount=0.25×48=$12; sale price=$36. If 8% tax applies to the sale price: tax=.08×36=$2.88; final=$38.88. Each stage has a different reference amount.","connect":"Connect error analysis to Science test corrections: preserve the first wrong step, repair the rule, and solve a parallel item. Connect Percent Applications & Error Analysis to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","success":"I can solve contextual percent problems and explain exactly why a tempting wrong method fails.","v":[["discount","an amount subtracted from original price"],["tax","an amount added based on a taxable subtotal"],["percent change","change divided by original amount, expressed as a percent"],["final amount","the starting amount after additions or subtractions"]],"w":"Solve a multi-step purchase with discount, tax, and tip or fee. Label each base, each change amount, and the final amount; then explain one likely error. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"math_v19_next_unit","title":"CURRENT SOURCE BRIDGE 4 • Integers, Rational Numbers & Coordinates","src":"Teacher uploads and 10/2 newsletter • next-unit newsletter sequence","teach":"Integers include positive whole numbers, their opposites, and zero. Absolute value is distance from zero, never direction. Rational numbers can be written a/b with b≠0 and can be ordered on a number line. Ordered pairs use x then y. Reflections across the y-axis change x; across the x-axis change y. Horizontal or vertical distance is the absolute difference of the changing coordinates.","model":"−7 and 7 are opposites and both have absolute value 7. −3/4=−0.75 lies left of −0.6. Point (−3,4) is Quadrant II; reflecting across y gives (3,4), a horizontal distance of 6.","connect":"Connect coordinates to map relationships while keeping coordinate-plane notation distinct from geographic latitude/longitude. Connect Integers, Rational Numbers & Coordinates to geography and data displays. Signs, ordered pairs, distance, and scale describe position and change; always name the reference point, axes, direction, and units before interpreting a map or model.","success":"I can use vocabulary and models from the next unit before formal practice begins.","v":[["integer","a positive or negative whole number or zero"],["opposite","a number the same distance from zero on the other side"],["absolute value","distance from zero"],["ordered pair","coordinates written (x,y)"]],"w":"Build one connected example using a negative percent change, rational-number comparison, a coordinate point, its reflection, and a distance calculation. CONNECTION: Create a coordinate or number-line situation connected to a map. Solve it, name the reference point and units, and explain what the numbers mean in the real situation."},{"id":"math_oct2_percent_connections","title":"CURRENT • Percent Master Map","src":"10/2 teacher newsletter + school percentage unit + 9/25 objectives","teach":"All percent problems connect three roles: whole = 100%, part = compared amount, percent = rate per 100. Do not choose an operation from a keyword alone. Identify the roles, predict whether the answer should be smaller or larger, choose a model or equation, solve, and label the result. Percents may be below 1% or above 100%.","model":"15 is 25% of what number? Part = 15, rate = 0.25, whole is unknown. Equation: 15 = 0.25w, so w = 60. Check with a model: 25% is one fourth; four groups of 15 make 60.","connect":"Connect percent reasoning to Science resource tables: a percentage is meaningful only when the whole, category, unit, and comparison are clear. Connect Percent Master Map to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","success":"I can identify the three roles, choose a model/equation without a label telling me the type, solve, and check reasonableness.","v":[["base/whole","the complete amount corresponding to 100%"],["rate","a comparison per a chosen base; percent is per 100"],["benchmark","a familiar value such as 10%, 25%, 50%, or 100% used to estimate"]],"w":"Create and solve one real-world problem for each form: find the part, find the percent, and find the whole. Label the three roles in every problem and include a reasonableness check. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"math_oct2_integers","title":"NEXT UNIT • Integers, Opposites & Absolute Value","src":"10/2 teacher newsletter + Grade 6 rational-number scope","teach":"Integers are whole numbers, their opposites, and zero. Opposites are the same distance from zero on different sides. Absolute value is distance from zero, so it is never negative. A negative sign tells direction or location; absolute value tells distance. Context determines what zero and direction mean.","model":"A submarine at −120 m and a drone at +120 m are opposites relative to sea level. Both have absolute value 120 because each is 120 units from zero. Their meanings differ: below versus above sea level.","connect":"Connect signed numbers to Science temperature and to geography/elevation: the sign communicates direction relative to a reference point. Connect Integers, Opposites & Absolute Value to geography and data displays. Signs, ordered pairs, distance, and scale describe position and change; always name the reference point, axes, direction, and units before interpreting a map or model.","success":"I can interpret, locate, compare, and explain integers, opposites, and absolute value in context.","v":[["integer","a whole number, its opposite, or zero"],["opposites","numbers the same distance from zero on different sides"],["absolute value","a number's distance from zero"]],"w":"Give two different real-world meanings for −12 and +12. Name the reference point and explain what absolute value means in each context. CONNECTION: Create a coordinate or number-line situation connected to a map. Solve it, name the reference point and units, and explain what the numbers mean in the real situation."},{"id":"math_oct2_rational_order","title":"NEXT UNIT • Rational Numbers on a Number Line","src":"10/2 teacher newsletter + Grade 6 rational-number scope","teach":"A rational number can be written as a fraction of integers with a nonzero denominator. Integers, fractions, and terminating or repeating decimals are rational. To compare, express values in compatible forms or place them on one number line. Farther right is greater. With negatives, the number closer to zero is greater.","model":"Order −1.5, −5/4, 0.2. Convert −5/4 to −1.25. On the number line: −1.5 is left of −1.25, and 0.2 is right of both. Order: −1.5 \u003c −1.25 \u003c 0.2.","connect":"Connect fraction–decimal–percent equivalence from the percent unit to rational-number comparison. Connect Rational Numbers on a Number Line to geography and data displays. Signs, ordered pairs, distance, and scale describe position and change; always name the reference point, axes, direction, and units before interpreting a map or model.","success":"I can convert when useful, plot, order, and justify rational-number comparisons using location.","v":[["rational number","a number expressible as a ratio a/b where b is not zero"],["coordinate","a number that gives a point's location"],["inequality","a comparison using symbols such as \u003c or >"]],"w":"Draw or describe a number line containing −1.25, −3/4, 0, 0.4, and 125%. Order them and explain two of the comparisons. CONNECTION: Create a coordinate or number-line situation connected to a map. Solve it, name the reference point and units, and explain what the numbers mean in the real situation."},{"id":"math_oct2_coordinate_plane","title":"NEXT UNIT • Coordinate Plane, Reflection & Distance","src":"10/2 teacher newsletter + Grade 6 coordinate-plane scope","teach":"An ordered pair (x, y) gives horizontal movement first, then vertical. Quadrants follow the sign patterns I (+,+), II (−,+), III (−,−), IV (+,−). Reflections across the y-axis change the sign of x; reflections across the x-axis change the sign of y. For points sharing an x- or y-coordinate, distance is the absolute difference of the other coordinates.","model":"P(−3, 4) is in Quadrant II. Reflect across the y-axis: (3, 4), because only x changes sign. Distance between (−3, 4) and (3, 4) is |3 − (−3)| = 6 units.","connect":"Connect coordinates to Social Studies maps: both use reference lines, direction, and ordered location, but map latitude/longitude is not the same notation as a classroom coordinate plane. Connect Coordinate Plane, Reflection & Distance to geography and data displays. Signs, ordered pairs, distance, and scale describe position and change; always name the reference point, axes, direction, and units before interpreting a map or model.","success":"I can plot and name quadrants, reflect points across axes, and find horizontal or vertical distance.","v":[["ordered pair","two coordinates written (x, y)"],["quadrant","one of four regions formed by the axes"],["reflection","a mirror image across a line"]],"w":"Create four points, one in each quadrant. Reflect one across the x-axis and one across the y-axis. Include one horizontal or vertical pair and calculate its distance. CONNECTION: Create a coordinate or number-line situation connected to a map. Solve it, name the reference point and units, and explain what the numbers mean in the real situation."},{"id":"m_wk0928_percent_meaning","title":"THIS WEEK • Percent Meaning, \u003c1% and >100%","src":"9/25 teacher newsletter + TN Grade 6 ratio/percent alignment","teach":"Percent means 'per 100.' A percent can be less than 1% or greater than 100%. Connect fractions, decimals, and percents by reasoning about hundredths and equivalent ratios. Do not rely on a shortcut without identifying what the number represents.","connect":"Connect Percent Meaning, \u003c1% and >100% to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","v":[["percent","a rate per 100"],["equivalent","having the same value"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"m_wk0928_percent_unknowns","title":"THIS WEEK • Find Part, Percent, or Whole","src":"9/25 teacher newsletter + TN Grade 6 percent reasoning + Math-Aids topic cross-check","teach":"Always identify the WHOLE, the PART, and the PERCENT before calculating. Part = percent × whole when percent is written as a decimal. To find the whole, divide the part by the decimal percent. To find the percent, compare part to whole and convert the ratio to a percent. Use a tape diagram, ratio table, or double number line when useful.","connect":"Connect Find Part, Percent, or Whole to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","v":[["whole","the total or base quantity"],["part","the portion being compared with the whole"],["percent","the rate per 100 connecting part to whole"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"m_ratio","title":"Ratios & Equivalent Ratios","src":"Teacher/newsletter + TN Grade 6 support","teach":"A ratio is a multiplicative comparison between two quantities. Equivalent ratios preserve the same relationship by scaling both quantities by the same factor.","connect":"Connect Ratios & Equivalent Ratios to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","v":[["ratio","a multiplicative comparison of two quantities"],["equivalent ratios","ratios that represent the same multiplicative relationship"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"m_unit","title":"Ratio Tables & Unit Rates","src":"TN 6.RP.A.3 support","teach":"A ratio table organizes equivalent pairs. A unit rate tells how much of one quantity corresponds to 1 unit of the other.","connect":"Connect Ratio Tables & Unit Rates to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","v":[["unit rate","a rate expressed per 1 unit"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"m_percent","title":"Percent = Per 100","src":"Teacher/newsletter + TN 6.RP.A.3c","teach":"Percent means “per hundred.” 25% = 25/100 = 0.25 = 1/4. Percent is therefore a special rate/ratio with 100 as the comparison base.","connect":"Connect Percent = Per 100 to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","v":[["percent","a rate per 100"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"m_parts","title":"PART • PERCENT • WHOLE","src":"Teacher understanding-first approach","teach":"WHOLE = the reference amount or everything. PART = the piece being compared. PERCENT = the rate per 100. Identify the unknown before calculating.","connect":"Connect PERCENT • WHOLE to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","v":[["whole","the complete reference amount"],["part","the piece compared with the whole"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"m_findpart","title":"Find the Part","src":"TN Grade 6 percent support","teach":"When percent and whole are known, find the part. Models such as a bar/tape diagram can show why the calculation works.","connect":"Connect Find the Part to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","v":[],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"m_findpercent","title":"Find the Percent","src":"TN Grade 6 percent support","teach":"When part and whole are known, compare part to whole and express the same relationship per 100.","connect":"Connect Find the Percent to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","v":[],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"m_findwhole","title":"Find the Whole","src":"TN Grade 6 percent support","teach":"When part and percent are known, rebuild 100%. Use a ratio table, tape diagram, double number line, or equation to explain the relationship.","connect":"Connect Find the Whole to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","v":[],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"m_models","title":"Tape Diagrams • Tables • Double Number Lines","src":"TN Grade 6 support","teach":"A tape/bar diagram shows parts of a whole. A ratio table shows equivalent pairs. A double number line aligns two related scales. An equation expresses the same relationship symbolically. Models explain why, not just what.","connect":"Connect Tables • Double Number Lines to geography and data displays. Signs, ordered pairs, distance, and scale describe position and change; always name the reference point, axes, direction, and units before interpreting a map or model.","v":[["double number line","two aligned number lines showing corresponding values"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Create a coordinate or number-line situation connected to a map. Solve it, name the reference point and units, and explain what the numbers mean in the real situation."},{"id":"m_error","title":"Error Analysis","src":"TN instructional focus","teach":"Error analysis means explaining why a method or answer is wrong, then correcting the reasoning with precise math language.","connect":"Connect Error Analysis to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","v":[["error analysis","finding, explaining, and correcting a reasoning mistake"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"m_transfer","title":"Context & Transfer","src":"Teacher + TN application expectations","teach":"Read for structure, not keywords. Identify whole, part, percent, and the unknown. Then choose a representation and solve.","connect":"Connect Context & Transfer to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","v":[],"w":"Choose one percent problem you solved. Explain how you identified the whole, part, percent, and unknown before calculating. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"m_blend","title":"Blend Math • Proportional Relationships","src":"Current newsletter + TN Grade 7 proportional relationships support","teach":"A proportional relationship has a constant multiplicative relationship. In a table, equivalent ratios share a constant unit rate. On a graph, a proportional relationship is a straight line through the origin. In an equation, y = kx, where k is the constant of proportionality.","connect":"Connect Proportional Relationships to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","v":[["constant of proportionality","the constant unit rate k in y = kx"],["origin","the point (0,0) on a coordinate plane"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"m_ixl_ratio_challenge","title":"IXL-Level Ratio Challenge","src":"Current IXL Grade 6 Tennessee alignment • 6.RP.A.1–3 + TCAP skill plan","teach":"IXL does not stop at naming a ratio. Be ready to read a word problem, build an equivalent ratio, find a unit rate, complete a table, compare two relationships, and connect a table to a graph. The hard part is deciding what relationship the numbers represent before calculating.","connect":"Connect IXL-Level Ratio Challenge to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","v":[["unit rate","a rate for 1 unit of the second quantity"],["equivalent ratios","ratios that describe the same multiplicative relationship"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"m_ixl_percent_models","title":"IXL-Level Percent Models & Conversions","src":"IXL Tennessee Grade 6 • 6.RP.A.3.c • U.1/U.3/U.4/U.7/U.8","teach":"Percent means per 100, but IXL can hide that idea inside grids, strip models, fractions, decimals, and word problems. Move flexibly among all four representations: picture, fraction, decimal, and percent. Do not rely on one shortcut.","connect":"Connect IXL-Level Percent Models & Conversions to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","v":[["benchmark percent","a familiar percent such as 10%, 25%, 50%, or 75% used to reason about another amount"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"m_ixl_percent_mixed","title":"IXL-Level Mixed Percent Problems","src":"IXL Tennessee Grade 6 • 6.RP.A.3.c • V.2–V.11 + TCAP percent skills","teach":"IXL mixes three structures without announcing which one it is: find the part, find the percent, or find the whole. Label PART, PERCENT, WHOLE first. Then solve and check whether the answer is reasonable.","connect":"Connect IXL-Level Mixed Percent Problems to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","v":[],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"m_ixl_checkpoint","title":"IXL/TCAP Ratio & Percent Checkpoint","src":"IXL 2026 Tennessee TCAP Grade 6 skill plan • ratio reasoning + percent word problems","teach":"This is a mixed, no-hint checkpoint. IXL and TCAP-style work can switch representations and problem types from one item to the next. Read the context, identify the relationship, choose a model or equation, and check the result.","connect":"Connect IXL/TCAP Ratio & Percent Checkpoint to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","v":[],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"m_ixl_8n4_exact","title":"IXL 8N4 • Percents of Numbers & Money Amounts","src":"Exact assigned IXL skill 8N4 • Percents of numbers and money amounts","teach":"8N4 asks Riley to find a given percent of a number or money amount. The numbers may be friendly or less obvious. Keep the meaning visible: p% of a quantity means p/100 times that quantity. Use benchmark percents when efficient—10%, 25%, 50%, 75%—and decompose harder percents. For money, report a sensible dollar amount.","connect":"Connect Percents of Numbers & Money Amounts to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","v":[["percent of a quantity","a part found by multiplying the quantity by a rate per 100"],["benchmark percent","a familiar percent such as 10%, 25%, 50%, or 75% that can help calculate another percent"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"m_mixed_practice_2","title":"★ RANDOM MIXED PRACTICE 2 • MATH • 15 New Questions","src":"Current classroom ratios/percent • assigned IXL 8N4 • TN Grade 6 ratio/percent reasoning","teach":"NEW MIXED RETRIEVAL. Questions appear in a different random order each time. Do not assume every problem asks for the same unknown. Decide whether you need the PART, PERCENT, WHOLE, ratio, or unit rate before calculating.","connect":"Connect MATH • 15 New Questions to Science resource decisions. Identify the part, whole, rate, units, and time period before comparing output, cost, efficiency, or change. Then explain what the calculation supports and what it cannot prove by itself.","v":[],"w":"Explain how you decide whether a percent problem is asking for the part, the percent, or the whole. Include one example. CONNECTION: Use this Math idea in an energy-resource comparison. Show the setup and units, identify the reference whole, interpret the result, and name one decision factor the number does not capture."},{"id":"extra_v35_math_a","title":"Big 8, integers and coordinates · practice A","src":"October 23 teacher newsletter scope; original extra practice, form A. Assigned private ELA guide and math practice test remain unverified.","teach":"Convert fractions by division; multiply a decimal by 100 to express a percent. Use exact repeating values. Order negatives by their positions on a number line. Reflect across x by changing y; reflect across y by changing x. Horizontal or vertical distance is an absolute coordinate difference.","model":"7/8 = 7 × 0.125 = 0.875 = 87.5%. For A=(-5,3), x-axis reflection is (-5,-3); y-axis reflection is (5,3). From (-5,3) to (4,3), distance is |4-(-5)|=9.","connect":"Use ELA evidence and reasoning to explain the geographic or mathematical relationship. Connect Big 8, integers and coordinates · practice A to geography and data displays. Signs, ordered pairs, distance, and scale describe position and change; always name the reference point, axes, direction, and units before interpreting a map or model.","success":"Complete first responses, repair missed skills, and return later for the other form. Writing needs review before readiness can be determined.","v":[],"w":"CONNECTION: Complete the written items in focused practice. Connect the prior idea to the new context; say what stays the same, what changes and which source detail or calculation supports your conclusion. CONNECTION: Create a coordinate or number-line situation connected to a map. Solve it, name the reference point and units, and explain what the numbers mean in the real situation.","learningTools":{"cue":"Work one item at a time.","task":"Open form A including written work and visual questions.","url":"exam-practice.html?set=extra_v35_math_a","apply":"Save your explanation before viewing the model response.","delayed":"Use the other form in a later session without notes."}},{"id":"extra_v35_math_b","title":"Big 8, integers and coordinates · practice B","src":"October 23 teacher newsletter scope; original extra practice, form B. Assigned private ELA guide and math practice test remain unverified.","teach":"Convert fractions by division; multiply a decimal by 100 to express a percent. Use exact repeating values. Order negatives by their positions on a number line. Reflect across x by changing y; reflect across y by changing x. Horizontal or vertical distance is an absolute coordinate difference.","model":"7/8 = 7 × 0.125 = 0.875 = 87.5%. For A=(-5,3), x-axis reflection is (-5,-3); y-axis reflection is (5,3). From (-5,3) to (4,3), distance is |4-(-5)|=9.","connect":"Use ELA evidence and reasoning to explain the geographic or mathematical relationship. Connect Big 8, integers and coordinates · practice B to geography and data displays. Signs, ordered pairs, distance, and scale describe position and change; always name the reference point, axes, direction, and units before interpreting a map or model.","success":"Complete first responses, repair missed skills, and return later for the other form. Writing needs review before readiness can be determined.","v":[],"w":"CONNECTION: Complete the written items in focused practice. Connect the prior idea to the new context; say what stays the same, what changes and which source detail or calculation supports your conclusion. CONNECTION: Create a coordinate or number-line situation connected to a map. Solve it, name the reference point and units, and explain what the numbers mean in the real situation.","learningTools":{"cue":"Work one item at a time.","task":"Open form B including written work and visual questions.","url":"exam-practice.html?set=extra_v35_math_b","apply":"Save your explanation before viewing the model response.","delayed":"Use the other form in a later session without notes."}},{"id":"math_v37_scale","title":"Graph distance: read the scale before counting","src":"Schoology Week 11: October 26-30; released submitted practice review, inspected October 28, 2026. Original parallel examples; no live test items or student records published.","teach":"A grid space is not automatically one unit. Read two neighboring numbered marks. Divide their difference by the number of equal spaces between them. Then read both coordinates. For points with the same x, subtract the y-coordinates and take the absolute value. For points with the same y, do the same with x. Count intervals between points, not endpoints. Check the result using coordinates. A distance has no negative sign.","model":"The y-axis shows 0 and 1 with two equal spaces between them: each space is 0.5 unit. A=(2,-1.5), B=(2,2). Seven spaces separate the points: 7 × 0.5=3.5 units. Coordinate check: |2-(-1.5)|=3.5. Counting seven spaces and writing 7 units would double the distance.","connect":"Graph scale connects to map scale and science measurements; units per interval must be read before interpreting position. Connect Graph distance: read the scale before counting to geography and data displays. Signs, ordered pairs, distance, and scale describe position and change; always name the reference point, axes, direction, and units before interpreting a map or model.","success":"Save first answers, explain every miss, complete fresh repair, then use the new-format delayed task after at least 24 hours. Writing needs review; practice completion is not mastery.","v":[["scale","The value represented by a graph interval."],["interval","The space between consecutive marks."],["distance","Nonnegative separation measured in the graph units."]],"w":"CONNECTION: Draw a graph with 0.25 unit per space and two horizontal points 1.75 units apart. Label coordinates and show both a space-count and coordinate-difference solution. Compare graph scale to an Egypt map scale: what stays the same, what changes, and what calculation proves the relationship? CONNECTION: Create a coordinate or number-line situation connected to a map. Solve it, name the reference point and units, and explain what the numbers mean in the real situation.","learningTools":{"cue":"Read the quantities and labels before choosing a method.","task":"Open matching practice, diagrams, printable student copy and written transfer. These repeat the same lesson checks; do not count a repeated answer as fresh evidence.","apply":"CONNECTION: Draw a graph with 0.25 unit per space and two horizontal points 1.75 units apart. Label coordinates and show both a space-count and coordinate-difference solution. Compare graph scale to an Egypt map scale: what stays the same, what changes, and what calculation proves the relationship?","delayed":"After at least 24 hours, make a new example with different quantities or organisms. Explain it without notes; have an adult review it.","url":"exam-practice.html?set=math_v37_scale"}},{"id":"math_v37_signs","title":"Opposites, absolute value and rational-number order","src":"Schoology Week 11: October 26-30; released submitted practice review, inspected October 28, 2026. Original parallel examples; no live test items or student records published.","teach":"An opposite reverses a number’s sign; the opposite of 0 is 0. Absolute value is distance from zero, so it is never negative. A minus sign OUTSIDE absolute-value bars acts after the distance is found: -|-9|=-9. Opposite is not reciprocal: the opposite of -12 is 12, while its reciprocal is -1/12. To order mixed fractions and decimals, convert to comparable values or common denominators, then place them on a number line. Farther left means less. Opposite changes cancel only when they have equal size and opposite signs.","model":"Compare -|-28|, |24|, -|-35| and |-2|: evaluate to -28, 24, -35 and 2. The greatest is 24. For -6 3/4, -6.04, 6 1/25 and -6 2/3, comparable values are -6.75,-6.04,6.04,-6.666… . Least to greatest: -6 3/4, -6 2/3, -6.04, 6 1/25.","connect":"Use signed numbers to interpret temperatures and changes in resources; evidence must show both magnitude and direction. Connect Opposites, absolute value and rational-number order to geography and data displays. Signs, ordered pairs, distance, and scale describe position and change; always name the reference point, axes, direction, and units before interpreting a map or model.","success":"Save first answers, explain every miss, complete fresh repair, then use the new-format delayed task after at least 24 hours. Writing needs review; practice completion is not mastery.","v":[["opposite","A number the same distance from zero in the other direction."],["absolute value","Distance from zero, never negative."],["reciprocal","A number that multiplies with a nonzero number to give 1."]],"w":"CONNECTION: Invent a pair of equal opposite bank changes and a pair that do not cancel. Show the sums. Compare bank direction to temperatures on a number line: name the prior/new ideas, what stays the same, what changes and evidence. Then explain why -|a| and |a| differ when a is nonzero. CONNECTION: Create a coordinate or number-line situation connected to a map. Solve it, name the reference point and units, and explain what the numbers mean in the real situation.","learningTools":{"cue":"Read the quantities and labels before choosing a method.","task":"Open matching practice, diagrams, printable student copy and written transfer. These repeat the same lesson checks; do not count a repeated answer as fresh evidence.","apply":"CONNECTION: Invent a pair of equal opposite bank changes and a pair that do not cancel. Show the sums. Compare bank direction to temperatures on a number line: name the prior/new ideas, what stays the same, what changes and evidence. Then explain why -|a| and |a| differ when a is nonzero.","delayed":"After at least 24 hours, make a new example with different quantities or organisms. Explain it without notes; have an adult review it.","url":"exam-practice.html?set=math_v37_signs"}},{"id":"math_v37_reflect","title":"Reflect without swapping coordinates","src":"Schoology Week 11: October 26-30; released submitted practice review, inspected October 28, 2026. Original parallel examples; no live test items or student records published.","teach":"Coordinates are ordered (x,y): horizontal first, vertical second. Across the x-axis, x stays the same and y changes sign. Across the y-axis, y stays the same and x changes sign. Across both axes, both signs change; their order does not swap. A point on the reflecting axis stays fixed. Verify with equal perpendicular distances from the axis. For distance along a horizontal or vertical line, use the changing coordinate only.","model":"P=(-4,2.5) becomes (-4,-2.5) across x, and (4,2.5) across y. Across x then y it becomes (4,-2.5). From (-4,2.5) to (-4,-6), the distance is |2.5-(-6)|=8.5 units, not a count including both endpoints.","connect":"Reflection rules can interpret field-study maps; the coordinate convention must be stated. Connect Reflect without swapping coordinates to geography and data displays. Signs, ordered pairs, distance, and scale describe position and change; always name the reference point, axes, direction, and units before interpreting a map or model.","success":"Save first answers, explain every miss, complete fresh repair, then use the new-format delayed task after at least 24 hours. Writing needs review; practice completion is not mastery.","v":[["reflection","A mirror transformation across a line."],["invariant","A quantity or feature that stays unchanged."],["ordered pair","An x-coordinate followed by a y-coordinate."]],"w":"CONNECTION: Plot (-2.5,3), reflect it across each axis separately and across both. Explain what stays/changes and prove equal distance from each reflecting axis. Connect this to an east/north field-study map; use different coordinates for a fresh example. CONNECTION: Create a coordinate or number-line situation connected to a map. Solve it, name the reference point and units, and explain what the numbers mean in the real situation.","learningTools":{"cue":"Read the quantities and labels before choosing a method.","task":"Open matching practice, diagrams, printable student copy and written transfer. These repeat the same lesson checks; do not count a repeated answer as fresh evidence.","apply":"CONNECTION: Plot (-2.5,3), reflect it across each axis separately and across both. Explain what stays/changes and prove equal distance from each reflecting axis. Connect this to an east/north field-study map; use different coordinates for a fresh example.","delayed":"After at least 24 hours, make a new example with different quantities or organisms. Explain it without notes; have an adult review it.","url":"exam-practice.html?set=math_v37_reflect"}}]},"SCI":{"name":"Science","lessons":[{"id":"science_v22_vocab_core","title":"CURRENT OCT. 17 • SCIENCE 1 • Teacher Ecology Vocabulary A–E","src":"Teacher-uploaded StudyMate Ecology vocabulary (10/17/2026) • teacher-uploaded textbook Section 1 “Everything Is Connected,” pp. 46–51 (10/17/2026) • 10/9/2026 newsletter","teach":"Learn the teacher’s exact vocabulary before the closed-note check. Abiotic means nonliving; biotic means living or once living. An adaptation is a trait that improves survival or reproduction in an environment. A biome is a large region defined mainly by climate and characteristic life; the biosphere is every part of Earth where life exists. A carnivore eats animals. Carrying capacity is the largest population an environment can support under the conditions at that time. A community contains all interacting populations in an area. An ecosystem is that community plus its abiotic environment. Consumers obtain energy by eating organisms; decomposers break down dead matter and recycle materials. An energy pyramid shows that usable energy decreases at higher feeding levels.","model":"MODEL: In a marsh, cordgrass, crabs, and birds are biotic; salt water, sunlight, and temperature are abiotic. All cordgrass populations, crabs, birds, and microbes form the community. Add the water, sediment, light, and temperature and the system becomes an ecosystem.","connect":"Connect exact definitions to the teacher textbook’s “Everything Is Connected” section. Connect SCIENCE 1 • Teacher Ecology Vocabulary A–E as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","success":"I can define, distinguish, and apply every A–E teacher term.","v":[["abiotic","the nonliving part of an environment"],["adaptation","a trait that helps an organism survive and reproduce"],["biome","a large region with a characteristic climate and community"],["biosphere","all parts of Earth where life exists"],["biotic","the living or once-living part of an environment"],["carnivore","an organism that eats animals"],["carrying capacity","largest population an environment can support under current conditions"],["community","all interacting populations in an area"],["consumer","an organism that obtains energy by eating other organisms"],["decomposer","an organism that breaks down dead material"],["ecosystem","a community and its abiotic environment"],["energy pyramid","a model showing less usable energy at higher feeding levels"]],"w":"Build one marsh example that correctly uses all twelve vocabulary terms. CONNECTION: Compare “community” and “ecosystem” the way ELA compares two related terms—state what stays the same and what changes. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#SCI","cue":"Build the relationship → change one factor → trace the consequence.","task":"Sort organism cards, build food-web arrows, then explain a disturbance. Use +/+, +/0, and +/- to retrieve symbiosis outcomes.","apply":"Return to the teacher’s alligator, gar, and cordgrass examples. Name the changed factor, trace a justified effect, and identify a model limitation.","delayed":"Tomorrow, draw a different food web and use six teacher vocabulary terms to explain a new limiting factor."}},{"id":"science_v22_organization","title":"CURRENT OCT. 17 • SCIENCE 2 • Five Levels of Environmental Organization","src":"Teacher-uploaded StudyMate Ecology vocabulary (10/17/2026) • teacher-uploaded textbook Section 1 “Everything Is Connected,” pp. 46–51 (10/17/2026) • 10/9/2026 newsletter","teach":"The textbook orders environmental organization from smallest to largest: organism → population → community → ecosystem → biosphere. One organism is one living individual. A population is individuals of the same species living in an area. A community is all populations that live and interact there. An ecosystem is a community plus its abiotic environment. The biosphere contains all ecosystems and includes life in the atmosphere, water, and land. The levels are nested: each larger level contains the smaller level plus something new.","model":"MODEL: One seaside sparrow = organism. All seaside sparrows in the marsh = population. Sparrows, cordgrass, crabs, snails, algae, and microbes = community. Add salt water, mud, light, oxygen, and temperature = ecosystem. Connect that marsh ecosystem with all other ecosystems = biosphere.","connect":"Connect the textbook diagram to classification, scale, and precise academic language. Connect SCIENCE 2 • Five Levels of Environmental Organization as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","success":"I can reconstruct the five levels, create my own example, and correct category errors.","v":[["organism","one individual living thing"],["population","same species living in one area"],["community","all populations living and interacting in an area"],["ecosystem","community plus abiotic environment"],["biosphere","all ecosystems; the part of Earth where life exists"],["ecology","study of interactions among organisms and their environment"]],"w":"Choose a nearby ecosystem and construct all five levels with specific examples. Then explain exactly what is added at each step. CONNECTION: Relate the nested hierarchy to ordered pairs or place-value organization without claiming they are identical. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#SCI","cue":"Build the relationship → change one factor → trace the consequence.","task":"Sort organism cards, build food-web arrows, then explain a disturbance. Use +/+, +/0, and +/- to retrieve symbiosis outcomes.","apply":"Return to the teacher’s alligator, gar, and cordgrass examples. Name the changed factor, trace a justified effect, and identify a model limitation.","delayed":"Tomorrow, draw a different food web and use six teacher vocabulary terms to explain a new limiting factor."}},{"id":"science_v24_textbook_review","title":"CURRENT SOURCE CHECK • EVERYTHING IS CONNECTED • EXACT SECTION REVIEW","src":"Teacher-uploaded textbook Section 1 “Everything Is Connected,” pp. 46–51 • teacher StudyMate Ecology vocabulary • teacher ecology schedule","teach":"Ecology studies connections among organisms and between organisms and their environment—the web of life. Use the five nested levels precisely: one organism; one-species population; interacting populations in a community; the community plus abiotic factors in an ecosystem; and all ecosystems in the biosphere. Ecosystem study boundaries are practical, not sealed: organisms, water, matter, and energy can cross them. In the salt marsh, cordgrass is a producer and living shelter, while salinity, sunlight, water, and temperature are abiotic. If cordgrass dies, food, shelter, populations, and connected feeding relationships can change. The textbook states that the biosphere extends about 9 km above sea level and 19 km below it, so its approximate vertical thickness is 9+19=28 km.","model":"MODEL: Claim—loss of cordgrass can reduce marsh populations. Evidence—cordgrass supplies food or shelter and begins energy pathways. Reasoning—when that resource disappears, it becomes a limiting factor; consumers lose energy or habitat, and effects can spread through the food web. For the textbook math check, add distances on opposite sides of sea level: 9 km above + 19 km below = 28 km total thickness.","connect":"Connect the teacher textbook’s marsh system to vocabulary, food webs, limiting factors, Math signed-distance reasoning, and ELA evidence writing. Connect EVERYTHING IS CONNECTED • EXACT SECTION REVIEW as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","success":"I can reconstruct all five levels, classify factors, explain connected effects, defend a study boundary, and solve the textbook biosphere-thickness check.","v":[["web of life","the network of relationships connecting living things and their environment"],["ecology","the study of interactions among organisms and their environment"],["biotic factor","a living or once-living part of an ecosystem"],["abiotic factor","a nonliving physical or chemical part of an ecosystem"],["ecosystem boundary","a practical study limit that can still exchange organisms, matter, and energy"]],"w":"Draw a cause-and-effect graphic organizer beginning with one changed abiotic factor and ending with at least two population effects. Then define a defensible ecosystem boundary, explain what may cross it, and show the 9+19=28 km biosphere calculation. CONNECTION: Write the causal explanation as an ELA claim-evidence-reasoning paragraph. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#SCI","cue":"Build the relationship → change one factor → trace the consequence.","task":"Sort organism cards, build food-web arrows, then explain a disturbance. Use +/+, +/0, and +/- to retrieve symbiosis outcomes.","apply":"Return to the teacher’s alligator, gar, and cordgrass examples. Name the changed factor, trace a justified effect, and identify a model limitation.","delayed":"Tomorrow, draw a different food web and use six teacher vocabulary terms to explain a new limiting factor."}},{"id":"science_v22_energy_roles","title":"CURRENT OCT. 17 • SCIENCE 3 • Food Chains, Food Webs & Feeding Roles","src":"Teacher-uploaded StudyMate Ecology vocabulary (10/17/2026) • teacher-uploaded textbook Section 1 “Everything Is Connected,” pp. 46–51 (10/17/2026) • 10/9/2026 newsletter","teach":"A producer uses sunlight directly to make food. A consumer eats other organisms. Herbivores eat plants, carnivores eat animals, and omnivores eat both. A scavenger eats dead or decaying material; a decomposer chemically breaks dead matter down and returns materials to the environment. A food chain is one linear energy path; a food web shows connected feeding relationships. In arrows used for food chains and webs, the arrow points from the food or energy source to the consumer. Primary consumers eat producers; secondary consumers eat primary consumers; tertiary consumers feed at a higher level, often on secondary consumers.","model":"MODEL: Sun → cordgrass → snail → fish → gull. Cordgrass is producer; snail is primary consumer/herbivore; fish is a secondary consumer; gull may be a tertiary consumer. A food web adds other paths, such as cordgrass → grasshopper → sparrow → hawk.","connect":"Connect energy roles to the salt-marsh organisms pictured in the teacher textbook. Connect SCIENCE 3 • Food Chains, Food Webs & Feeding Roles as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","success":"I can read arrows, classify feeding roles, and predict cautious food-web effects.","v":[["food chain","one linear path of energy and nutrients through organisms"],["food web","connected feeding relationships in an ecosystem"],["herbivore","consumer that eats plants"],["omnivore","consumer that eats plants and animals"],["producer","organism that uses sunlight to make food"],["scavenger","organism that feeds on dead or decaying organic matter"],["tertiary consumer","higher-level consumer that feeds on secondary consumers"]],"w":"Create a five-organism marsh food web, label every feeding role, and predict two effects of removing one producer. CONNECTION: Use ELA evidence-and-reasoning language to justify each prediction. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#SCI","cue":"Build the relationship → change one factor → trace the consequence.","task":"Sort organism cards, build food-web arrows, then explain a disturbance. Use +/+, +/0, and +/- to retrieve symbiosis outcomes.","apply":"Return to the teacher’s alligator, gar, and cordgrass examples. Name the changed factor, trace a justified effect, and identify a model limitation.","delayed":"Tomorrow, draw a different food web and use six teacher vocabulary terms to explain a new limiting factor."}},{"id":"science_v22_relationships","title":"CURRENT OCT. 17 • SCIENCE 4 • Predator, Prey & Symbiosis","src":"Teacher-uploaded StudyMate Ecology vocabulary (10/17/2026) • teacher-uploaded textbook Section 1 “Everything Is Connected,” pp. 46–51 (10/17/2026) • 10/9/2026 newsletter","teach":"A predator captures and consumes prey. Adaptations may help organisms act as predator or avoid becoming prey. Symbiosis means two different species live in close association. In mutualism both benefit (+/+). In commensalism one benefits and the other is not meaningfully affected (+/0). In parasitism the parasite benefits while the host is harmed (+/−). The host provides habitat or resources for another organism. Classify from evidence about effects—not from whether the relationship looks “nice.”","model":"MODEL: A tick feeds on a deer: tick benefits, deer is harmed, so this is parasitism; deer is the host. A remora rides a shark and eats scraps while the shark is not meaningfully affected: commensalism. A bee gets nectar while pollinating a flower: mutualism.","connect":"Connect interaction labels to evidence rather than memorized animal pairs. Connect SCIENCE 4 • Predator, Prey & Symbiosis as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","success":"I can distinguish predator–prey from all three symbiotic relationships and defend the classification.","v":[["predator","organism that captures and consumes another organism"],["prey","organism hunted and consumed for food"],["host","organism providing habitat or resources to another organism"],["symbiosis","close association between two different species"],["mutualism","both species benefit"],["commensalism","one benefits and the other is unaffected"],["parasitism","one benefits and the host is harmed"],["symbiotic","describing a close association between different species"]],"w":"Classify four unfamiliar relationships using +, −, and 0, name the host/predator/prey when relevant, and cite the evidence. CONNECTION: Compare the classification logic to ELA compare/contrast. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#SCI","cue":"Build the relationship → change one factor → trace the consequence.","task":"Sort organism cards, build food-web arrows, then explain a disturbance. Use +/+, +/0, and +/- to retrieve symbiosis outcomes.","apply":"Return to the teacher’s alligator, gar, and cordgrass examples. Name the changed factor, trace a justified effect, and identify a model limitation.","delayed":"Tomorrow, draw a different food web and use six teacher vocabulary terms to explain a new limiting factor."}},{"id":"science_v22_limits","title":"CURRENT OCT. 17 • SCIENCE 5 • Niche, Limiting Factors & Invasive Species","src":"Teacher-uploaded StudyMate Ecology vocabulary (10/17/2026) • teacher-uploaded textbook Section 1 “Everything Is Connected,” pp. 46–51 (10/17/2026) • 10/9/2026 newsletter","teach":"A habitat is where an organism lives; its niche is the role it performs and how it uses resources and interacts. A native species normally lives and thrives in an ecosystem. A nonnative species comes from elsewhere; it is invasive only when it causes or is likely to cause environmental, economic, or health harm. A limiting factor is a scarce resource or condition that restricts population size. Carrying capacity changes when food, water, space, shelter, disease, predators, or other conditions change. A biome is broader than one ecosystem and is characterized by climate and typical communities.","model":"MODEL: An introduced vine spreads across a Tennessee woodland, blocks light, and reduces native seedlings. Evidence supports calling it invasive because it is nonnative and harmful. Light and space become limiting factors, and the carrying capacity for some native plants may fall.","connect":"Connect population limits to Math comparison and Science food-web consequences. Connect SCIENCE 5 • Niche, Limiting Factors & Invasive Species as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","success":"I can distinguish habitat/niche and native/nonnative/invasive, then reason about population limits.","v":[["niche","a species’ role and resource use in an ecosystem"],["native species","species that normally occurs in an ecosystem"],["invasive species","nonnative species that causes or is likely to cause harm"],["limiting factor","resource or condition that restricts a population"],["habitat","place where an organism lives"],["carrying capacity","population an environment can support under current conditions"]],"w":"Analyze a new invasive-species scenario: identify habitat, niche, limiting factor, carrying-capacity effect, evidence, and one solution tradeoff. CONNECTION: Express one population as a fraction, decimal, and percent. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#SCI","cue":"Build the relationship → change one factor → trace the consequence.","task":"Sort organism cards, build food-web arrows, then explain a disturbance. Use +/+, +/0, and +/- to retrieve symbiosis outcomes.","apply":"Return to the teacher’s alligator, gar, and cordgrass examples. Name the changed factor, trace a justified effect, and identify a model limitation.","delayed":"Tomorrow, draw a different food web and use six teacher vocabulary terms to explain a new limiting factor."}},{"id":"science_v21_ecosystem_vocab","title":"CURRENT OCT. 9 • SCIENCE 1 • Environments & Populations Vocabulary","src":"10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • Tennessee Grade 6 Science 6.LS2.1–6.LS2.5 • prior STEMscopes resource remains referenced-only until accessible","teach":"An organism is one living individual. A population is all members of the same species in one area. A community includes all the different populations in an area. An ecosystem includes the community plus nonliving environmental factors. Biotic factors are living or once-living; abiotic factors are nonliving physical or chemical conditions. Habitat is where an organism lives; niche is its role and use of resources. These levels must not be swapped.","model":"MODEL: In a pond, one frog is an organism; all frogs of that species are a population; frogs, insects, plants, and microbes form the community; those organisms plus water, light, soil, oxygen, and temperature form the ecosystem.","connect":"Connect category precision to ELA: definitions supply boundaries, and examples must meet every part of the definition. Connect SCIENCE 1 • Environments & Populations Vocabulary as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","success":"I can classify unfamiliar examples at the correct ecological level.","v":[["organism","one living individual"],["population","members of the same species in one area"],["community","all populations living together in an area"],["ecosystem","living community plus nonliving environment"],["biotic factor","a living or once-living part of a system"],["abiotic factor","a nonliving physical or chemical part of a system"]],"w":"Build a nested example from organism to population to community to ecosystem. Label at least three biotic and three abiotic factors and explain one niche. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"science_v21_population_variables","title":"CURRENT OCT. 9 • SCIENCE 2 • Variables, Resources & Population Size","src":"10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • Tennessee Grade 6 Science 6.LS2.1–6.LS2.5 • prior STEMscopes resource remains referenced-only until accessible","teach":"Population size changes through births, deaths, immigration, and emigration. Environmental variables include resources such as food, water, oxygen, shelter, and space, plus temperature, precipitation, disease, predators, and disturbances. A limiting factor restricts population growth. Carrying capacity is the largest population an environment can support over time under current conditions. It is not a permanent fixed number: conditions and resources can change it. Use tables and graphs to identify patterns, then distinguish correlation from a proven cause.","model":"MODEL: A rabbit population rises while rainfall and plant growth rise, then declines during drought. The data support an association between water/food availability and rabbit population. To claim drought caused the decline, check timing, competing explanations, and additional evidence.","connect":"Connect population percent change to Math: identify the original population before calculating increase or decrease. Connect SCIENCE 2 • Variables, Resources & Population Size as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","success":"I can use data to explain how living and nonliving variables influence population size.","v":[["limiting factor","a condition or resource that restricts population growth"],["carrying capacity","population size an environment can sustain over time"],["immigration","movement into a population"],["emigration","movement out of a population"]],"w":"Analyze a fresh population graph. State the pattern, identify a plausible limiting factor, cite two data points, and explain what the graph does not prove. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"science_v21_interactions","title":"CURRENT OCT. 9 • SCIENCE 3 • Competition, Predation, Symbiosis & Food Webs","src":"10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • Tennessee Grade 6 Science 6.LS2.1–6.LS2.5 • prior STEMscopes resource remains referenced-only until accessible","teach":"Competition occurs when organisms seek the same limited resource. Predation occurs when one organism captures and eats another. Symbiosis is a close relationship: mutualism benefits both, commensalism benefits one while the other is not significantly helped or harmed, and parasitism benefits one while harming the host. Food webs model feeding relationships and energy pathways. Arrows point from the food or energy source toward the consumer. A population change can create indirect effects through the web, but predictions should be qualified because ecosystems have multiple pathways.","model":"MODEL: Grass → rabbit → hawk. If rabbits decline, hawks may have less food and grass may experience less grazing. “May” is appropriate because hawks might use other prey and other herbivores may eat grass.","connect":"Connect relationships to ELA comparison: classify each interaction by who benefits, is harmed, or is unaffected, then support the classification. Connect SCIENCE 3 • Competition, Predation, Symbiosis & Food Webs as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","success":"I can classify interactions and use a food web to make evidence-based, appropriately cautious predictions.","v":[["competition","interaction over a limited shared resource"],["predation","one organism capturing and consuming another"],["mutualism","both species benefit"],["commensalism","one benefits and the other is largely unaffected"],["parasitism","one benefits while the host is harmed"],["food web","model of connected feeding relationships"]],"w":"Create a food web with at least five organisms. Predict two consequences of one population change and explain one uncertainty. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"science_v21_health_invasive","title":"CURRENT OCT. 9 • SCIENCE 4 • Ecosystem Health, Biodiversity & Invasive Species","src":"10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • Tennessee Grade 6 Science 6.LS2.1–6.LS2.5 • prior STEMscopes resource remains referenced-only until accessible","teach":"Ecosystem health is evaluated through patterns in biotic and abiotic indicators such as biodiversity, population balance, dissolved oxygen, water quality, soil condition, temperature, and habitat stability. Biodiversity includes variation in living organisms and can support resilience. An invasive species is nonnative and causes or is likely to cause harm; “nonnative” alone is not enough. Tennessee Grade 6 science requires analyzing evidence about a specific invasive species and designing a solution that reduces harm while considering tradeoffs. A benchmark may sample vocabulary, data, models, interactions, and CER reasoning.","model":"MODEL: Data show an introduced plant spreading while native plant diversity and insect abundance decline. Claim—it may be harming the native community. Evidence—paired trends across repeated samples. Reasoning—the invader may take light, space, or nutrients. Solution—targeted removal plus monitoring; tradeoff—labor and risk of disturbing native habitat.","connect":"Connect solution tradeoffs to Social Studies resource decisions and Math data comparison. Connect SCIENCE 4 • Ecosystem Health, Biodiversity & Invasive Species as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","success":"I can evaluate ecosystem evidence and defend a solution rather than memorize definitions alone.","v":[["biodiversity","variety of living organisms in a system"],["ecosystem health","condition of a system judged from multiple biotic and abiotic indicators"],["invasive species","a nonnative species that causes or is likely to cause harm"],["mitigate","reduce the severity of harm"],["indicator","a measurable sign used to evaluate a condition"]],"w":"Use a fresh invasive-species dataset to write a CER and propose a mitigation plan. Include one benefit, one tradeoff, and one monitoring measure. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"science_v19_particles","title":"CURRENT SOURCE PATH 1 • Temperature, Thermal Energy & Particle Motion","src":"Teacher uploads and 10/2 newsletter • 49-question Heat Transfer/Energy Resources guide • SCI-01–03","teach":"Temperature measures average particle kinetic energy. Thermal energy depends on particle motion/interactions and amount of matter. Heat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference, from higher temperature to lower temperature until thermal equilibrium. A large cooler object can have more total thermal energy than a tiny hotter object, so temperature and total energy are not synonyms.","model":"Place warm metal in cool water: energy transfers from metal to water. Metal particles lose average kinetic energy; water particles gain it. Transfer slows as temperatures approach equality. At equilibrium, particles still move, but there is no net transfer caused by a temperature difference.","connect":"Connect the particle model to Math: average and total answer different questions. Connect Temperature, Thermal Energy & Particle Motion across one energy system: energy source → transfer mechanism → change in particles or temperature → larger effect. Then connect the mechanism to weather, technology, ecosystems, or resource use and support the explanation with a model, observation, or data.","success":"I can predict direction and explain the particle mechanism without mixing key terms.","v":[["kinetic energy","energy of motion"],["temperature","measure related to average particle kinetic energy"],["thermal energy","energy associated with all particles in a system"],["thermal equilibrium","equal-temperature condition with no net temperature-driven transfer"]],"w":"Explain a hot/cool contact scenario from particle motion through equilibrium. Distinguish temperature, thermal energy, and heat transfer. CONNECTION: Trace an energy pathway using this lesson. Name the source, transfer mechanism, observable effect, and one larger-system connection. Include evidence and reasoning."},{"id":"science_v19_transfer","title":"CURRENT SOURCE PATH 2 • Conduction, Convection, Radiation & Materials","src":"Teacher uploads and 10/2 newsletter • 49-question guide • SCI-04–08","teach":"Conduction transfers energy through direct contact and particle collisions, especially in solids. Convection transfers energy by bulk motion of a fluid: warmer, less-dense fluid rises while cooler, denser fluid sinks under gravity. Radiation transfers energy by electromagnetic waves and needs no matter. Conductors transfer readily; insulators slow transfer. Real systems often use more than one mechanism.","model":"Pot on stove: burner-to-pot contact is conduction; circulating water is convection; glowing burner and hot surfaces emit radiation. A metal handle conducts, while an insulating grip slows transfer. Name where each mechanism occurs.","connect":"Connect mechanisms to ELA analysis: label, cite scenario evidence, then explain effect. Connect Conduction, Convection, Radiation & Materials across one energy system: energy source → transfer mechanism → change in particles or temperature → larger effect. Then connect the mechanism to weather, technology, ecosystems, or resource use and support the explanation with a model, observation, or data.","success":"I can distinguish mechanisms and justify conductor/insulator choices in mixed systems.","v":[["conduction","transfer by contact and particle interactions"],["convection","transfer by moving liquid or gas"],["radiation","transfer by electromagnetic waves"],["insulator","material that slows thermal transfer"]],"w":"Analyze one home or kitchen system. Locate all present transfer mechanisms, justify each label, and propose a material change. CONNECTION: Trace an energy pathway using this lesson. Name the source, transfer mechanism, observable effect, and one larger-system connection. Include evidence and reasoning."},{"id":"science_v19_resources","title":"CURRENT SOURCE PATH 3 • Resource Acquisition & Energy Conversion","src":"Teacher uploads and 10/2 newsletter • 49-question guide • resource poster • SCI-09–10","teach":"Renewable describes replenishment on a human timescale; nonrenewable describes finite formation/use balance. It does not mean impact-free or useless. Trace each resource from acquisition to conversion: coal mining and combustion; natural-gas drilling/fracking and combustion; biomass growth/collection and combustion or processing; geothermal drilling to underground heat; hydropower moving water; wind moving air; solar photovoltaic conversion of radiant energy. Turbine-generator chains apply to many systems, but typical solar PV converts directly.","model":"Wind: moving air→blade/turbine motion→generator→electrical energy. Coal: chemical energy→combustion thermal energy→steam motion→turbine→generator. Solar PV: radiant energy→electrical energy, no steam turbine required.","connect":"Connect the chains to narrative and historical chronology: every arrow must state a cause or transformation. Connect Resource Acquisition & Energy Conversion as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","success":"I can classify, acquire, and trace each teacher-assigned energy resource accurately.","v":[["renewable","replenished on a relevant human timescale"],["nonrenewable","used faster than natural replacement"],["acquisition","how a resource is obtained"],["conversion","change from one energy form to another"]],"w":"Create complete acquisition-and-conversion chains for all seven assigned resources and mark which use combustion, turbines, generators, or direct PV conversion. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"science_v19_tradeoffs","title":"CURRENT SOURCE PATH 4 • Tradeoffs, Climate & Engineering Defense","src":"Teacher uploads and 10/2 newsletter • resource poster • SCI-11–14 • 2025–26 TN three-dimensional science practices","teach":"Resource decisions weigh availability, reliability, cost, infrastructure, land/water/habitat/community effects, emissions, waste, and timescale. Fossil-fuel combustion adds carbon dioxide; greenhouse gases absorb and re-emit infrared energy, changing Earth's energy balance. Deforestation can reduce carbon uptake/storage. Engineering uses criteria and constraints, evidence, models, and fair comparisons; every design has tradeoffs.","model":"A sunny remote school may use solar, but evening demand creates a storage or backup constraint. A complete defense traces energy, quantifies relevant output, names land/material/cost impacts, compares an alternative, and explains why the recommendation fits this place—not everywhere.","connect":"Connect the defense to ELA claim-evidence-reasoning and Math fair percent comparisons. Connect Tradeoffs, Climate & Engineering Defense as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","success":"I can explain mechanisms and defend a solution under unfamiliar conditions.","v":[["criterion","a standard used to judge solutions"],["constraint","a limit a solution must satisfy"],["tradeoff","a benefit paired with a cost or limitation"],["greenhouse gas","gas that affects atmospheric infrared energy transfer"]],"w":"Defend a resource plan for a named community and an improved solar-cooker design. Use criteria, constraints, conversion chains, quantitative evidence, and tradeoffs. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"s_vocab_rescue_heat","title":"DO FIRST • SCIENCE VOCAB RESCUE 1 • Heat & Particle Words","src":"Teacher 49-question Heat Transfer + Energy Resources guide • classroom Heat & Heat Transfer notes","teach":"LEARN THESE WORDS BEFORE TESTING. Use the chain MOTION → TEMPERATURE → TRANSFER → EQUAL. KINETIC ENERGY means energy of motion. TEMPERATURE measures the AVERAGE kinetic energy of particles. THERMAL ENERGY is energy associated with all the particles in a system, so amount of matter matters too. HEAT or HEAT TRANSFER is thermal energy moving because two places have different temperatures: HOT → COOL → EQUAL. THERMAL EQUILIBRIUM means equal temperature, so there is no net transfer caused by a temperature difference. Celsius and Kelvin are temperature scales—not kinds of energy.","connect":"Connect SCIENCE VOCAB RESCUE 1 • Heat & Particle Words across one energy system: energy source → transfer mechanism → change in particles or temperature → larger effect. Then connect the mechanism to weather, technology, ecosystems, or resource use and support the explanation with a model, observation, or data.","v":[["kinetic energy","energy associated with motion"],["temperature","a measure of the average kinetic energy of particles"],["thermal energy","energy associated with particle motion and interactions in a system"],["heat transfer","thermal energy moving because of a temperature difference"],["thermal equilibrium","equal-temperature condition with no net temperature-driven transfer"],["matter","anything that has mass and takes up space"],["Celsius","a temperature scale written °C"],["Kelvin","a scientific temperature scale written K"]],"w":"Teach the chain MOTION → TEMPERATURE → TRANSFER → EQUAL in your own words. Use kinetic energy, temperature, heat transfer, and thermal equilibrium correctly. CONNECTION: Trace an energy pathway using this lesson. Name the source, transfer mechanism, observable effect, and one larger-system connection. Include evidence and reasoning."},{"id":"s_vocab_rescue_transfer","title":"DO SECOND • SCIENCE VOCAB RESCUE 2 • Transfer & Material Words","src":"Teacher 49-question guide • classroom notes Topics 3–7 + Sum It Up","teach":"Use three proof clues: CONTACT → CONDUCTION. CIRCULATING LIQUID OR GAS → CONVECTION. RAYS/WAVES ACROSS SPACE → RADIATION. A CONDUCTOR carries thermal energy well; an INSULATOR interrupts or slows it. A FLUID is a liquid or gas. In a CONVECTION CURRENT, warmer fluid is usually less dense and rises while cooler fluid is more dense and sinks. RADIATION travels by ELECTROMAGNETIC WAVES and can cross empty space. Teacher examples include visible light, infrared, and ultraviolet.","connect":"Connect SCIENCE VOCAB RESCUE 2 • Transfer & Material Words across one energy system: energy source → transfer mechanism → change in particles or temperature → larger effect. Then connect the mechanism to weather, technology, ecosystems, or resource use and support the explanation with a model, observation, or data.","v":[["conduction","thermal-energy transfer through direct particle contact/interactions"],["convection","thermal-energy transfer by movement of a liquid or gas"],["radiation","energy transfer by electromagnetic waves"],["conductor","material that transfers thermal energy efficiently"],["insulator","material that slows thermal-energy transfer"],["fluid","a liquid or gas"],["density","how much mass is packed into a given volume"],["convection current","circulation caused as warmer and cooler fluid move"],["electromagnetic wave","a wave that can carry energy through space without matter"],["infrared","electromagnetic radiation strongly associated with thermal transfer"]],"w":"Create one original example each for conduction, convection, and radiation. For each, name the proof clue. CONNECTION: Trace an energy pathway using this lesson. Name the source, transfer mechanism, observable effect, and one larger-system connection. Include evidence and reasoning."},{"id":"s_vocab_rescue_resources","title":"DO THIRD • SCIENCE VOCAB RESCUE 3 • Energy Resource Names","src":"Teacher Energy Resources chart + 49-question answer key","teach":"RENEWABLE means naturally replenished on a human time scale; NONRENEWABLE means used much faster than nature replaces it. Sort with the memory line: SUN, WIND, WATER, EARTH HEAT, GROWING THINGS renew. Ancient buried fuels do not. SOLAR uses sunlight, WIND uses moving air, HYDROPOWER uses moving water, GEOTHERMAL uses heat inside Earth, and BIOMASS uses recently living material. COAL and NATURAL GAS are nonrenewable fossil fuels. Know both the name and how each source is obtained or used.","connect":"Connect SCIENCE VOCAB RESCUE 3 • Energy Resource Names as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","v":[["renewable resource","resource replenished naturally on a human time scale"],["nonrenewable resource","resource used faster than natural processes replace it"],["solar energy","energy from sunlight captured for heat or electricity"],["wind energy","moving air turns turbine blades"],["hydropower","moving or falling water turns turbines"],["geothermal energy","energy from heat inside Earth"],["biomass","plant, animal, or other recently living material used for energy"],["coal","nonrenewable fossil fuel mined and burned"],["natural gas","nonrenewable fossil fuel obtained by drilling, including fracking"]],"w":"Sort solar, wind, hydropower, geothermal, biomass, coal, and natural gas into renewable/nonrenewable, then explain how each produces usable energy. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"s_vocab_rescue_systems","title":"DO FOURTH • SCIENCE VOCAB RESCUE 4 • Machines, Tradeoffs & Climate Words","src":"Teacher Energy Resources chart + 49-question answer key • exact advantages/disadvantages and climate connection","teach":"A TURBINE is a bladed machine turned by moving water, air, or steam. A GENERATOR converts that mechanical motion into electrical energy. A TRADEOFF means gaining a benefit while accepting a cost or limitation. RELIABILITY asks whether a source can provide energy when needed. An ECOSYSTEM is living things interacting with their environment. Burning FOSSIL FUELS releases CARBON DIOXIDE (CO₂). A GREENHOUSE GAS absorbs and traps thermal energy near Earth's surface. DEFORESTATION removes forests, so fewer trees absorb CO₂. Renewable does NOT mean zero impact: dams, turbines, land use, cost, and intermittency still matter.","connect":"Connect SCIENCE VOCAB RESCUE 4 • Machines, Tradeoffs & Climate Words as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","v":[["turbine","bladed machine turned by moving water, air, or steam"],["generator","device that converts mechanical motion into electrical energy"],["tradeoff","a benefit gained while accepting a cost or limitation"],["reliability","ability to provide energy when it is needed"],["ecosystem","living things interacting with each other and their environment"],["fossil fuel","coal, oil, or natural gas formed from ancient organic matter"],["carbon dioxide (CO₂)","greenhouse gas released in large amounts by fossil-fuel combustion"],["greenhouse gas","gas that absorbs and traps thermal energy in the atmosphere"],["deforestation","large-scale removal of forests"],["intermittent","not continuously available; output changes with conditions"]],"w":"Compare two energy resources using reliability, environmental effects, cost, and availability. State at least one benefit and one limitation of each. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"science_oct2_corrections","title":"CURRENT • Science Test-Correction Lab","src":"10/2 teacher newsletter + teacher Heat Transfer and Energy Resources assessment scope","teach":"A correction must show why the original thinking failed and why the repaired answer works. For each missed test item: copy the concept (not the entire protected test), record the original choice or reasoning, classify the error—vocabulary, mechanism, direction, resource classification, conversion chain, evidence, or tradeoff—then explain the correct idea in a new scenario. Copying an answer is not a correction.","model":"Original claim: 'The Sun warms Earth by convection.' Error type: mechanism. Repair: convection needs moving matter, but space between Sun and Earth has no circulating fluid. Electromagnetic radiation can cross space. Transfer check: warmth felt from a fire across a gap is also radiation.","connect":"Connect test corrections to ELA evidence/reasoning and Math error analysis: identify the exact first wrong step and justify the repair. Connect Science Test-Correction Lab to ELA evidence/reasoning and Math error analysis: preserve the first response, identify the exact wrong idea or step, replace it with the correct scientific mechanism, and prove the repair on a new parallel situation.","success":"I can name my original error, explain the correct science, and solve a fresh parallel scenario without notes.","v":[["mechanism","the process that explains how or why something occurs"],["misconception","an idea that seems plausible but conflicts with evidence or the scientific model"],["parallel item","a new problem testing the same concept in a different context"]],"w":"For one missed Science test idea, write: original thinking; error type; correct concept; why the correct concept works; and a new scenario with your answer. Do not copy the teacher's wording. CONNECTION: Choose one misconception from this lesson. State the original thinking, identify why it fails, explain the correct mechanism with evidence, and solve a fresh parallel example."},{"id":"science_oct2_resource_systems","title":"CURRENT • Natural Resources: From Source to Electricity","src":"10/2 teacher newsletter + teacher 49-question Energy Resources guide + STEMscopes/STEMscopedia-linked support","teach":"A resource poster must explain more than renewable/nonrenewable. Trace acquisition, conversion, reliability, advantages, disadvantages, and effects. Coal is mined; natural gas is drilled/fracked; biomass is grown/collected; geothermal accesses underground heat; hydropower uses moving/stored water; wind uses moving air; solar photovoltaic cells convert radiant energy directly. Many systems turn a turbine that drives a generator, but photovoltaic panels do not.","model":"Hydropower chain: stored or flowing water → moving water turns turbine → turbine shaft drives generator → electrical energy. Advantage: controllable generation where water is available. Costs: dam construction, changed river flow/habitat, community/land impacts, and drought dependence.","connect":"Connect resource chains to ELA sequence/central idea and Math percent/data interpretation: every arrow needs a correct cause, and every comparison needs units and a reference whole. Connect Natural Resources: From Source to Electricity as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","success":"I can trace acquisition and energy conversion and evaluate at least one real advantage and one real limitation for every assigned resource.","v":[["acquisition","how a resource is obtained or accessed"],["conversion","change from one form of energy to another"],["reliability","ability to provide energy when it is needed"],["infrastructure","built systems such as panels, dams, wires, roads, pipes, and generators"]],"w":"Choose one of biomass, natural gas, geothermal, solar, hydropower, coal, or wind. Build a six-arrow explanation from acquisition through useful electricity, then add two advantages, two disadvantages, and one reliability condition. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"science_oct2_poster_defense","title":"CURRENT • Resource Poster Evidence & Defense","src":"10/2 teacher newsletter natural-resource poster assignment + teacher energy-resource guide","teach":"A strong poster makes a defensible claim for a particular place and need. Use Place–Power–Price–Planet: availability/location, amount and reliability of output, construction/operation/storage/transmission costs, and effects across extraction, construction, operation, waste, land, water, habitat, and climate. Do not argue that renewable means perfect or nonrenewable means no short-term benefit.","model":"Scenario: a sunny remote school needs dependable evening power. Claim: solar can be a major source, but panels alone do not meet the nighttime constraint. Evidence: sunlight is available by day; photovoltaic output varies. Reasoning: storage, grid connection, or backup is needed, and cost/land/material impacts must be weighed.","connect":"Connect the poster defense to ELA claim–evidence–reasoning, Social Studies geography, and Math comparisons: the best source depends on location, constraints, evidence, and tradeoffs. Connect Resource Poster Evidence & Defense as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","success":"I can defend a resource recommendation, acknowledge limits, compare alternatives fairly, and answer a challenge with evidence.","v":[["constraint","a limit a solution must satisfy"],["criterion","a standard used to judge choices"],["tradeoff","a benefit accepted with a cost or limitation"]],"w":"Poster defense: recommend an energy resource for a specific community. State the place and need, trace the energy chain, use Place–Power–Price–Planet, include at least two advantages and two disadvantages, compare one alternative, and answer one likely challenge. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"science_v34_levels_quiz","title":"CURRENT WEEK • Five Levels + Living and Nonliving Quiz","src":"Sunset Middle School 6th grade newsletter dated October 23, 2026","teach":"The Tuesday, October 27 Science quiz focuses on eight terms: ecology, biotic, abiotic, organism, population, community, ecosystem, and biosphere. The five nested levels are organism → population → community → ecosystem → biosphere. Biotic means living or once-living. Abiotic means nonliving. An ecosystem includes a community and its abiotic environment.","model":"MODEL: One frog is an organism. All frogs of the same species in one pond form a population. Frogs, fish, insects, plants, and microbes form a community. Add water, light, temperature, rocks, and oxygen to describe the ecosystem. The biosphere includes all places on Earth where life exists.","connect":"The nested-level structure resembles ELA section structure: each larger level contains and adds to earlier information. Connect Five Levels + Living and Nonliving Quiz as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","success":"I can define all eight terms, order the five levels, and classify unfamiliar examples.","v":[["ecology","study of relationships among organisms and their environments"],["biotic","living or once-living"],["abiotic","nonliving"],["organism","one individual living thing"],["population","members of one species in one area"],["community","all interacting populations in an area"],["ecosystem","a community plus abiotic factors"],["biosphere","all regions of Earth where life exists"]],"w":"Draw five nested circles and label the organization levels. Add one accurate example to each. Then sort twelve local examples as biotic or abiotic and explain two choices. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"science_v34_food_web_energy","title":"CURRENT WEEK • Matter & Energy in Food Webs Guided-Notes Support","src":"Sunset Middle School 6th grade newsletter dated October 23, 2026","teach":"The STEMscopedia guided-notes packet on Matter and Energy in Food Webs is homework due Friday, October 30. This week introduces food chains, food webs, and energy pyramids. A food chain shows one pathway of energy transfer. A food web connects many feeding pathways. Arrows point from the food or energy source toward the consumer. Producers capture energy, consumers obtain energy by eating, and decomposers break down dead material and waste. An energy pyramid shows that less usable energy is available at higher levels. The exact private packet pages are not recovered, so this lesson does not claim to reproduce its questions.","model":"MODEL: Sun → grass → grasshopper → frog → hawk. The arrow from grass to grasshopper means energy in the grass moves to the grasshopper when it is eaten. If grass declines, several consumers may be affected, but the strength of each effect depends on alternate food sources shown in the full web.","connect":"Use ELA evidence language—claim, diagram detail, reasoning—when explaining a food-web change. Connect Matter & Energy in Food Webs Guided-Notes Support as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","success":"I can read arrows, distinguish chains from webs, interpret an energy pyramid, and explain a change without claiming certainty.","v":[["food chain","one pathway of energy transfer"],["food web","connected feeding pathways"],["producer","organism that makes food using an energy source"],["consumer","organism that eats other organisms for energy"],["decomposer","organism that breaks down dead material and waste"],["energy pyramid","model showing decreasing usable energy at higher feeding levels"]],"w":"Complete the assigned guided-notes packet from the actual STEMscopedia pages. Separately, draw one five-organism food web with arrows, label each role, and explain two possible effects of removing one organism. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"science_v29_ecology_song","title":"SCIENCE MEMORY LAB • Ecology Song → System Proof","src":"Mr. Parr Ecology Song • Riley ecosystem vocabulary and textbook materials","link":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FYvQfwtI3Fo","teach":"Use the song as a cue for ecology, competition, and the three symbiotic relationships. Turn it off before retrieval. It does not replace Riley’s 33 exact vocabulary terms, organization levels, food webs, energy pyramids, carrying capacity, limiting factors, native/invasive species, or the teacher’s alligator–gar–cordgrass ecosystem examples.","model":"Mutualism: both species benefit. Commensalism: one benefits and the other is unaffected. Parasitism: the parasite benefits while the host is harmed. These are all close interactions, but their outcomes differ.","connect":"A catchy label starts recall; a system explanation must trace organisms, resources, energy, and cause/effect evidence. Connect Ecology Song → System Proof as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","success":"Without replaying, I can classify an interaction and explain its effect on the ecosystem.","v":[["ecology","study of relationships among organisms and their environment"],["competition","organisms seeking the same limited resource"],["symbiosis","close relationship between different species"],["carrying capacity","largest population an environment can support over time"]],"w":"CONNECTION: With the video off, draw a four-organism food web. Add one symbiotic relationship and one limiting factor, then explain how a change travels through the system using at least six exact vocabulary terms. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#SCI","cue":"Build the relationship → change one factor → trace the consequence.","task":"Sort organism cards, build food-web arrows, then explain a disturbance. Use +/+, +/0, and +/- to retrieve symbiosis outcomes.","apply":"Return to the teacher’s alligator, gar, and cordgrass examples. Name the changed factor, trace a justified effect, and identify a model limitation.","delayed":"Tomorrow, draw a different food web and use six teacher vocabulary terms to explain a new limiting factor."}},{"id":"s_wk0928_heat_mastery","title":"THIS WEEK • Heat Transfer Mastery","src":"Teacher Heat Transfer study-guide scope + classroom notes + current TN 6.PS3","teach":"For Friday's combined test, distinguish temperature, thermal energy, and heat transfer precisely. Temperature reflects average kinetic energy of particles. Heating is energy transfer caused by a temperature difference, from warmer toward cooler until equilibrium. Conduction involves direct interaction/contact, convection involves bulk movement in fluids driven by density differences, and radiation transfers energy by electromagnetic waves and can cross empty space. Conductors transfer thermal energy readily; insulators resist transfer.","connect":"Connect Heat Transfer Mastery across one energy system: energy source → transfer mechanism → change in particles or temperature → larger effect. Then connect the mechanism to weather, technology, ecosystems, or resource use and support the explanation with a model, observation, or data.","v":[["thermal equilibrium","state in which objects in thermal contact have the same temperature and net heat transfer stops"],["conductor","material that transfers thermal energy readily"],["insulator","material that resists thermal energy transfer"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Trace an energy pathway using this lesson. Name the source, transfer mechanism, observable effect, and one larger-system connection. Include evidence and reasoning."},{"id":"s_wk0928_resources","title":"THIS WEEK • Energy Resources & Tradeoffs","src":"Teacher combined-test scope + STEMscopedia-linked Energy Resources support","teach":"Know renewable versus nonrenewable resources, how energy is obtained and transformed, and the advantages and disadvantages of major sources. Evaluate reliability, environmental effects, emissions, land/water impacts, and resource availability. Many electricity systems transform an energy source into motion of a turbine and then electrical energy in a generator; solar photovoltaic systems convert radiant energy more directly to electrical energy.","connect":"Connect Energy Resources & Tradeoffs as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","v":[["renewable resource","resource replenished naturally on a human time scale"],["nonrenewable resource","resource used faster than natural processes replace it on a human time scale"],["tradeoff","a benefit gained while accepting a cost or disadvantage"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"s_terms","title":"Temperature • Thermal Energy • Heating","src":"Teacher study guide + science support","teach":"Temperature describes thermal state and relates to average particle motion. Thermal energy is microscopic energy in a system. Heating/heat transfer is energy moving because of a temperature difference.","connect":"Connect Thermal Energy • Heating across one energy system: energy source → transfer mechanism → change in particles or temperature → larger effect. Then connect the mechanism to weather, technology, ecosystems, or resource use and support the explanation with a model, observation, or data.","v":[["thermal energy","microscopic energy associated with particles in a system"],["heat transfer","energy moving because of a temperature difference"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Trace an energy pathway using this lesson. Name the source, transfer mechanism, observable effect, and one larger-system connection. Include evidence and reasoning."},{"id":"s_three","title":"Conduction • Convection • Radiation","src":"Teacher 49-question guide","teach":"Conduction = direct particle interactions/contact. Convection = bulk circulation in liquids or gases. Radiation = electromagnetic waves and can travel without matter. Memory: CONTACT • CIRCULATE • RAYS.","connect":"Connect Convection • Radiation across one energy system: energy source → transfer mechanism → change in particles or temperature → larger effect. Then connect the mechanism to weather, technology, ecosystems, or resource use and support the explanation with a model, observation, or data.","v":[["conduction","thermal transfer through direct particle interactions/contact"],["convection","energy transfer through circulation in a fluid"],["radiation","energy transfer by electromagnetic waves"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Trace an energy pathway using this lesson. Name the source, transfer mechanism, observable effect, and one larger-system connection. Include evidence and reasoning."},{"id":"s_em","title":"Radiation & EM Waves","src":"Teacher study guide","teach":"The teacher guide includes visible light, infrared, and ultraviolet as electromagnetic waves. Radiation can cross empty space, so energy from the Sun can reach Earth.","connect":"Connect Radiation & EM Waves across one energy system: energy source → transfer mechanism → change in particles or temperature → larger effect. Then connect the mechanism to weather, technology, ecosystems, or resource use and support the explanation with a model, observation, or data.","v":[["electromagnetic wave","a wave that can carry energy through space without requiring matter"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Trace an energy pathway using this lesson. Name the source, transfer mechanism, observable effect, and one larger-system connection. Include evidence and reasoning."},{"id":"s_materials","title":"Conductors & Insulators","src":"Teacher study guide examples","teach":"Conductors allow thermal energy to transfer readily. Metals such as copper and aluminum are strong classroom examples. Insulators slow transfer; teacher examples include rubber, fiberglass, fabric, and many plastics.","connect":"Connect Conductors & Insulators across one energy system: energy source → transfer mechanism → change in particles or temperature → larger effect. Then connect the mechanism to weather, technology, ecosystems, or resource use and support the explanation with a model, observation, or data.","v":[["conductor","material that allows thermal energy to transfer readily"],["insulator","material that slows thermal energy transfer"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Trace an energy pathway using this lesson. Name the source, transfer mechanism, observable effect, and one larger-system connection. Include evidence and reasoning."},{"id":"s_eq","title":"Thermal Equilibrium","src":"Teacher study guide","teach":"When warmer and cooler systems interact, energy transfers from warmer toward cooler until there is no net temperature-driven transfer. That condition is thermal equilibrium.","connect":"Connect Thermal Equilibrium across one energy system: energy source → transfer mechanism → change in particles or temperature → larger effect. Then connect the mechanism to weather, technology, ecosystems, or resource use and support the explanation with a model, observation, or data.","v":[["thermal equilibrium","a condition with no net temperature-driven energy transfer between interacting systems"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Trace an energy pathway using this lesson. Name the source, transfer mechanism, observable effect, and one larger-system connection. Include evidence and reasoning."},{"id":"s_density","title":"Density & Convection Currents","src":"Teacher study guide","teach":"Density tells how much mass is packed into a given volume. In the classroom convection model, warmer fluid often becomes less dense and rises relative to cooler, denser fluid, which sinks and creates circulation.","connect":"Connect Density & Convection Currents across one energy system: energy source → transfer mechanism → change in particles or temperature → larger effect. Then connect the mechanism to weather, technology, ecosystems, or resource use and support the explanation with a model, observation, or data.","v":[["density","mass packed into a given volume"],["convection current","circulating motion in a fluid caused by differences such as temperature/density"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Trace an energy pathway using this lesson. Name the source, transfer mechanism, observable effect, and one larger-system connection. Include evidence and reasoning."},{"id":"s_multi","title":"Multiple Mechanisms","src":"Teacher scenarios + applied reasoning","teach":"Real systems can involve several mechanisms at once. Identify what each mechanism is doing instead of forcing one label onto the whole system.","connect":"Connect Multiple Mechanisms as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","v":[],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"s_renew","title":"Renewable vs Nonrenewable","src":"Teacher Energy Resources chart","teach":"Renewable resources replenish naturally on human-relevant timescales: biomass, geothermal, solar, hydropower, wind. Nonrenewable resources form or replace too slowly for human use: coal and natural gas in the teacher set.","connect":"Connect Renewable vs Nonrenewable as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","v":[["renewable","replenished naturally on a human-relevant timescale"],["nonrenewable","forms or is replaced too slowly for human use"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"s_vocab","title":"Energy Resource Vocabulary","src":"Teacher chart + STEMscopes-linked support","teach":"Biomass = plant/animal material used for energy. Geothermal = Earth’s internal heat. Hydropower = moving/falling water. Solar = sunlight. Wind = moving air. Coal and natural gas = fossil fuels formed over long geologic times.","connect":"Connect Energy Resource Vocabulary as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","v":[["biomass","plant or animal material used as an energy resource"],["geothermal","energy from Earth’s internal heat"],["hydropower","energy from moving or falling water"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"s_path","title":"Resource → Turbine → Generator","src":"Teacher resource chart + classroom slides","teach":"A turbine has blades turned by moving water, steam, or air. A generator converts mechanical input from the turning turbine into electrical energy.","connect":"Connect Resource → Turbine → Generator as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","v":[["turbine","a bladed device turned by moving fluid"],["generator","a device that converts mechanical input into electrical energy"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"s_trade","title":"Energy Tradeoffs","src":"Teacher/classroom resource work + STEMscopes-linked comparison","teach":"Wind: low operational emissions but variable/location-dependent and wildlife concerns. Hydro: controllable but dams affect ecosystems and depend on water. Geothermal: dependable where accessible but geographically limited/costly to install. Solar: intermittent. Fossil fuels: finite; combustion releases pollutants/greenhouse gases. Biomass: renewable when replenished but has land-use/emission tradeoffs.","connect":"Connect Energy Tradeoffs as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","v":[["tradeoff","a balance in which gaining one advantage may involve a cost or disadvantage"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"s_co2","title":"CO₂ • Deforestation • Greenhouse Gases","src":"Teacher study guide","teach":"The teacher guide connects fossil-fuel combustion with increasing atmospheric CO₂. Deforestation can reduce CO₂ uptake and can release stored carbon. Greenhouse gases absorb and re-radiate infrared energy, affecting Earth’s energy balance and temperature.","connect":"Connect Deforestation • Greenhouse Gases as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","v":[["carbon dioxide (CO₂)","a greenhouse gas released by processes including fossil-fuel combustion"],["deforestation","large-scale removal of forests"],["greenhouse gas","a gas that absorbs and re-radiates infrared energy"]],"w":"Write a short CER: Which energy resource would you recommend for a community with strong geothermal access but weak wind? State a claim, give two pieces of evidence, and explain your reasoning. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"s_solar","title":"Solar Cooker Application","src":"Current classroom solar-cooker work","teach":"A solar cooker receives energy by radiation. Reflective surfaces can direct more incoming radiation toward the cooking area. Dark surfaces can absorb radiant energy. Insulation reduces unwanted energy loss.","connect":"Connect Solar Cooker Application as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","v":[],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"s_precision","title":"Teacher-Style Precision Traps","src":"Teacher guide + parent science nuance","teach":"For test preparation, recognize classroom shorthand but understand the precise idea. “Heat rises” means warmer, less-dense fluid tends to rise in convection. Do not memorize “all energy on Earth comes from the Sun” as a scientific absolute; other energy sources exist.","connect":"Connect Teacher-Style Precision Traps as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","v":[],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"s_tn_current_ps3","title":"Current TN Standard • Energy in Systems","src":"Tennessee Academic Standards for Science adopted 2022, implemented 2025–26 • 6.PS3.1","teach":"The current Tennessee standard goes beyond naming heat-transfer types. Riley should analyze energy in a system and track transfers among kinetic energy, potential energy such as gravitational or elastic, chemical energy, and thermal energy. The key idea is conservation: energy can transfer or transform, but the system accounting must make sense.","connect":"Connect Energy in Systems as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","v":[["kinetic energy","energy associated with motion"],["potential energy","stored energy associated with position or condition"],["chemical energy","energy stored in chemical systems"],["conservation of energy","energy is transferred or transformed rather than created from nothing or destroyed"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"s_tn_particle_models","title":"Current TN/IXL • Particle Motion & Thermal Models","src":"Current Tennessee 6.PS3.2 + IXL Grade 6 L.1–L.4 and M.1–M.3","teach":"IXL-level science asks Riley to reason from a model, graph, or particle picture. Faster average particle motion generally corresponds to higher temperature. Thermal energy depends on more than temperature alone; the amount of matter also matters. When systems at different temperatures interact, predict the direction of energy transfer and the resulting temperature change.","connect":"Connect Particle Motion & Thermal Models across one energy system: energy source → transfer mechanism → change in particles or temperature → larger effect. Then connect the mechanism to weather, technology, ecosystems, or resource use and support the explanation with a model, observation, or data.","v":[["particle model","a representation used to reason about matter too small to observe directly"],["system","the matter or objects chosen for analysis"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Trace an energy pathway using this lesson. Name the source, transfer mechanism, observable effect, and one larger-system connection. Include evidence and reasoning."},{"id":"s_tn_resources_data","title":"Current TN Standard • Resources, Data & Sustainability","src":"Tennessee 6.ESS3.1 + IXL BB.2–BB.4","teach":"The current standard requires more than sorting renewable and nonrenewable. Riley should use data to explain consumption, availability, sustainability, and impacts on Earth systems. A claim should be supported by the evidence in a table, graph, or scenario.","connect":"Connect Resources, Data & Sustainability as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","v":[["sustainability","using or managing resources in ways that can continue over time"],["consumption","the amount of a resource that is used"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"s_tn_tech_compare","title":"Current TN Standard • Compare Energy Technologies","src":"Tennessee 6.ESS3.2 • teacher energy-resource unit","teach":"Riley should compare technologies using evidence, not memorize that one source is always best. Consider energy availability, reliability, location, cost, environmental effects, and how the technology converts a resource into usable energy.","connect":"Connect Compare Energy Technologies as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","v":[["constraint","a limit or condition a solution must satisfy"],["tradeoff","a benefit gained while accepting a cost or disadvantage"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"s_tn_engineering","title":"Current TN Standard • Thermal Engineering Challenge","src":"Tennessee 6.ETS1.2 • design, construct, test, and communicate a thermal-transfer solution","teach":"The current engineering standard expects Riley to design and test a device that minimizes or maximizes thermal-energy transfer. That means define the goal, choose materials based on conduction, convection, and radiation, collect data, compare results, and explain how evidence supports an improved design. Her solar-cooker work is a natural application.","connect":"Connect Thermal Engineering Challenge across one energy system: energy source → transfer mechanism → change in particles or temperature → larger effect. Then connect the mechanism to weather, technology, ecosystems, or resource use and support the explanation with a model, observation, or data.","v":[["criterion","a feature used to judge whether a design succeeds"],["test data","measurements collected to evaluate a design"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Trace an energy pathway using this lesson. Name the source, transfer mechanism, observable effect, and one larger-system connection. Include evidence and reasoning."},{"id":"s_class_temp_heat","title":"CLASS HEAT • Temperature vs Heat","src":"Riley classroom Heat & Heat Transfer notes • Topics 1–2 + Sum It Up","teach":"CLASS SOURCE FIRST. Kinetic energy is energy associated with motion. Temperature measures the AVERAGE kinetic energy of particles. Riley's sheet uses Celsius (°C) and Kelvin (K). Temperature does not depend on the size/amount of a substance: two different-size pots can both boil at 100°C, while the larger pot can contain more thermal energy overall because it contains more matter. The class sheet treats HEAT as a transfer/flow of thermal energy caused by a temperature difference, with net transfer from warmer toward cooler.","connect":"Connect Temperature vs Heat across one energy system: energy source → transfer mechanism → change in particles or temperature → larger effect. Then connect the mechanism to weather, technology, ecosystems, or resource use and support the explanation with a model, observation, or data.","v":[["kinetic energy","energy associated with motion"],["temperature","a measure of average kinetic energy of particles"],["Celsius","a temperature scale used in science"],["Kelvin","a temperature scale used in science"],["heat","transfer/flow of thermal energy due to a temperature difference"]],"w":"Explain why a burning match can have a higher temperature than a large pot of boiling water while the pot can have more thermal energy overall. CONNECTION: Trace an energy pathway using this lesson. Name the source, transfer mechanism, observable effect, and one larger-system connection. Include evidence and reasoning."},{"id":"s_class_equilibrium_conduction","title":"CLASS HEAT • Equilibrium & Conduction","src":"Riley classroom Heat & Heat Transfer notes • Topics 3–4","teach":"THERMAL EQUILIBRIUM occurs after interacting objects reach the same temperature, so there is no longer a net temperature-driven transfer between them. Riley's diagram begins with 100°C and 0°C blocks and ends with both at 50°C. CONDUCTION is heat transfer through a material by direct particle contact/interactions. In the classroom model, heated particles move faster and pass energy through interactions with neighboring particles. Class examples include hot sand to bare feet, a warm phone to fingertips, and a metal object touching a heated surface.","connect":"Connect Equilibrium & Conduction across one energy system: energy source → transfer mechanism → change in particles or temperature → larger effect. Then connect the mechanism to weather, technology, ecosystems, or resource use and support the explanation with a model, observation, or data.","v":[["thermal equilibrium","equal-temperature condition with no net temperature-driven heat transfer"],["conduction","heat transfer through a material by direct particle contact/interactions"]],"w":"Describe what happens from the moment a hotter solid touches a cooler solid until thermal equilibrium is reached. CONNECTION: Trace an energy pathway using this lesson. Name the source, transfer mechanism, observable effect, and one larger-system connection. Include evidence and reasoning."},{"id":"s_class_materials_convection","title":"CLASS HEAT • Conductors, Insulators & Convection","src":"Riley classroom Heat & Heat Transfer notes • Topics 5–6 + Sum It Up","teach":"CONDUCTORS allow thermal energy to move through efficiently; the class notes use metals as the major example. INSULATORS slow thermal-energy transfer and can help keep heat in or out; source examples include feathers, wool, and rubber. CONVECTION transfers energy through a FLUID—a liquid or gas—by movement of the fluid. In Riley's class model, warmer fluid becomes less dense and rises relative to cooler, more-dense fluid, which sinks. This circulation is a CONVECTION CURRENT. Examples include boiling water, baseboard-heated air, warmer attics, and ocean circulation.","connect":"Connect Conductors, Insulators & Convection across one energy system: energy source → transfer mechanism → change in particles or temperature → larger effect. Then connect the mechanism to weather, technology, ecosystems, or resource use and support the explanation with a model, observation, or data.","v":[["conductor","material through which thermal energy transfers efficiently"],["insulator","material that slows thermal-energy transfer"],["fluid","a liquid or gas"],["convection","thermal-energy transfer by movement of a fluid"],["convection current","circulating movement of warmer and cooler fluid"],["density","how much mass is packed into a given volume"]],"w":"Describe a convection current using warmer, cooler, less dense, more dense, rises, and sinks. CONNECTION: Trace an energy pathway using this lesson. Name the source, transfer mechanism, observable effect, and one larger-system connection. Include evidence and reasoning."},{"id":"s_class_radiation","title":"CLASS HEAT • Radiation & Electromagnetic Rays","src":"Riley classroom Heat & Heat Transfer notes • Topic 7 + Sum It Up","teach":"RADIATION transfers thermal energy by electromagnetic waves. It does not require matter between source and receiver, so it can cross empty space; this is why energy from the Sun reaches Earth. Riley's class notes also say this radiation travels at the speed of light and can be modeled as traveling in straight lines, and call it thermal radiation. A campfire warming your body without direct contact is another class example. Teacher material also names visible, infrared, and ultraviolet as electromagnetic waves.","connect":"Connect Radiation & Electromagnetic Rays across one energy system: energy source → transfer mechanism → change in particles or temperature → larger effect. Then connect the mechanism to weather, technology, ecosystems, or resource use and support the explanation with a model, observation, or data.","v":[["radiation","energy transfer by electromagnetic waves"],["electromagnetic wave","a wave that can carry energy through space without requiring matter"],["thermal radiation","electromagnetic radiation associated with thermal-energy transfer"]],"w":"Explain why radiation, but not conduction or convection, can transfer energy from the Sun to Earth through space. CONNECTION: Trace an energy pathway using this lesson. Name the source, transfer mechanism, observable effect, and one larger-system connection. Include evidence and reasoning."},{"id":"s_class_source_review","title":"CLASS HEAT • Source-Matched Mixed Review","src":"Riley classroom Sum It Up + Bill Nye Heat Transfer worksheet","teach":"Use the exact class distinctions together. DIRECT CONTACT → conduction. MOVING LIQUID/GAS with warmer/less-dense material rising and cooler/more-dense material sinking → convection. ELECTROMAGNETIC WAVES, including transfer across empty space → radiation. TEMPERATURE describes average particle kinetic energy; HEAT describes thermal-energy transfer caused by a temperature difference. Real situations can involve several mechanisms, but class questions often ask for the mechanism highlighted by the stated evidence.","connect":"Connect Source-Matched Mixed Review across one energy system: energy source → transfer mechanism → change in particles or temperature → larger effect. Then connect the mechanism to weather, technology, ecosystems, or resource use and support the explanation with a model, observation, or data.","v":[],"w":"Create one original example each of conduction, convection, and radiation and identify the clue that proves each classification. CONNECTION: Trace an energy pathway using this lesson. Name the source, transfer mechanism, observable effect, and one larger-system connection. Include evidence and reasoning."},{"id":"s_mixed_practice_3","title":"★ RANDOM MIXED PRACTICE 3 • SCIENCE • 15 New Questions","src":"Riley classroom Heat & Heat Transfer worksheets + teacher Energy Resources guide + current TN science alignment","teach":"NEW MIXED RETRIEVAL. Questions appear in a different random order each time. Heat-transfer concepts are mixed with temperature, thermal energy, equilibrium, particle motion, conductors/insulators, and energy resources so Riley has to identify the concept from evidence.","connect":"Connect SCIENCE • 15 New Questions as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","v":[],"w":"A metal thermos and an insulated thermos start with equally hot water. Predict which will cool more slowly and explain using conduction, convection, and/or radiation. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits."},{"id":"science_v35_notes_environment","title":"Environments and populations: repair the guided notes","src":"Uploaded Environments and Populations Guided Notes; corrected explanations checked against biological definitions. Original parallel application items.","teach":"An ecosystem includes biotic and abiotic components. Water and sunlight are abiotic. Soil contains minerals, water and air, but also organisms and dead organic matter: distinguish soil conditions from its biological contents. Photosynthesis uses light energy to make sugars; plants do not manufacture water. Different species have different resource needs. A niche is a role, not just a place. Competition depends on limited resources, not merely sharing a location. Zebras mainly graze; the worksheet's description of them as browsers is inaccurate. Organisms require water, energy, nutrients and suitable living conditions; many animals also need shelter from weather or predators. Sunlight supports photosynthesis, warmth and daily activity cycles. Species tolerate different temperature ranges. Some archaea tolerate very high temperatures, including the approximately 93°C example in the notes. Cacti reduce water loss; kangaroo rats obtain water from food and metabolism. These adaptations do not eliminate the need for water. Forest soil often retains more moisture and organic matter than desert soil, but soil properties vary within both ecosystems. Choose garden plants using local light, soil, temperature and water conditions, including irrigation; also consider animals that may eat them.","model":"In a dry garden, low water availability is an abiotic limiting factor. Two plants competing for the remaining water have a biotic interaction over an abiotic resource. In a one-square-kilometre habitat with 24 deer, density is 24 deer per square kilometre. Capacity may fall if sustained drought reduces available food.","connect":"Connect irrigation in Egypt with water as an abiotic resource. Explain what stays the same (water limits growth) and what changes (people manage delivery). Connect Environments and populations: repair the guided notes as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","success":"Complete distinct practice, write the transfer, repair mistakes, then use a fresh delayed check. Only independent responses count as readiness evidence.","v":[["ecosystem","Interacting organisms and their nonliving surroundings."],["population","Members of the same species in a particular area."],["photosynthesis","Using light energy to make sugars from carbon dioxide and water, releasing oxygen in oxygenic photosynthesis."],["irrigation","Supplying water to soil or crops through a managed system."],["food web","A model of connected feeding relationships."],["browser","An animal that eats leaves and shoots of woody plants; giraffes are browsers."],["population density","The number of individuals per unit area or volume."],["carrying capacity","The population of a particular species an environment can sustain under specified resource conditions; it can change."],["marine","Related to saltwater environments."],["freshwater","Water environments with low salt concentration."],["terrestrial","Related to land environments."],["abiotic factors","Nonliving physical and chemical conditions such as light, water, temperature, and mineral composition."],["biotic factors","Organisms and their biological interactions; living and once-living material in the classroom classification."],["limiting factor","A condition or resource that restricts population growth or survival."],["adaptation","An inherited characteristic that can improve survival or reproduction in a particular environment."],["predator","An organism that hunts and eats another organism."],["prey","An organism hunted and eaten by a predator."],["competition","Organisms using the same limited resource."],["niche","An organism's ecological role, resource use, and relationships."],["interspecific competition","Competition between members of different species."],["intraspecific competition","Competition between members of the same species."]],"w":"CONNECTION: Sort water, light, mineral particles, grass, fungi, and deer into abiotic components or biotic components. Explain the mixed nature of whole soil. Compare same-species and different-species competition; calculate density for 18 animals in 3 square kilometres. Predict how declining resources may change carrying capacity and explain the mechanism. Repeat with fresh examples in a later session. Name the prior idea, the new idea, what stays the same, what changes, and evidence.  CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits.","learningTools":{"cue":"Read the explanation and worked model first.","task":"Open focused practice. Answer before looking at feedback; save your explanation.","url":"exam-practice.html?set=science_v35_notes_environment","apply":"Explain a new example using a claim, specific evidence and reasoning.","delayed":"Return in a later session and choose a fresh form before rereading."}},{"id":"science_v35_notes_foodweb","title":"Matter and energy in food webs: roles and change","src":"Uploaded Matter and Energy in Food Webs and Relationships within Ecosystems pages; packet has a missing producer/consumer continuation and ambiguous printed food-web arrows, so full packet coverage remains partial","teach":"Energy flows through feeding pathways, while matter is recycled. Producers transform an energy source into chemical energy stored in food; they do not create energy. Food-web arrows point from food to consumer. Consumer level depends on the pathway. In grass -> grasshopper -> mouse -> snake, the mouse is secondary and snake tertiary; if the mouse eats grass directly, those levels change. Decomposers act on remains from multiple levels. Removing one species may affect others, but alternate food sources matter; do not guarantee total collapse. Competition, predation and symbiosis are different relationships; mutualism benefits both partners, parasitism harms the host. Autotroph means producer. Herbivores eat producers, carnivores eat animals, and omnivores eat both; trophic level still depends on a particular pathway. Many zooplankton are invertebrates, or animals without backbones. Aquatic systems cover most of Earth’s surface and include low-salt freshwater and salty marine habitats. Plants and algae store chemical energy made available through photosynthesis; muscles and nervous systems use chemical energy obtained from food. Energy is conserved overall, but usable energy disperses as heat at each transfer.","model":"Trace algae -> plant-eating zooplankton -> small fish -> large fish. The same fish may occupy another level if it eats different prey. A frog decline may reduce food for a snake, but a prediction must check other prey in the actual web. Gut bacteria gaining food while providing vitamins illustrate mutualism.","connect":"Connect energy transfer to the earlier energy-conservation unit and use claim, diagram evidence, and reasoning from ELA when predicting change. Connect Matter and energy in food webs: roles and change as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","success":"Complete distinct practice, write the transfer, repair mistakes, then use a fresh delayed check. Only independent responses count as readiness evidence.","v":[["producer","An organism that builds organic food from inorganic materials using an energy source."],["consumer","An organism that obtains energy by eating other organisms or organic material."],["primary consumer","A consumer feeding directly on a producer in the specified pathway."],["secondary consumer","A consumer feeding on a primary consumer in the specified pathway."],["tertiary consumer","A consumer feeding on a secondary consumer in the specified pathway."],["quaternary consumer","A consumer feeding on a tertiary consumer in the specified pathway."],["decomposer","An organism such as certain fungi or bacteria that breaks down dead material or waste and recycles nutrients."],["phytoplankton","Microscopic photosynthetic organisms that form an important aquatic producer base; not all are plants."],["zooplankton","Drifting aquatic consumers; many eat phytoplankton, but their diets vary."],["predation","A feeding interaction in which a predator eats prey."],["symbiosis","A close, long-term relationship between different species."],["mutualism","A relationship in which both species benefit."],["parasitism","A relationship in which a parasite benefits while harming its host."],["parasite","An organism that obtains resources from a host and harms it."],["host","An organism on or in which a parasite lives or feeds."],["autotroph","A producer that builds organic food from inorganic materials using light or chemical energy."],["herbivore","A consumer that eats plants or other producers."],["carnivore","A consumer that eats animals."],["omnivore","A consumer that eats both producer material and animals."],["invertebrate","An animal without a backbone; many small aquatic consumers are invertebrates."]],"w":"CONNECTION: Draw two food chains sharing a consumer, mark energy arrows, and label each feeding level. Explain a role that changes between pathways. Compare mutualism and parasitism using benefits and harms. Predict one direct and one indirect consequence of a frog decline while stating the assumptions. Repeat later with a new aquatic chain. Name the prior idea, the new idea, what stays the same, what changes, and evidence.  CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits.","learningTools":{"cue":"Read the explanation and worked model first.","task":"Open focused practice. Answer before looking at feedback; save your explanation.","url":"exam-practice.html?set=science_v35_notes_foodweb","apply":"Explain a new example using a claim, specific evidence and reasoning.","delayed":"Return in a later session and choose a fresh form before rereading."}},{"id":"science_v37_biosphere","title":"Biotic describes; biosphere names a level","src":"Ecology vocabulary and five-level classroom scope; released quiz review inspected October 28, 2026. Original corrective teaching and examples; private responses omitted.","teach":"Bio means life, but words sharing a root need not have the same meaning. BIOTIC describes living components, such as algae, trees and birds. BIOSPHERE names all parts of Earth where life exists. It is the largest of these five organization levels: organism → population → community → ecosystem → biosphere. A community contains populations of different species. An ecosystem includes their interactions with nonliving conditions. The ocean water itself is abiotic; the organisms living in it are biotic.","model":"A single cattail is an organism. The cattails in one marsh are a population. Cattails, fish and frogs form part of its community. Add water, light and temperature and their interactions: a marsh ecosystem. All Earth’s life-supporting regions together make up the biosphere. Biotic is an adjective describing the cattail; biosphere is a noun naming the largest level.","connect":"Use ELA noun/adjective roles to distinguish science words that share a root. Connect Biotic describes; biosphere names a level as a resource–energy–Earth system: where a resource comes from, how its energy is converted, what service it provides, and what effects or tradeoffs follow. Use Math for fair data comparisons and ELA claim–evidence–reasoning to defend a conclusion.","success":"Save first answers, explain every miss, complete fresh repair, then use the new-format delayed task after at least 24 hours. Writing needs review; practice completion is not mastery.","v":[["biotic","Describing living components of an environment."],["biosphere","All parts of Earth where life exists."],["ecosystem","A community and its interactions with nonliving surroundings."]],"w":"CONNECTION: Write one sentence using biotic as an adjective and one using biosphere as a noun. Then compare a local forest ecosystem with the biosphere: what stays the same, what changes in scale, and what evidence supports your distinction? After 24 hours, repeat with a coral reef without notes. Parent checks both definitions and examples. CONNECTION: Build a resource-system explanation: acquisition, conversion, output, two impacts, and a comparison. Use one number or data statement and explain its limits.","learningTools":{"cue":"Read the quantities and labels before choosing a method.","task":"Open matching practice, diagrams, printable student copy and written transfer. These repeat the same lesson checks; do not count a repeated answer as fresh evidence.","apply":"CONNECTION: Write one sentence using biotic as an adjective and one using biosphere as a noun. Then compare a local forest ecosystem with the biosphere: what stays the same, what changes in scale, and what evidence supports your distinction? After 24 hours, repeat with a coral reef without notes. Parent checks both definitions and examples.","delayed":"After at least 24 hours, make a new example with different quantities or organisms. Explain it without notes; have an adult review it.","url":"exam-practice.html?set=science_v37_biosphere"}}]},"SS":{"name":"Social Studies","lessons":[{"id":"ss_v34_egypt_quiz","title":"CURRENT WEEK • Ancient Egypt Geography, Irrigation, Architecture & Pharaohs","src":"Sunset Middle School 6th grade newsletter dated October 23, 2026","teach":"The Thursday Ancient Egyptian Geography quiz asks Riley to connect place to life in antiquity. The Nile flows north toward the Mediterranean. Upper Egypt is south and upstream; Lower Egypt is north near the delta. Seasonal water and deposited silt supported farming, while irrigation helped people direct water. Deserts affected travel and offered some protection; the river supported transportation and exchange. Pharaohs held political and religious authority. Architecture required resources, skilled workers, planning, and organized labor. Geography influenced choices, but people and institutions shaped the results.","model":"MODEL: Condition—the Nile valley provided water and fertile floodplain soil. Human response—farmers used irrigation and communities coordinated labor. Consequence—more dependable food production could support specialists, administrators, builders, and larger settlements. Limitation—the river did not automatically create monuments or government; people made decisions and performed the work.","connect":"Compare Nile geography with the Tigris-Euphrates system, and connect irrigation to Science systems thinking. Connect Ancient Egypt Geography, Irrigation, Architecture & Pharaohs through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","success":"I can use a map and evidence to explain how geography and human choices shaped ancient Egyptian life.","v":[["Nile Delta","fertile branching river region in northern Egypt"],["Upper Egypt","southern, upstream Nile region"],["Lower Egypt","northern, downstream region near the delta"],["irrigation","directing water to crops"],["pharaoh","Egyptian ruler with political and religious authority"],["architecture","design and construction of buildings and monuments"]],"w":"Draw and label the Nile, delta, Upper Egypt, Lower Egypt, Mediterranean Sea, Red Sea, Sahara, and Nubia. Then write a condition → response → consequence explanation using irrigation, surplus, specialization, pharaoh, and architecture. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence."},{"id":"ss_v21_israel_final","title":"CURRENT OCT. 9 • SOCIAL STUDIES 1 • Ancient Israel Final Retrieval","src":"10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • teacher Ancient Israel guide • Tennessee Grade 6 Ancient Egypt standards 6.13–6.19 • Michigan Open Book project identified; exact Egypt chapter not yet recovered","teach":"The Ancient Israel test is Wednesday, October 21. Do not replace the official 24-target path; retrieve it. Reconstruct maps and migrations, explain Abraham and Moses, relate Scripture/Tanakh/Torah/covenant, distinguish Exodus and diaspora, explain Saul-David-Solomon, rebuild the divided-kingdom chain, and connect captivity to Cyrus. The final repair sequence is north Israel—Assyria—722 BCE; south Judah/Jerusalem—Babylon—captivity; Babylon—Persia/Cyrus—539 BCE—return permitted.","model":"MODEL: “Why was Cyrus important?” Person—Persian ruler. Event—conquered Babylon in 539 BCE. Effect—permitted Jewish exiles to return to Jerusalem and rebuild the Temple. Significance—changed the migration timeline from forced exile toward authorized return.","connect":"Connect “why important?” answers to ELA claim-evidence-reasoning. Connect SOCIAL STUDIES 1 • Ancient Israel Final Retrieval through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","success":"I can teach every official target without notes and score at least 90% on the fresh readiness form.","v":[["retrieval","recalling learning without looking at notes"],["chronology","events arranged in time order"],["significance","why a person or event mattered"],["map evidence","location or movement information supported by a map"]],"w":"Without notes, complete the official 24-target teach-back, a blank map, and the migration/kingdom/conquest timeline. Mark any hesitation for repair before the 30-question form. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence."},{"id":"ss_v21_egypt_geography","title":"CURRENT OCT. 9 • SOCIAL STUDIES 2 • Ancient Egypt Map & Nile Geography","src":"10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • teacher Ancient Israel guide • Tennessee Grade 6 Ancient Egypt standards 6.13–6.19 • Michigan Open Book project identified; exact Egypt chapter not yet recovered","teach":"Ancient Egyptian civilization developed along the Nile River in northeastern Africa. Required map features include the Mediterranean Sea, Nile Delta, Nile River, Red Sea, Upper Egypt, Lower Egypt, Nubia, and the Sahara. “Upper” Egypt is south/upstream at higher elevation; “Lower” Egypt is north/downstream near the delta. The Nile generally flows north toward the Mediterranean. Cataracts are rocky rapids that hindered navigation. Deserts limited settlement but also provided some protection; seas, river travel, and routes still allowed trade and contact.","model":"MODEL: Start south in Upper Egypt/Nubia, trace the Nile north through its valley, then fan into the Nile Delta in Lower Egypt before reaching the Mediterranean. Red Sea lies east; Sahara surrounds much of the fertile river corridor. Explain directions using flow and elevation, not the page’s top alone.","connect":"Connect Egypt’s river corridor to Mesopotamia’s river valleys while avoiding the claim that geography determined every outcome. Connect SOCIAL STUDIES 2 • Ancient Egypt Map & Nile Geography through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","success":"I can locate and use every map feature as evidence in a cause-and-effect explanation.","v":[["Nile Delta","fan-shaped fertile region where the Nile branches before the Mediterranean"],["Upper Egypt","southern, upstream region of ancient Egypt"],["Lower Egypt","northern, downstream delta region"],["Nubia","region south of ancient Egypt along the Nile"],["cataract","rocky rapid that makes river travel difficult"]],"w":"Draw a blank Ancient Egypt map and label all eight required features. Add arrows for Nile flow and one sentence per feature explaining historical significance. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence."},{"id":"ss_v21_egypt_rule","title":"CURRENT OCT. 9 • SOCIAL STUDIES 3 • Beginnings of Egyptian Rule","src":"10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • teacher Ancient Israel guide • Tennessee Grade 6 Ancient Egypt standards 6.13–6.19 • Michigan Open Book project identified; exact Egypt chapter not yet recovered","teach":"Before political unification, communities developed along the Nile and Upper and Lower Egypt emerged as regions. Egyptian tradition associates Narmer, often called Menes in later accounts, with unifying the two regions around 3100 BCE and establishing early dynastic rule. A dynasty is a sequence of rulers from the same family. Pharaoh became the title for Egypt’s ruler, who held political and religious authority and was regarded as a god-king. Central rule helped coordinate taxation, labor, irrigation, defense, recordkeeping, and large projects, but society also depended on farmers, artisans, laborers, scribes, officials, and enslaved people.","model":"MODEL: Cause—settlements and agricultural production along the Nile required coordination and created resources. Event—Upper and Lower Egypt were unified under early dynastic rule. Effects—central administration, taxation, organized labor, defense, and shared royal authority expanded. Do not reduce civilization to one ruler; name the work of ordinary people and institutions.","connect":"Connect government coordination to Mesopotamian irrigation and recordkeeping, noting similarities and differences. Connect SOCIAL STUDIES 3 • Beginnings of Egyptian Rule through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","success":"I can explain political beginnings through people, institutions, geography, and labor—not ruler memorization alone.","v":[["unification","joining separate regions under one rule"],["dynasty","a line of rulers from the same family"],["pharaoh","title for an Egyptian ruler with political and religious authority"],["god-king","a ruler believed to have divine status or connection"],["scribe","a trained writer and recordkeeper"]],"w":"Explain the chain from Nile settlements to unification and central rule. Include Narmer/Menes, dynasty, pharaoh, scribes, labor, and one benefit and burden of centralized power. CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link."},{"id":"ss_v21_egypt_nile","title":"CURRENT OCT. 9 • SOCIAL STUDIES 4 • How Geography Influenced Egyptian History","src":"10/9/2026 teacher newsletter • teacher Ancient Israel guide • Tennessee Grade 6 Ancient Egypt standards 6.13–6.19 • Michigan Open Book project identified; exact Egypt chapter not yet recovered","teach":"The Nile provided water, fertile silt, transportation, fish, reeds/papyrus, and a corridor for settlement. Its seasonal floods were more predictable than Mesopotamian river floods, although outcomes still varied and required human planning. Irrigation extended water to fields. Deserts and cataracts could hinder invasion and movement, while the Mediterranean, Red Sea, Nile, and land routes supported trade and cultural exchange. Geography created opportunities and constraints; people made choices, developed technology, organized labor, traded, fought, worshiped, and built institutions. Never claim the environment automatically caused one culture.","model":"MODEL: Condition—most of Egypt was arid, while the Nile corridor supplied water and fertile floodplain. Response—communities clustered near the river and used irrigation and organized labor. Effects—surplus, specialization, trade, government, and dense settlements. Boundary—the river did not by itself create writing, religion, or monarchy.","connect":"Connect the Egypt chain to the earlier Neolithic and Mesopotamia units and use ELA cause/effect structure. Connect SOCIAL STUDIES 4 • How Geography Influenced Egyptian History through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","success":"I can explain multiple geographic influences with evidence, human responses, benefits, costs, and limits.","v":[["silt","fine fertile soil deposited by water"],["irrigation","moving water to crops"],["floodplain","land beside a river that can receive floodwater and sediment"],["papyrus","Nile plant used for materials including writing sheets"],["geographic determinism","the incorrect idea that geography alone controls human history"]],"w":"Write a cause-and-effect explanation linking the Nile to settlement, farming, surplus, specialization, government, and trade. Add one limitation showing geography did not determine everything. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence."},{"id":"ss_v20_study_guide","title":"ANCIENT ISRAEL • The Official 24-Target Path","src":"Teacher’s Ancient Israel Study Guide and class maps","teach":"1. Israelites Map and Migrations Map — Locate the Mediterranean Sea, Canaan, Jerusalem, Sea of Galilee, Jordan River, Dead Sea, Red Sea, Sinai Peninsula, Egypt, Mesopotamia, Assyria, Babylon, and Persia. Connect every migration arrow to its cause and result. 2. Jerusalem — David made it the capital; Solomon built the first Temple there; it remained in southern Judah after the split; Babylon conquered it; Cyrus later permitted exiles to return there. 3. Abraham — Important for covenant, the early monotheistic tradition, and migration from Mesopotamia toward Canaan. 4. Moses — Important for leading the Exodus from slavery in Egypt and receiving covenant law, including the Ten Commandments, at Mount Sinai. 5. Tanakh — The Hebrew Bible, a collection of Jewish sacred writings. 6. Torah — The foundational teaching and law in the first five books of the Tanakh. 7. Covenant — A binding relationship or agreement with responsibilities. 8. Scripture — Sacred writing. The Tanakh is Jewish Scripture; the Torah is a central section within it. 9. Exodus — The Israelites’ departure from slavery in Egypt under Moses. 10. Hebrew migrations — Movements caused by covenant, famine, escape from slavery, conquest and exile, or permitted return. 11. Ten Commandments — Central moral and religious laws associated with Moses and Mount Sinai. 12. King Saul — First king, military leader, and unifier of the tribes. 13. King David — United the kingdom and made Jerusalem its capital. 14. King Solomon — Known for wisdom and Proverbs; built the first Temple in Jerusalem. 15. Monotheism — Belief in one God. 16. Jewish diaspora — The wider dispersion of Jewish people beyond their homeland. 17. Breakup of the kingdom — After Solomon, the united kingdom divided into Israel and Judah. 18. North, south, and Jerusalem — Israel was north; Judah was south; Jerusalem was in Judah. 19. Babylonian Captivity — Babylon conquered Judah and Jerusalem and forced many Judeans into exile in Babylon. 20. Who conquered the Babylonians? — Persia under Cyrus the Great in 539 BCE. 21. Cyrus the Great — Persian king who conquered Babylon and permitted Jewish exiles to return to Jerusalem and rebuild the Temple. 22. Mesopotamia — Land between the Tigris and Euphrates; an early-civilization region connected to Abraham’s migration tradition. 23. Paleolithic — Mobile hunter-gatherer life using stone tools. 24. Neolithic — Farming, domestication, permanent settlements, specialization, and the growth of civilization. Keep the official 24-target order unchanged when you retrieve it.","model":"Target 18 requires three linked facts: Israel was the northern kingdom; Judah was the southern kingdom; Jerusalem remained in Judah.","connect":"Use the target number to decide whether the answer needs a location, definition, cause, sequence, or explanation of significance. Connect every target to an earlier and later target. Connect The Official 24-Target Path through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","success":"I can number a blank page 1–24 and explain every target in the teacher’s exact order without notes.","v":[["target","one exact item on the teacher’s study guide"],["significance","why a person, place, belief, or event mattered"],["chronology","events arranged in time order"]],"w":"On blank paper, write 1–24. Give each target name and a complete one- or two-sentence answer. Circle any target you cannot explain without notes. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#SS","cue":"Place → person → cause → movement → result.","task":"Use map reconstruction, timeline sorting, and the teacher’s 24 ordered recall cards. Keep the teacher’s order.","apply":"Draw the geographic anchors; explain each migration’s cause and result; rebuild all three conquest-and-return rows from memory.","delayed":"Tomorrow, redraw the maps and explain the conquest sequence backward as well as forward."}},{"id":"ss_v20_israelites_song","title":"TEST TODAY • “The Israelites” Song Memory Bridge","src":"Mr. Nicky — THE ISRAELITES song; matched to the teacher’s 24-target Ancient Israel guide","link":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=88ubPVlgp-g","teach":"Listen once for the story, then turn the video off and retrieve it. The song reinforces these guide connections: Ten Commandments—Torah—Mount Sinai; monotheism—covenant—Abraham—Canaan; famine—Egypt—slavery—Moses—Exodus; Tanakh as the Hebrew Bible; Saul—David—Jerusalem—Solomon—Temple—kingdom split; Assyrian attack; Judah—Babylonian Captivity—diaspora; Persia—Cyrus—return permitted. The song is a memory bridge, not the study guide. Add the exact facts it does not secure: Israel was north; Judah was south and contained Jerusalem; Israel—Assyria—722 BCE; Judah/Jerusalem—Babylon—captivity; Babylon—Persia/Cyrus—539 BCE—return permitted. The map labels and migrations must still be reconstructed independently.","model":"After listening, say: “Abraham connects covenant and migration; Moses connects Exodus and law; Saul, David, and Solomon connect the monarchy to Jerusalem and the Temple; division leads to Assyrian and Babylonian conquest; Cyrus connects Persia to permitted return.” Then add the two dates and the north/south locations without replaying.","connect":"Connect the song’s chronological story to the numbered teacher guide. For each remembered lyric idea, name its target number and supply any missing map, date, direction, cause, or consequence. Connect “The Israelites” Song Memory Bridge through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","success":"Without replaying, I can name at least twelve song anchors, match each to the teacher guide, add the exact 722 BCE and 539 BCE chains, and redraw the required maps.","v":[["memory bridge","a cue that helps retrieval but does not replace complete learning"],["anchor","a key fact used to rebuild a larger sequence"],["supplement","an added support used alongside the controlling study guide"]],"w":"VIDEO OFF: Write twelve song anchors from memory. Beside each, write the matching study-guide target number. Then add both dates, the north/south/Jerusalem facts, the three-row chain, and both maps. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#SS","cue":"Place → person → cause → movement → result.","task":"Use map reconstruction, timeline sorting, and the teacher’s 24 ordered recall cards. Keep the teacher’s order.","apply":"Draw the geographic anchors; explain each migration’s cause and result; rebuild all three conquest-and-return rows from memory.","delayed":"Tomorrow, redraw the maps and explain the conquest sequence backward as well as forward."}},{"id":"ss_v20_test_eve","title":"TEST TODAY • 24-Target Closed-Note Recall","src":"Teacher’s Ancient Israel Study Guide and class maps","teach":"Use this after the official 24-target lesson and song retrieval bridge. Before revealing choices, answer each numbered teacher target aloud in a complete sentence. On Pass 1, identify every weak target. Repair only those targets in the matching lesson. On Pass 2, answer all 24 again in a new order. Finish by drawing both maps and the three-row kingdom-to-return sequence from memory.","model":"A complete significance answer names the person, the action or idea, and why it mattered. Example structure: “Moses was important because he led the Exodus from slavery in Egypt and is associated with covenant law and the Ten Commandments at Mount Sinai.”","connect":"Tell the complete story: Abraham and covenant migration → famine and Egypt → Moses, Exodus, and law → Saul, David, and Solomon → divided kingdom → Assyrian and Babylonian conquest → captivity and diaspora → Persia, Cyrus, and permitted return. Connect 24-Target Closed-Note Recall through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","success":"I can answer all 24 teacher targets without notes, redraw both maps with causes on every arrow, and reproduce the three-row sequence forward and backward. Any miss is repaired and retested before the final check.","v":[["closed-note recall","producing an answer from memory before seeing choices"],["repair","correcting a missed idea and explaining why the correction is right"],["retest","answering the repaired idea again without support"]],"w":"FINAL ORAL PROOF: Number a blank page 1–24 and answer every target in order. Then draw both maps and write: Israel—Assyria—722 BCE; Judah/Jerusalem—Babylon—captivity; Babylon—Persia/Cyrus—539 BCE—return permitted. CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link."},{"id":"ss_v20_maps","title":"TARGET 1 • Reconstruct the Maps and Migration Routes","src":"Teacher’s Ancient Israel Study Guide and class maps","link":"ancient-israel-maps.html","teach":"Build from large anchors to smaller ones. The Mediterranean Sea is west of Canaan. The Jordan River runs south from the Sea of Galilee to the Dead Sea. Jerusalem is inland west of the Dead Sea. Sinai lies between Egypt and Canaan, with the Red Sea along its southern sides. Mesopotamia is east of Canaan between the Tigris and Euphrates. Assyria was north and northeast; Babylon was farther southeast; Persia lay east of Babylon. Draw these routes: Mesopotamia toward Canaan for Abraham’s covenant journey; Canaan to Egypt during famine; Egypt across Sinai toward Canaan in the Exodus; northern Israel toward Assyrian control after 722 BCE; Judah/Jerusalem to Babylon in captivity; Babylon toward Jerusalem when Cyrus permitted return after 539 BCE.","model":"Place the Mediterranean on the left, Canaan to its east, and the Jordan system inside Canaan. Put Jerusalem west of the Dead Sea; Egypt southwest; Sinai between Egypt and Canaan; Mesopotamia east; Assyria north/northeast; Babylon southeast; Persia farther east. Add routes only after places are correct.","connect":"Label every arrow with who moved, why, and whether it was covenant journey, survival, escape, forced exile, or permitted return. Connect Reconstruct the Maps and Migration Routes through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","success":"I can draw and label both maps and explain every route.","v":[["Canaan","eastern Mediterranean region central to the Israelite story"],["Sinai Peninsula","land between Egypt and Canaan"],["migration route","path of movement from one place to another"]],"w":"Open the map aid, study it, close it, and redraw both maps. Label all required places and six routes. Add the cause and result beside every arrow. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#SS","cue":"Place → person → cause → movement → result.","task":"Use map reconstruction, timeline sorting, and the teacher’s 24 ordered recall cards. Keep the teacher’s order.","apply":"Draw the geographic anchors; explain each migration’s cause and result; rebuild all three conquest-and-return rows from memory.","delayed":"Tomorrow, redraw the maps and explain the conquest sequence backward as well as forward."}},{"id":"ss_v20_people_texts","title":"TARGETS 2–11 • Jerusalem, Abraham, Moses, Sacred Texts and Beliefs","src":"Teacher’s Ancient Israel Study Guide and class maps","teach":"Jerusalem is the geographic thread: David’s capital, Solomon’s Temple city, part of Judah, conquered by Babylon, and the destination of Cyrus-permitted return. Abraham joins migration, covenant, and monotheism. Moses joins Exodus, Sinai, covenant law, and the Ten Commandments. Scripture means sacred writing. The Tanakh is the Hebrew collection of Scripture. The Torah is the foundational teaching and law in the first five books of the Tanakh. Covenant is a binding relationship with responsibilities, not a book. Exodus is one departure from Egypt; diaspora is the broader dispersion across places and time. Monotheism means belief in one God.","model":"Scripture is the broad category; Tanakh is the Hebrew collection; Torah is a foundational section; covenant is the binding relationship described in the tradition.","connect":"Connect Abraham to covenant and migration; Moses to Exodus and law; Jerusalem to monarchy, Temple, conquest, captivity, and return. Connect Jerusalem, Abraham, Moses, Sacred Texts and Beliefs through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","success":"I can define and relate every term, then explain Abraham and Moses in complete significance statements.","v":[["Scripture","sacred writing"],["Tanakh","the Hebrew Bible"],["Torah","foundational teaching and law within the Tanakh"],["covenant","binding relationship with responsibilities"],["Exodus","departure from Egypt under Moses"],["diaspora","dispersion beyond the homeland"]],"w":"Write one connected paragraph using Jerusalem, Abraham, Moses, Scripture, Tanakh, Torah, covenant, Exodus, Ten Commandments, monotheism, and diaspora accurately. CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#SS","cue":"Place → person → cause → movement → result.","task":"Use map reconstruction, timeline sorting, and the teacher’s 24 ordered recall cards. Keep the teacher’s order.","apply":"Draw the geographic anchors; explain each migration’s cause and result; rebuild all three conquest-and-return rows from memory.","delayed":"Tomorrow, redraw the maps and explain the conquest sequence backward as well as forward."}},{"id":"ss_v20_migrations","title":"TARGET 10 and TARGET 16 • Migrations, Exodus and Diaspora","src":"Teacher’s Ancient Israel Study Guide and class maps","teach":"Hebrew migration is not one trip. Abraham’s household moves from Mesopotamia toward Canaan in connection with covenant. Jacob’s family moves from Canaan to Egypt during famine. The Exodus is escape from slavery in Egypt under Moses. Assyria’s conquest of northern Israel in 722 BCE and Babylon’s conquest of Judah and Jerusalem cause forced movement. Diaspora is the broader dispersion; Babylonian Captivity is one forced-exile episode. After Persia conquers Babylon in 539 BCE, Cyrus permits exiles to return, though permission does not mean every person returned.","model":"Classify cause: Abraham—covenant; Egypt—famine; Exodus—freedom; Assyria/Babylon—forced exile; Cyrus—permitted return.","connect":"Compare voluntary journey, survival migration, liberation, forced exile, dispersion, and permitted return. Connect Migrations, Exodus and Diaspora through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","success":"I can sequence every movement and explain start, destination, cause, choice or force, and consequence.","v":[["famine","severe food shortage"],["exile","forced removal from a homeland"],["captivity","forced exile under a conqueror"],["return","movement back to a homeland"]],"w":"Create a table with movement, start/destination, cause, chosen or forced, and consequence. Include every route. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#SS","cue":"Place → person → cause → movement → result.","task":"Use map reconstruction, timeline sorting, and the teacher’s 24 ordered recall cards. Keep the teacher’s order.","apply":"Draw the geographic anchors; explain each migration’s cause and result; rebuild all three conquest-and-return rows from memory.","delayed":"Tomorrow, redraw the maps and explain the conquest sequence backward as well as forward."}},{"id":"ss_v20_kings","title":"TARGETS 12–14 • Saul, David and Solomon","src":"Teacher’s Ancient Israel Study Guide and class maps","teach":"Saul was the first king, a military leader, and helped unite the tribes. David followed Saul, united the kingdom, strengthened government and army, and made Jerusalem the capital. Solomon followed David, was known for wisdom and Proverbs, expanded wealth and trade, and built the first Temple in Jerusalem. Solomon’s death was followed by the breakup of the united kingdom.","model":"Person → action → effect → importance. David made Jerusalem the capital, creating a political and religious center that later links Judah, Babylonian conquest, captivity, and return.","connect":"Keep the anchors exact: Saul begins the monarchy; David unites and centers it; Solomon builds the Temple and is followed by the split. Connect Saul, David and Solomon through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","success":"I can explain each king’s importance and place them in order.","v":[["monarchy","government led by a king or queen"],["united kingdom","kingdom before division after Solomon"],["Temple","central place of worship built in Jerusalem under Solomon"]],"w":"Write three significance responses using person → action → effect → importance. Add the change that followed Solomon. CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#SS","cue":"Place → person → cause → movement → result.","task":"Use map reconstruction, timeline sorting, and the teacher’s 24 ordered recall cards. Keep the teacher’s order.","apply":"Draw the geographic anchors; explain each migration’s cause and result; rebuild all three conquest-and-return rows from memory.","delayed":"Tomorrow, redraw the maps and explain the conquest sequence backward as well as forward."}},{"id":"ss_v20_kingdom_cyrus","title":"TARGETS 17–21 • Divided Kingdom, Captivity and Cyrus","src":"Teacher’s Ancient Israel Study Guide and class maps","teach":"After Solomon, the kingdom divided. Remember the labels: Israel northern kingdom; Judah southern kingdom, with Jerusalem in Judah. Memorize and explain the repair sequence: north Israel—Assyria—722 BCE; south Judah/Jerusalem—Babylon—captivity; Babylon—Persia/Cyrus—539 BCE—return permitted. Babylonian Captivity followed Babylon’s conquest of Judah; it is not the Exodus and not the entire diaspora. Cyrus came later: he conquered Babylon and permitted Jewish exiles to return to Jerusalem and rebuild the Temple.","model":"Row 1: northern Israel | Assyria | 722 BCE | conquest and displacement. Row 2: southern Judah/Jerusalem | Babylon | captivity and Temple destruction. Row 3: Babylon | Persia/Cyrus | 539 BCE | return permitted.","connect":"Jerusalem links Rows 2 and 3: the Temple city in Judah was conquered by Babylon and became the destination named in Cyrus’s return policy. Connect Divided Kingdom, Captivity and Cyrus through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","success":"I can reconstruct all three rows in both directions and explain cause and effect.","v":[["Israel","northern kingdom"],["Judah","southern kingdom containing Jerusalem"],["Babylonian Captivity","forced exile after Babylon conquered Judah"],["Cyrus the Great","Persian king who conquered Babylon and permitted return"]],"w":"Reproduce the three-row sequence exactly. Turn each row into a cause-and-effect sentence and explain how Jerusalem links Rows 2 and 3. CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link.","learningTools":{"url":"learning-tools.html#SS","cue":"Place → person → cause → movement → result.","task":"Use map reconstruction, timeline sorting, and the teacher’s 24 ordered recall cards. Keep the teacher’s order.","apply":"Draw the geographic anchors; explain each migration’s cause and result; rebuild all three conquest-and-return rows from memory.","delayed":"Tomorrow, redraw the maps and explain the conquest sequence backward as well as forward."}},{"id":"ss_v19_map","title":"CURRENT SOURCE PATH 1 • Teacher Canaan Map Mastery","src":"Teacher uploads and 10/2 newsletter • Canaan teacher map/key • OER Ancient Israel reference","teach":"Locate Mediterranean Sea, Canaan, Phoenicia, Israel, Judah, Jordan River, Sea of Galilee, Dead Sea, Jerusalem, Sinai Desert/Peninsula, and Red Sea. Learn relative relationships: Mediterranean west; Phoenicia north/northwest; Sea of Galilee north of Dead Sea; Jordan connects the lake region southward; Jerusalem west of the Dead Sea; Sinai and Red Sea south/southwest. Map evidence supports location and movement claims, not private beliefs.","model":"Cover labels and rebuild the map from anchors: coast→Mediterranean; inland water chain→Sea of Galilee, Jordan, Dead Sea; city→Jerusalem west of Dead Sea; southern bridge→Sinai toward Red Sea. Then reverse every relation.","connect":"Connect maps to coordinate reasoning: both encode location, but geographic maps are not classroom ordered-pair planes. Connect Teacher Canaan Map Mastery through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","success":"I can reconstruct and explain the teacher map without a word bank.","v":[["Canaan","ancient eastern Mediterranean region"],["Phoenicia","coastal region north of ancient Israel"],["Judah","southern Hebrew kingdom after division"],["relative location","position compared with another place"]],"w":"Draw and label the full teacher map from memory, then write six two-way relative-location statements and one geographic inference with a limitation. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence."},{"id":"ss_v19_vocab","title":"CURRENT SOURCE PATH 2 • Ancient Israel Vocabulary Network","src":"Teacher uploads and 10/2 newsletter • Ancient Israel Vocabulary pages","teach":"Hebrew people/Israelites are terms used for the ancient people in this unit; Jew becomes a historical/religious identity term connected to Judah and Judaism. Judaism is the religion; monotheism means belief in one God. The Tanakh is the Hebrew Bible collection; the Torah is its foundational teaching/law section. Diaspora means dispersion of a people from a homeland. Captivity is forced detention/exile. Use terms precisely rather than as interchangeable labels.","model":"Build categories: PEOPLE—Hebrews/Israelites/Jews; BELIEF—Judaism/monotheism; TEXT—Tanakh/Torah; MOVEMENT/POWER—migration/diaspora/captivity. Then explain the historical relationship within each category.","connect":"Connect vocabulary categories to ELA context clues and Science term precision. Connect Ancient Israel Vocabulary Network through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","success":"I can define, distinguish, and connect the teacher's terms in historical context.","v":[["Israelite","member of the ancient people of Israel"],["Judaism","religion of the Jewish people"],["Tanakh","collection of Hebrew sacred scriptures"],["diaspora","dispersion of a people from a homeland"]],"w":"Make a concept map with every teacher vocabulary term, category, definition, example, and one non-example. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence."},{"id":"ss_v19_patriarchs","title":"CURRENT SOURCE PATH 3 • Abraham, Moses, Exodus & Law","src":"Teacher uploads and 10/2 newsletter • Ancient Israel Walk of Fame • Show What You Know","teach":"In the traditions studied, Abraham is associated with migration from Mesopotamia toward Canaan and covenant. Moses is associated with leading the Exodus from Egypt, receiving the Ten Commandments at Mount Sinai, and covenant/law. Exodus means departure; the Ten Commandments are moral/religious laws. A historical classroom answer should identify when a claim comes from religious tradition and use geography to explain routes without pretending a map proves motives.","model":"Sequence: Abraham tradition and Canaan promise/covenant→Israelites in Egypt→Moses and Exodus→Mount Sinai and Ten Commandments→movement toward Canaan. This is a learning sequence, not permission to invent exact dates or map-proven feelings.","connect":"Connect chronology to narrative sequence and cause/effect while distinguishing source types. Connect Abraham, Moses, Exodus & Law to source type and point of view. Laws, artifacts, maps, and religious or historical texts answer different questions. Identify the source, make a limited claim, and explain what additional evidence would be needed.","success":"I can sequence the patriarch/Exodus/law material and explain the evidence type.","v":[["covenant","solemn relationship/agreement with responsibilities"],["Exodus","departure of Israelites from Egypt in the biblical tradition"],["Ten Commandments","central moral and religious laws in the tradition"],["Mount Sinai","place associated with Moses receiving the commandments"]],"w":"Create an annotated route/timeline for Abraham and Moses. Label tradition, geography evidence, covenant, Exodus, Sinai, and law without merging them. CONNECTION: Make one claim using an appropriate source type, explain what the source supports, and name one conclusion it cannot establish alone."},{"id":"ss_v19_saul","title":"CURRENT SOURCE PATH 4 • Judges, Tribes & King Saul","src":"Teacher uploads and 10/2 newsletter • Ancient Israel Walk of Fame: King Saul","teach":"Before monarchy, Israelite tribes were led at times by judges. The teacher source presents Saul as the first king, around 1050 BCE, chosen/anointed in the tradition, a strong military leader who united scattered tribes, and initially well loved, courageous, and generous. His significance is political unification and the transition toward monarchy; do not confuse him with later kings.","model":"Problem/response frame: scattered tribes and external threats→desire for centralized leadership→Saul's monarchy and military unification. Evaluate both gain (coordination) and risk (concentrated power) rather than memorizing 'first king' alone.","connect":"Connect political unification to Mesopotamian city-state/empire changes without treating them as identical. Connect Judges, Tribes & King Saul to source type and point of view. Laws, artifacts, maps, and religious or historical texts answer different questions. Identify the source, make a limited claim, and explain what additional evidence would be needed.","success":"I can identify Saul and analyze the transition to monarchy.","v":[["judge","leader in the pre-monarchy Israelite period"],["monarchy","government led by a king or queen"],["anoint","ceremonially designate for a role"],["unification","bringing separate groups under shared leadership"]],"w":"Explain why Saul is historically significant using the problem/response frame, two source details, and one benefit/risk of centralized monarchy. CONNECTION: Make one claim using an appropriate source type, explain what the source supports, and name one conclusion it cannot establish alone."},{"id":"ss_v19_david","title":"CURRENT SOURCE PATH 5 • King David: Jerusalem, Unity & Tradition","src":"Teacher uploads and 10/2 newsletter • Ancient Israel Walk of Fame: King David","teach":"The teacher materials connect David with the David-and-Goliath tradition, musical skill and songs/psalms, friendship with Saul's son Jonathan, rule around 1000 BCE, establishment of Jerusalem as capital/holy city, and a united kingdom with a strong army and central government. Separate biography/tradition details from his larger political significance.","model":"Evidence chain: choosing Jerusalem as a capital creates a central political and religious place→central government and army support unity→the kingdom's organization grows. The Goliath story illustrates the remembered young-warrior tradition, but it is not the sole reason David matters historically.","connect":"Connect capital-city analysis to geography: location can support authority and identity but does not force outcomes. Connect King David: Jerusalem, Unity & Tradition to source type and point of view. Laws, artifacts, maps, and religious or historical texts answer different questions. Identify the source, make a limited claim, and explain what additional evidence would be needed.","success":"I can distinguish David's stories, relationships, and state-building role.","v":[["capital","central city of government"],["central government","authority organized over a larger territory"],["psalm","sacred song or poem"],["Goliath","Philistine warrior in the David tradition"]],"w":"Compare David's remembered traditions with his political significance. Use Jerusalem, unity, government, Goliath, and one cultural detail. CONNECTION: Make one claim using an appropriate source type, explain what the source supports, and name one conclusion it cannot establish alone."},{"id":"ss_v19_solomon","title":"CURRENT SOURCE PATH 6 • King Solomon: Temple, Wisdom & Strain","src":"Teacher uploads and 10/2 newsletter • Ancient Israel Walk of Fame: King Solomon","teach":"The teacher materials present Solomon as David and Bathsheba's son, Israel's third king (about 965 BCE in the slide sequence), known for wisdom and Proverbs, builder of the first temple in Jerusalem, and ruler for about forty years. Major building and royal projects can strengthen a state and shared center while also requiring labor and resources. The kingdom divided after his reign, so evaluate achievement and strain together.","model":"Cause/effect frame: centralized wealth/labor→temple and building projects→Jerusalem's religious/political importance grows; demands and tensions contribute to instability→after Solomon, division into Israel in the north and Judah in the south.","connect":"Connect building-project tradeoffs to Science resource decisions: benefits, costs, and long-term consequences coexist. Connect King Solomon: Temple, Wisdom & Strain to source type and point of view. Laws, artifacts, maps, and religious or historical texts answer different questions. Identify the source, make a limited claim, and explain what additional evidence would be needed.","success":"I can explain Solomon's cultural, religious, political, and economic significance.","v":[["temple","sacred building; Solomon is associated with the first Jerusalem temple"],["Proverbs","collection of wisdom sayings associated with Solomon in the tradition"],["Israel","northern kingdom after division"],["Judah","southern kingdom after division"]],"w":"Evaluate Solomon's reign with a two-column evidence chart: achievements and costs/strains. End with the division into Israel and Judah. CONNECTION: Make one claim using an appropriate source type, explain what the source supports, and name one conclusion it cannot establish alone."},{"id":"ss_v19_conquest","title":"CURRENT SOURCE PATH 7 • Division, Assyria, Babylon & Diaspora","src":"Teacher uploads and 10/2 newsletter • Show What You Know levels 4–5","teach":"After the kingdom divided, Israel was in the north and Judah in the south. The Assyrian Empire conquered the northern kingdom of Israel in 722 BCE. Later, the Chaldean/Neo-Babylonian Empire conquered Judah and Jerusalem, destroyed the first temple, and forced many into Babylonian Captivity (commonly dated 586 BCE for Jerusalem's destruction). Diaspora is the broader dispersion of Jewish communities; captivity is one forced episode. Keep empire, kingdom, date, and consequence paired correctly.","model":"Timeline: united monarchy→division→Israel/north conquered by Assyria, 722 BCE→Judah/south conquered by Chaldeans/Babylonians, Jerusalem/temple destroyed, 586 BCE→captivity and wider diaspora. Use north/south anchors to prevent swapping the events.","connect":"Connect timeline cause/effect to ELA structure and distinguish sequence from proof of causation. Connect Division, Assyria, Babylon & Diaspora to source type and point of view. Laws, artifacts, maps, and religious or historical texts answer different questions. Identify the source, make a limited claim, and explain what additional evidence would be needed.","success":"I can accurately pair every conquest, kingdom, date, and consequence.","v":[["Assyrians","empire that conquered northern Israel in 722 BCE"],["Chaldeans","Neo-Babylonians who conquered Judah/Jerusalem"],["Babylonian Captivity","forced exile of many Judeans to Babylon"],["diaspora","dispersion of Jewish communities beyond the homeland"]],"w":"Build the full post-Solomon timeline with map direction, empire, kingdom, date, event, and consequence. Then explain captivity versus diaspora. CONNECTION: Make one claim using an appropriate source type, explain what the source supports, and name one conclusion it cannot establish alone."},{"id":"ss_v19_practice","title":"CURRENT SOURCE PATH 8 • Torah, Tanakh, Rabbis, Sabbath & Cultural Continuity","src":"Teacher uploads and 10/2 newsletter • Ancient Israel Vocabulary • Show What You Know","teach":"The Torah contains foundational teachings/laws and is part of the Tanakh. Rabbis are Jewish teachers/scholars. The Sabbath is a weekly day of rest and worship. Proverbs are wisdom sayings, traditionally associated in this unit with Solomon. These texts, teachers, laws, and practices helped communities preserve identity and traditions across political change, captivity, and diaspora. Explain continuity through mechanisms, not by listing terms.","model":"Continuity chain: sacred teachings and laws are preserved→teachers interpret and teach them→weekly practices reinforce community memory→communities can maintain identity even when political control or location changes. This is a historical explanation of cultural continuity.","connect":"Connect cultural transmission to ELA theme and source evidence: ideas persist through texts, teaching, and repeated practice. Connect Torah, Tanakh, Rabbis, Sabbath & Cultural Continuity to source type and point of view. Laws, artifacts, maps, and religious or historical texts answer different questions. Identify the source, make a limited claim, and explain what additional evidence would be needed.","success":"I can connect religious/cultural terms to historical continuity rather than memorize isolated definitions.","v":[["rabbi","Jewish teacher or scholar"],["Sabbath","weekly day of rest and worship"],["Torah","foundational Jewish teaching/law and part of Tanakh"],["Proverbs","wisdom sayings associated with Solomon in the tradition"]],"w":"Explain how Torah/Tanakh, rabbis, Sabbath, Proverbs, and memory of leaders could support continuity during captivity and diaspora. Use a cause/effect chain. CONNECTION: Make one claim using an appropriate source type, explain what the source supports, and name one conclusion it cannot establish alone."},{"id":"ss_oct2_canaan_quiz","title":"CURRENT • Ancient Canaan Geography Quiz Path","src":"10/2 teacher newsletter + 9/25 Ancient Israel/Canaan launch + TN Grade 6 Ancient Israel geography alignment","teach":"Ancient Canaan/Israel lay along the eastern Mediterranean, connecting northeastern Africa and Southwest Asia. Know relationships, not only a memorized list: Mediterranean Sea west; Jordan River flows toward the Dead Sea; Jerusalem inland west of the Dead Sea; Sinai Peninsula between the Mediterranean and Red seas; Red Sea south. Geography creates opportunities and constraints for settlement, movement, exchange, defense, and cultural contact.","model":"Reason from relationships: If the Mediterranean is west of Canaan, then Canaan is east of the Mediterranean. If the Jordan flows toward the Dead Sea, a settlement near the river may gain freshwater access and a travel corridor—but that does not prove every historical claim about the settlement.","connect":"Connect to Mesopotamia: both regions used water and routes, but their exact rivers, seas, landforms, and settlement patterns differ. Connect Ancient Canaan Geography Quiz Path through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","success":"I can locate every required feature in both directions and explain one evidence-based human effect without claiming geography determines everything.","v":[["Canaan","an ancient region along the eastern Mediterranean associated with ancient Israel"],["relative location","where a place is compared with another place"],["corridor","a route or zone that supports movement between regions"]],"w":"Without notes, describe Canaan's location using the Mediterranean Sea, Jordan River, Dead Sea, Red Sea, Sinai Peninsula, and Jerusalem. Then explain one opportunity and one constraint created by geography. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence."},{"id":"ss_oct2_migration_judaism","title":"CURRENT • Migration, Canaan & Origins of Early Judaism","src":"10/2 teacher newsletter + classroom Ancient Israel/Canaan scope; teacher-introduced depth only","teach":"Human migration is movement of people from one place to another. Geography can shape routes, stops, risks, resources, and contact, but it does not by itself explain beliefs. In the tradition studied in Ancient Israel history, Abraham is connected with a journey from Mesopotamia toward Canaan and with a covenant relationship with one God. Treat religious traditions respectfully and distinguish what a map can show from what a historical or religious text claims.","model":"A map may support: a route connects Mesopotamia and Canaan through a broader land corridor. A map cannot by itself prove: why a person believed the journey should occur. That requires another source. Use geography for location evidence and texts for beliefs/motives.","connect":"Connect migration to ELA point of view and evidence: different source types can describe the same movement while answering different questions. Connect Migration, Canaan & Origins of Early Judaism through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","success":"I can explain how geography influenced a migration route and distinguish map evidence from a belief or tradition reported in another source.","v":[["migration","movement of people from one place to another"],["covenant","a solemn agreement; in early Judaism, a central relationship between God and the people"],["monotheism","belief in one God"],["source type","the kind of evidence, such as a map, artifact, law, or religious text"]],"w":"Explain a possible Mesopotamia-to-Canaan migration using geographic evidence. Then name one claim a map supports and one claim that requires a different source. Include migration, route, Canaan, and covenant accurately. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence."},{"id":"ss_oct2_judaism_core","title":"CURRENT • Early Judaism: Core Ideas & Evidence","src":"10/2 teacher newsletter origins-of-early-Judaism notice + Tennessee Grade 6 Ancient Israel sequence; teacher-introduced depth","teach":"Early Judaism developed among the ancient Israelites. Core ideas commonly introduced in this unit include monotheism, covenant, moral/ethical responsibilities, and sacred traditions later preserved in the Torah. Sequence matters: distinguish an early origin idea from later events and leaders. Use respectful language and identify whether a statement comes from religious tradition, historical study, or material evidence.","model":"Claim: covenant is important to early Judaism. Evidence type: religious tradition/text describes a binding relationship and responsibilities. Reasoning: the idea helps explain identity and moral obligations. This is different from saying an artifact alone proves every theological claim.","connect":"Connect to Mesopotamian religion carefully: Mesopotamian classroom sources emphasize polytheism; early Judaism emphasizes monotheism. Compare beliefs without ranking people's worth or flattening either tradition. Connect Early Judaism: Core Ideas & Evidence to source type and point of view. Laws, artifacts, maps, and religious or historical texts answer different questions. Identify the source, make a limited claim, and explain what additional evidence would be needed.","success":"I can define the core ideas, compare monotheism and polytheism respectfully, sequence teacher-introduced origins, and match claims to appropriate evidence.","v":[["Torah","sacred teaching/text central to Judaism"],["ethics","principles about right conduct"],["tradition","beliefs or practices passed through generations"],["polytheism","belief in many gods"]],"w":"Compare one religious idea from Mesopotamia with one from early Judaism. Define both accurately, use respectful language, identify the source type for each claim, and explain one similarity or difference without overgeneralizing. CONNECTION: Make one claim using an appropriate source type, explain what the source supports, and name one conclusion it cannot establish alone."},{"id":"ss_wk0928_israel_map","title":"THIS WEEK • Ancient Israel/Canaan Geography","src":"9/25 teacher newsletter + current TN Grade 6 Ancient Israel geography standards","teach":"This week begins Ancient Israel (Canaan) with geography. Be able to locate and reason about the Mediterranean Sea, Jordan River, Dead Sea, Red Sea, Sinai Peninsula, Jerusalem, and the broader position of Canaan/ancient Israel between larger regions. Geography is not just map labeling: water, terrain, routes, and neighboring regions influenced settlement, agriculture, movement, trade, defense, and culture.","connect":"Connect Ancient Israel/Canaan Geography through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","v":[["Canaan","ancient region along the eastern Mediterranean associated with the development of ancient Israel"],["Sinai Peninsula","land bridge region between northeastern Africa and southwestern Asia"],["Jordan River","major river flowing through the region toward the Dead Sea"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence."},{"id":"ss_wk0928_geo_culture","title":"THIS WEEK • How Geography Shaped Civilization & Culture","src":"9/25 teacher newsletter + TN Grade 6 Ancient Israel geography/history alignment","teach":"Use cause-and-effect reasoning. Canaan's position along eastern Mediterranean routes encouraged contact, trade, migration, and conflict. Hills, valleys, plains, deserts, and water availability affected settlement and agriculture. A TCAP-style question may give a map or short source and ask what conclusion is best supported; use only what the evidence supports.","connect":"Connect How Geography Shaped Civilization & Culture through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","v":[["strategic location","a place important because of its position relative to routes, resources, or other regions"],["migration","movement of people from one place to another"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence."},{"id":"ss_geo","title":"Geography & Fertile Crescent","src":"Classroom + Michigan Open Book/LearnBPS Ch. 3","teach":"Mesopotamia developed around the Tigris and Euphrates in the Fertile Crescent. Rivers supported farming, drinking water, transportation, and trade. The same accessible geography could create challenges such as flooding and vulnerability to attack.","connect":"Connect Geography & Fertile Crescent through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","v":[["Fertile Crescent","a curved region of relatively fertile land in Southwest Asia"],["Mesopotamia","land between the rivers; the region associated with the Tigris and Euphrates"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence."},{"id":"ss_map","title":"Map Mission","src":"Uploaded classroom map assignment","teach":"Know: Mediterranean Sea, Red Sea, Persian Gulf; Tigris and Euphrates; Sumer, Akkad, Assyria, Babylonia; Ur, Nineveh, Babylon; Fertile Crescent, Syrian Desert, Zagros Mountains.","connect":"Connect Map Mission through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","v":[],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence."},{"id":"ss_silt","title":"Silt • Irrigation • Levees","src":"From Nomads to Farmers + LearnBPS geography","teach":"Silt is fine soil/mineral material carried by water and deposited when water slows or recedes; it can help make river-valley soil fertile. Irrigation is a human-made system for moving water to crops. A levee is a raised bank/barrier used to help control water.","connect":"Connect Irrigation • Levees through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","v":[["silt","fine soil and mineral particles carried and deposited by water"],["irrigation","a system for directing water to crops"],["levee","a raised bank or barrier used to help contain/control water"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence."},{"id":"ss_farm","title":"Nomads to Farmers & Stable Food","src":"From Nomads to Farmers + LearnBPS","teach":"Reliable agriculture supported permanent settlements. A stable food supply meant fewer people had to spend all their time producing food, allowing specialization and larger, more complex communities.","connect":"Connect Nomads to Farmers & Stable Food through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","v":[["surplus","more of something than is immediately needed"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence."},{"id":"ss_seven","title":"Seven Characteristics of Civilization","src":"Michigan Open Book/LearnBPS + classroom mnemonic","teach":"Cow Girls Wear Short Skirts To Run = Cities; Government & Law; Writing; Specialization; Social System; Complex Technology; Organized Religion. Riley must recognize examples, not just recite the list.","connect":"Connect Seven Characteristics of Civilization through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","v":[["civilization","a complex society with organized features such as cities, government, writing, specialization, social systems, technology, and religion"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link."},{"id":"ss_city","title":"City-States • Government • Law","src":"LearnBPS Government and Law + classroom work","teach":"A city-state is an independent city plus surrounding territory with its own government. As cities grew, government and law helped organize resources, disputes, infrastructure, behavior, and defense.","connect":"Connect Government • Law to source type and point of view. Laws, artifacts, maps, and religious or historical texts answer different questions. Identify the source, make a limited claim, and explain what additional evidence would be needed.","v":[["city-state","an independent city and surrounding territory with its own government"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Make one claim using an appropriate source type, explain what the source supports, and name one conclusion it cannot establish alone."},{"id":"ss_theocracy","title":"Theocracy & Ziggurats","src":"LearnBPS Government and Law","teach":"A theocracy is government by divine authority or religious leadership. Early Sumerian city-states connected temple and government functions. A ziggurat is a large stepped temple structure associated with the city and its patron deity.","connect":"Connect Theocracy & Ziggurats to source type and point of view. Laws, artifacts, maps, and religious or historical texts answer different questions. Identify the source, make a limited claim, and explain what additional evidence would be needed.","v":[["theocracy","government by divine authority or religious leadership"],["ziggurat","a large stepped temple structure"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Make one claim using an appropriate source type, explain what the source supports, and name one conclusion it cannot establish alone."},{"id":"ss_write","title":"Cuneiform & Stylus","src":"Cuneiform classroom material + LearnBPS","teach":"Cuneiform used wedge-shaped marks pressed into clay. A stylus was the writing tool. Writing helped record goods and transactions, administer cities, communicate, and preserve laws.","connect":"Connect Cuneiform & Stylus to source type and point of view. Laws, artifacts, maps, and religious or historical texts answer different questions. Identify the source, make a limited claim, and explain what additional evidence would be needed.","v":[["cuneiform","a Mesopotamian writing system using wedge-shaped marks"],["stylus","a tool used to press/write marks in clay"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Make one claim using an appropriate source type, explain what the source supports, and name one conclusion it cannot establish alone."},{"id":"ss_social","title":"Specialization vs Social System","src":"LearnBPS Social Systems + classroom","teach":"Specialization = what work people do. Social system = how groups/jobs are organized and ranked by status. Top levels included priests, kings, landowners, officials; middle levels merchants, artisans, farmers, fishers; enslaved people were at the bottom.","connect":"Connect Specialization vs Social System through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","v":[["specialization","people focusing on particular kinds of work"],["social system","the organization of groups into social levels/status"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link."},{"id":"ss_artisan","title":"Artisans & Merchants","src":"LearnBPS Social Systems","teach":"An artisan is a skilled craftsperson who makes useful or decorative goods. A merchant buys, sells, or trades goods/services. Both are examples of specialized work and appear in the social structure.","connect":"Connect Artisans & Merchants through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","v":[["artisan","a skilled craftsperson"],["merchant","a person who buys, sells, or trades goods/services"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link."},{"id":"ss_tech","title":"Technology & Aqueducts","src":"Classroom + Michigan Open Book","teach":"Ancient complex technology includes irrigation, levees, plows, wheels, building methods, and aqueducts. An aqueduct is a human-built structure/system for carrying water from one place to another.","connect":"Connect Technology & Aqueducts through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","v":[["aqueduct","a human-built structure/system for carrying water"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link."},{"id":"ss_religion","title":"Polytheism & Gods/Goddesses","src":"Uploaded gods/goddesses sheet + LearnBPS","teach":"Polytheistic means belief in multiple gods. Classroom names include An/Anu, Enlil, Utu/Shamash, Nanna/Sin, Inanna/Ishtar, and Enki/Ea. The class sheet asks what people believed the gods controlled.","connect":"Connect Polytheism & Gods/Goddesses to source type and point of view. Laws, artifacts, maps, and religious or historical texts answer different questions. Identify the source, make a limited claim, and explain what additional evidence would be needed.","v":[["polytheistic","believing in multiple gods"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Make one claim using an appropriate source type, explain what the source supports, and name one conclusion it cannot establish alone."},{"id":"ss_seals","title":"Cylinder Seals","src":"Kids Discover classroom material","teach":"Cylinder seals were carved cylinders rolled over clay to leave an impression. They could mark goods or documents and connect to identity, ownership, authentication, and recordkeeping.","connect":"Connect Cylinder Seals to source type and point of view. Laws, artifacts, maps, and religious or historical texts answer different questions. Identify the source, make a limited claim, and explain what additional evidence would be needed.","v":[["cylinder seal","a carved cylinder rolled over clay to leave an identifying impression"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Make one claim using an appropriate source type, explain what the source supports, and name one conclusion it cannot establish alone."},{"id":"ss_akkad","title":"Akkadian • Sargon","src":"Uploaded empire carousel + LearnBPS early empires","teach":"Classroom carousel: 2334–2193 BCE; Sargon; capital Agade; conquered Sumerian city-states; strong military; cuneiform continued; Akkadian became prominent. The slide calls it the world’s first empire—remember that as classroom wording.","connect":"Connect Sargon through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","v":[["empire","a group of territories/peoples controlled by one ruling power"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link."},{"id":"ss_bab","title":"Old Babylonian • Hammurabi","src":"Uploaded empire carousel + LearnBPS Babylonian","teach":"Classroom carousel: 1900–1600 BCE; Hammurabi; capital Babylon; written code/system of laws; roads, agriculture, trade, and economy. Broad “fairness” wording is source framing, not an absolute historical judgment.","connect":"Connect Hammurabi through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","v":[],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link."},{"id":"ss_assyria","title":"Assyrian • Ashurbanipal","src":"Uploaded empire carousel + Michigan Open Book","teach":"Classroom carousel: 934–612 BCE; Ashurbanipal; capital Nineveh; iron weapons, horses, siege warfare; elaborate palaces; major cuneiform tablet library.","connect":"Connect Ashurbanipal through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","v":[["siege warfare","military methods used to surround/attack fortified places"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link."},{"id":"ss_neo","title":"Neo-Babylonian • Nebuchadnezzar II","src":"Uploaded empire carousel","teach":"Classroom carousel: 626–539 BCE; Nebuchadnezzar II; capital Babylon; military expansion; mathematics/astronomy. The classroom slide attributes the Hanging Gardens and “first sundial”; remember those as classroom-slide claims where historically contested.","connect":"Connect Nebuchadnezzar II through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","v":[],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link."},{"id":"ss_persia","title":"Persian • Cyrus & Successors","src":"Uploaded empire carousel","teach":"Classroom carousel: 539–331 BCE; Cyrus the Great, later Cambyses/Darius; provinces/local governance, permanent army/central control, common currency, roads, trade. Classroom religion wording is simplified and should remain labeled as classroom wording.","connect":"Connect Cyrus & Successors through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","v":[["province","an administrative region within a larger state/empire"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link."},{"id":"ss_phoen","title":"Phoenicians • Traders, Not an Empire","src":"Uploaded empire carousel","teach":"Classroom range: 1150–800 BCE. Phoenicians are Mediterranean traders and expert navigators living in wealthy city-states. The classroom material emphasizes an influential alphabet and navigation using the North Star. They are not another empire in the Mesopotamian empire chain.","connect":"Connect Traders, Not an Empire through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","v":[],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link."},{"id":"ss_chrono","title":"Chronology & Comparison","src":"Classroom empire carousel","teach":"Core sequence: Sumerian city-states → Akkadian → Old Babylonian → Assyrian → Neo-Babylonian → Persian. Phoenicians overlap historically but belong in a different comparison category.","connect":"Connect Chronology & Comparison through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","v":[["chronology","the order of events in time"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link."},{"id":"ss_synth","title":"Environment ↔ People Synthesis","src":"Michigan Open Book/LearnBPS chapter inquiry + classroom","teach":"The chapter asks both directions. Environment shaped people through rivers, dry climate, fertile deposits, building-material limits, and accessibility. People changed environment through irrigation, levees, farming, cities, mud-brick construction, and water technology.","connect":"Connect Environment ↔ People Synthesis through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","v":[],"w":"Use at least four specific Mesopotamian examples to answer: Which had the greater impact—environment on people, or people on environment? Address both directions before making your conclusion. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence."},{"id":"ss_sg_01_10","title":"Teacher Study Guide 1–10 • Geography","src":"Teacher Mesopotamia Study Guide (items 1–10) + classroom map + verified source support","teach":"These ten teacher-guide targets are added to—not substituted for—the geography lessons already in your path. 1) The Fertile Crescent is the curved fertile region of Southwest Asia where early farming and civilizations developed. 2) Much of ancient Mesopotamia lies in modern Iraq. 3) Mesopotamia means “land between the rivers.” 4) The rivers are the Tigris and Euphrates. 5) They flow toward the Persian Gulf. 6) Living near rivers provided water for people and crops, fertile deposits, irrigation opportunities, and transportation/trade. 7) Major Mesopotamian cities were developing by about 3500 BCE. 8) Mesopotamia is in the eastern part of the Fertile Crescent. 9) Silt is fine material deposited by water that can enrich soil. 10) Two classroom-friendly nicknames to remember are “Land Between Two Rivers” and “Cradle of Civilization.”","connect":"Connect Geography through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","v":[["Fertile Crescent","a curved region of fertile land in Southwest Asia"],["Iraq","the modern country containing much of ancient Mesopotamia"],["Mesopotamia","a name meaning land between the rivers"],["Tigris River","one of Mesopotamia’s two major rivers"],["Euphrates River","one of Mesopotamia’s two major rivers"],["Persian Gulf","body of water southeast of Mesopotamia"],["silt","fine soil/mineral material deposited by moving water"],["Cradle of Civilization","a nickname emphasizing Mesopotamia’s importance to early urban civilization"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence."},{"id":"ss_sg_11_20","title":"Teacher Study Guide 11–20 • Sumer, Writing & Religion","src":"Teacher Mesopotamia Study Guide (items 11–20) + classroom cuneiform/religion materials + verified source support","teach":"These targets connect Sumer to writing, building, and religion. 11) Sumer was an early civilization in southern Mesopotamia. 12) Clay tablets were durable writing surfaces. 13) Irrigation deliberately moved water to fields using systems such as canals and ditches. 14) Mud/clay bricks were major building materials where timber and stone were limited. 15) Pictographs are picture-based symbols. 16) Cuneiform developed into wedge-shaped writing. 17) A stylus was pressed into clay to make marks. 18) Early writing was especially important for recording and storing economic information such as goods and distributions. 19) Ziggurats were massive stepped temple platforms/complexes. 20) Priests served religious roles and held important positions in Mesopotamian communities.","connect":"Connect Sumer, Writing & Religion to source type and point of view. Laws, artifacts, maps, and religious or historical texts answer different questions. Identify the source, make a limited claim, and explain what additional evidence would be needed.","v":[["Sumer","an early civilization of southern Mesopotamia"],["clay tablet","a clay surface used for writing and records"],["irrigation","deliberately supplying water to land/crops"],["mud brick","building material made from mud or clay, often mixed and dried"],["pictograph","a picture symbol used to represent an object or idea"],["cuneiform","wedge-shaped writing impressed into clay"],["stylus","a pointed writing tool used to make marks in clay"],["ziggurat","a massive stepped temple platform/complex"],["priest","a person serving an organized religious role"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Make one claim using an appropriate source type, explain what the source supports, and name one conclusion it cannot establish alone."},{"id":"ss_sg_21_30","title":"Teacher Study Guide 21–30 • Beliefs, Inventions & Leaders","src":"Teacher Mesopotamia Study Guide (items 21–30) + gods/goddesses + empire carousel + verified source support","teach":"Now connect belief, technology, literature, and government. 21) Mesopotamian gods were associated with forces of nature and aspects of life. 22) Bronze is an alloy made primarily from copper and tin. 23) Sumerian/Mesopotamian innovations commonly taught in this unit include writing, the wheel, the plow, irrigation, bricks, mathematics/timekeeping, and other technologies. 24) A city-state is a city and surrounding territory functioning as an independent political unit. 25) Gilgamesh is the legendary king of Uruk at the center of the Epic of Gilgamesh. 26) Sargon conquered Sumerian city-states and built the Akkadian Empire. 27) An empire controls multiple territories or peoples. 28) Mesopotamian deities in Riley’s classroom source set include An/Anu, Enlil, Enki/Ea, Inanna/Ishtar, Utu/Shamash, and Nanna/Sin. 29) Hammurabi was an Old Babylonian king. 30) Polytheism means belief in many gods.","connect":"Connect Beliefs, Inventions & Leaders through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","v":[["bronze","an alloy made mainly from copper and tin"],["city-state","a city and surrounding territory acting as an independent political unit"],["Gilgamesh","legendary king of Uruk and central figure of an ancient epic"],["Sargon","Akkadian ruler who conquered Sumerian city-states"],["empire","multiple territories or peoples under one ruling power"],["polytheism","belief in many gods"],["Hammurabi","Old Babylonian king associated with a famous law collection"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link."},{"id":"ss_sg_31_40","title":"Teacher Study Guide 31–40 • Law, Eras & Civilization","src":"Teacher Mesopotamia Study Guide (items 31–40) + classroom review + verified source support","teach":"Finish the teacher guide with law and civilization. 31) The Code of Hammurabi is a famous Old Babylonian collection of laws. 32) Punishments were not equal for everyone; consequences could vary with social status and other circumstances. 33) The traditional classroom count is 282 laws. 34) Know the colored Mesopotamia map: major waters, rivers, regions, cities, Fertile Crescent, desert, and Zagros Mountains. 35) Neolithic Era: later Stone Age associated with farming, domestication, and permanent settlements. 36) Paleolithic Era: earlier Stone Age associated mainly with hunting and gathering. 37) Civilization features in Riley’s source work include stable food supply, cities, specialization, government/law, writing, social systems, complex technology, and organized religion. 38) Food surplus means producing more food than is immediately needed. 39) Metallurgy is working with metals to create useful objects/materials. 40) Job specialization/division of labor means people perform different jobs instead of everyone doing the same work.","connect":"Connect Law, Eras & Civilization to source type and point of view. Laws, artifacts, maps, and religious or historical texts answer different questions. Identify the source, make a limited claim, and explain what additional evidence would be needed.","v":[["Code of Hammurabi","a famous Old Babylonian collection of laws"],["Neolithic Era","later Stone Age associated with farming and permanent settlements"],["Paleolithic Era","earlier Stone Age associated mainly with hunting and gathering"],["food surplus","food produced beyond immediate needs"],["metallurgy","the working and processing of metals"],["job specialization","people developing particular occupations or skills"],["division of labor","work divided among people with different jobs"]],"w":"Explain the central idea of this lesson in your own words. Create and solve or analyze a fresh example. CONNECTION: Make one claim using an appropriate source type, explain what the source supports, and name one conclusion it cannot establish alone."},{"id":"ss_memory_mrnicky","title":"MEMORY BOOST • Teacher-Shared Mesopotamia Song","src":"Teacher-shared • Mr. Nicky — Ancient Mesopotamia Song","link":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fdblRch6m3g","teach":"USE THIS AS A MEMORY HOOK, NOT AS A REPLACEMENT FOR THE STUDY GUIDE. Listen once for the big sequence, then recall the anchors without the video: Tigris + Euphrates and the cradle of civilization; flooding and fertile mud; irrigation/water control; Sumerian city-states and cuneiform; Akkadians and Sargon; Babylonians and Hammurabi's code. These overlap strongly with Riley's teacher study guide. After listening, close the video and explain the chain from geography → farming → cities → government/writing.","connect":"Connect Teacher-Shared Mesopotamia Song through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","v":[],"w":"Without replaying the song, write the six memory anchors you remember and connect at least three of them in a cause-and-effect chain. CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link."},{"id":"ss_mixed_practice_2","title":"★ RANDOM MIXED PRACTICE 2 • SOCIAL STUDIES • 15 New Questions","src":"Teacher 40-item Mesopotamia Study Guide + classroom sources + teacher-shared Mr. Nicky memory aid","teach":"NEW MIXED RETRIEVAL. Questions appear in a different random order each time. The set deliberately jumps among geography, vocabulary, religion, rulers, writing, technology, and civilization so Riley must retrieve the idea rather than rely on lesson order.","connect":"Connect SOCIAL STUDIES • 15 New Questions through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","v":[],"w":"Explain how the Tigris and Euphrates helped create conditions for civilization. Use at least five of these terms: silt, irrigation, food surplus, city-state, specialization, writing, government. CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link."},{"id":"ss_v35_egypt_map14","title":"Ancient Egypt: all 14 teacher map locations","src":"Uploaded 14-location teacher checklist and Evan-Moor EMC 3706 Pocket 1 map; Harvard Digital Giza and university Egypt maps support placements. Original schematic, not to scale.","teach":"The Nile flows north: Upper Egypt is southern and upstream; Lower Egypt is northern and downstream. Reconstruct a map by locating the Mediterranean to the north, Red Sea to the east, Sinai between its northern gulfs, and Nubia to the south. Locate cities and burial sites relative to the Nile and one another. The Western Sahara wording on the teacher sheet means the desert west of the Nile here. See the numbered map activity for cities, burial sites, regions and water. A dot identifies a city; a triangle identifies a burial site. Regions occupy areas rather than single points.","model":"Begin at the Nile Delta. Move south to Memphis, then farther upstream to Thebes. Giza lies west of the Nile near modern Cairo, while the Valley of the Kings is on the west bank opposite Thebes. Alexandria is on the Mediterranean coast.","connect":"Connect river direction to elevation rather than page position. Compare Egypt and Mesopotamia by locating river-supported farming while explaining that human irrigation work is still necessary. Connect Ancient Egypt: all 14 teacher map locations through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","success":"Complete distinct practice, write the transfer, repair mistakes, then use a fresh delayed check. Only independent responses count as readiness evidence.","v":[["Upper Egypt","The southern, upstream Nile region; Thebes is in Upper Egypt."],["Lower Egypt","The northern, downstream region around the Nile Delta."],["Nile Delta","The branching river-mouth region at the Mediterranean Sea."],["Sinai Peninsula","The triangular land east of the Nile Delta, between the Gulf of Suez and Gulf of Aqaba."],["Western Sahara Desert","On this teacher map: the desert west of the Nile. Do not confuse it with the modern Western Sahara territory in northwestern Africa."],["Nubia","The region south of Egypt along the Nile, with its own societies and political traditions."],["Mediterranean Sea","The sea north of Egypt into which the Nile flows."],["Nile River","The river flowing north through Egypt to the Mediterranean Sea."],["Red Sea","The sea east of Egypt, south of the Sinai Peninsula."],["Memphis","An ancient capital near the southern edge of the Nile Delta, south of modern Cairo."],["Thebes","An ancient city in Upper Egypt, around modern Luxor."],["Alexandria","A city on Egypt's Mediterranean coast at the western side of the delta."],["Pyramids at Giza","A pyramid complex west of the Nile, near modern Cairo and north of Memphis."],["Valley of the Kings","A royal burial area on the west bank of the Nile opposite ancient Thebes."]],"w":"CONNECTION: On a blank outline label all 14 named features, use dots for cities and triangles for Giza and Valley of the Kings, add north and a Nile flow arrow. Explain why Upper Egypt is south. Repeat later with shuffled location prompts. Name the prior idea, the new idea, what stays the same, what changes, and evidence.  CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence.","learningTools":{"cue":"Read the explanation and worked model first.","task":"Open focused practice. Answer before looking at feedback; save your explanation.","url":"exam-practice.html?set=ss_v35_egypt_map14","apply":"Explain a new example using a claim, specific evidence and reasoning.","delayed":"Return in a later session and choose a fresh form before rereading."}},{"id":"ss_v35_egypt_vocab23","title":"Ancient Egypt vocabulary: meanings and boundaries","src":"Uploaded Egypt Vocabulary and Journals Through Time-Egypt vocabulary sheets; checked institutional definitions with unresolved source-specific wording flagged","teach":"Build accurate meanings before recall. Distinguish a pharaoh from a dynasty, a mummy from mummification, a scribe from a writing system, and a papyrus plant from writing material. Menes and Narmer need a historical boundary: the traditional name and archaeological king should not be treated as certainly identical. Hatshepsut was a major female pharaoh, not the first female leader. Do not memorize the inaccurate note that Hatshepsut was the first female leader. She was an important female pharaoh. The journal convention uses Narmer for unification; Menes is a traditional first-king name whose identification is debated.","model":"A pharaoh is an individual ruler; a dynasty is a succession of rulers from a family. A preserved body is a mummy; preserving it is mummification. An artifact supplies evidence but does not interpret itself.","connect":"Connect bureaucracy and commerce to prior civilization traits, identifying both the shared idea and Egypt-specific evidence. Connect Ancient Egypt vocabulary: meanings and boundaries through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","success":"Complete distinct practice, write the transfer, repair mistakes, then use a fresh delayed check. Only independent responses count as readiness evidence.","v":[["delta","Land formed by sediment deposited near a river's mouth."],["pharaoh","An ancient Egyptian ruler with political and religious authority."],["dynasty","A sequence of rulers belonging to the same family."],["mummy","A body preserved after death."],["cataracts","Rocky river rapids that can hinder navigation."],["social class","A grouping associated with social position, status, or economic circumstances."],["occupation","A person's work or job; it is not necessarily hereditary."],["bureaucracy","A system of officials and offices that administer government."],["afterlife","An existence believed to follow death."],["mummification","The process of preserving a body after death."],["pyramids","Monumental structures with sloping triangular faces; several served as royal tombs in Egypt."],["artifacts","Objects made or modified by humans that provide evidence about the past."],["hieroglyphics","An Egyptian writing system whose signs can represent sounds and meanings."],["papyrus","A wetland plant, also the writing material made from its stems."],["Sphinx","An Egyptian composite figure, commonly with a lion's body and a human head."],["cultural diffusion","The spread of cultural ideas, practices, or technologies between groups."],["commerce","The buying, selling, and exchange of goods and services."],["Menes","A traditional name for Egypt's first king; identification with Narmer or Aha is uncertain."],["Khufu","An Old Kingdom pharaoh associated with the Great Pyramid at Giza."],["Hyksos","Rulers of West Asian origin who controlled part of Egypt during the Second Intermediate Period."],["Queen Hatshepsut","An important female pharaoh known for major building projects and trade; not Egypt's first female leader."],["Tutankhamun","A New Kingdom pharaoh whose largely intact tomb became famous after its discovery."],["Ramses the Great","Ramesses II, a long-reigning New Kingdom pharaoh known for monuments and military campaigns."]],"w":"CONNECTION: Create six paired contrasts from this vocabulary. Use two terms to explain an original evidence-based Egypt scenario. Then retrieve all meanings without the list in a later session. Name the prior idea, the new idea, what stays the same, what changes, and evidence.  CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link.","learningTools":{"cue":"Read the explanation and worked model first.","task":"Open focused practice. Answer before looking at feedback; save your explanation.","url":"exam-practice.html?set=ss_v35_egypt_vocab23","apply":"Explain a new example using a claim, specific evidence and reasoning.","delayed":"Return in a later session and choose a fresh form before rereading."}},{"id":"ss_v35_journal20","title":"Egypt journal · all 20 classroom questions","src":"Uploaded Journals Through Time-Egypt prompts matched to Discovery Education, Journals Through History: Ancient Egypt—Land of Abundance (2006); public transcript match, with museum-supported qualifications.","teach":"Use the Egypt geography and vocabulary lessons to explain the journal questions. Retrieve the idea, then give a cause, example or geographic relationship. The video uses Narmer for unification; distinguish that convention from uncertainty about the name Menes. The flood calendar is a simplified account: dates shifted over centuries.","model":"A canal is a human response to water availability. Connect the resource to the action and result: river water → managed channel → water reaches crops. This explains irrigation instead of merely naming it.","connect":"Connect Nile geography with resource management and use ELA evidence and reasoning to explain human decisions. Connect Egypt journal · all 20 classroom questions through cause/effect, chronology, and evidence. Place events in sequence, explain mechanisms rather than coincidence, and compare continuity and change across societies without treating one detail as the whole story.","success":"Retrieve all twenty meanings independently, explain the relationships and repeat in a later session. Written responses await review.","v":[["menhed","A scribal writing kit."],["Kemet","Black land: the fertile Nile land."],["Akhet","The inundation season."],["Shemu","The harvest season."]],"w":"CONNECTION: Use the twenty written prompts in focused practice. Then connect one environmental condition to a human response and a possible result. Name the prior idea, the new idea, what stays the same, what changes and the evidence. CONNECTION: Explain a cause-and-effect chain from this lesson, connect it to an earlier or later event, and cite the evidence that supports each link.","learningTools":{"cue":"Learn the geography and vocabulary before opening the journal prompts.","task":"Open all twenty journal questions, save your own response and then compare the review.","url":"exam-practice.html?set=ss_v35_journal20","apply":"Explain how a resource and a human action worked together.","delayed":"Later, explain the same relationship using another journal example."}},{"id":"extra_v35_ss_a","title":"Egypt geography and explanations · practice A","src":"October 23 teacher newsletter scope; original extra practice, form A. Assigned private ELA guide and math practice test remain unverified.","teach":"Use the Nile’s northward flow to distinguish upstream Upper Egypt from downstream Lower Egypt. Learn all fourteen map locations. Support explanations with the supplied study paragraph and distinguish environmental opportunity from guaranteed results.","model":"Start at the Mediterranean, trace the Nile south from the delta, and locate Memphis before Thebes. Thebes is farther upstream; a north arrow does not make Upper Egypt northern.","connect":"Use ELA evidence and reasoning to explain the geographic or mathematical relationship. Connect Egypt geography and explanations · practice A through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","success":"Complete first responses, repair missed skills, and return later for the other form. Writing needs review before readiness can be determined.","v":[],"w":"CONNECTION: Complete the written items in focused practice. Connect the prior idea to the new context; say what stays the same, what changes and which source detail or calculation supports your conclusion. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence.","learningTools":{"cue":"Work one item at a time.","task":"Open form A including written work and visual questions.","url":"exam-practice.html?set=extra_v35_ss_a","apply":"Save your explanation before viewing the model response.","delayed":"Use the other form in a later session without notes."}},{"id":"extra_v35_ss_b","title":"Egypt geography and explanations · practice B","src":"October 23 teacher newsletter scope; original extra practice, form B. Assigned private ELA guide and math practice test remain unverified.","teach":"Use the Nile’s northward flow to distinguish upstream Upper Egypt from downstream Lower Egypt. Learn all fourteen map locations. Support explanations with the supplied study paragraph and distinguish environmental opportunity from guaranteed results.","model":"Start at the Mediterranean, trace the Nile south from the delta, and locate Memphis before Thebes. Thebes is farther upstream; a north arrow does not make Upper Egypt northern.","connect":"Use ELA evidence and reasoning to explain the geographic or mathematical relationship. Connect Egypt geography and explanations · practice B through geography → opportunity or constraint → human choice → outcome. Compare Mesopotamia and Canaan using map evidence, but avoid saying geography forced every decision. Use ELA source analysis to separate what a map shows from what another source claims.","success":"Complete first responses, repair missed skills, and return later for the other form. Writing needs review before readiness can be determined.","v":[],"w":"CONNECTION: Complete the written items in focused practice. Connect the prior idea to the new context; say what stays the same, what changes and which source detail or calculation supports your conclusion. CONNECTION: Use one map fact to explain an opportunity or constraint, a human choice, and an outcome. Compare it with the other region and state one limit of the map evidence.","learningTools":{"cue":"Work one item at a time.","task":"Open form B including written work and visual questions.","url":"exam-practice.html?set=extra_v35_ss_b","apply":"Save your explanation before viewing the model response.","delayed":"Use the other form in a later session without notes."}}]}}};
